// Rep Max questions: Macroeconomics (Themes 2 and 4), Pearson Edexcel A level Economics A (9EC0). One question per line.
// Fields: id, s, t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest), q, o (four options), a (index 0-3), x (explanation), w (why each wrong option is wrong).
// Written by Sonnet 5.5 (API) or Gemini (handoff GH-FNF-261122-134908, econ-181 to 228), checked by Sonnet 5.5 and Opus 5.5 (provenance FNF_RepMax_E1_*, E2_*, G1_*).
{"id": "econ-019", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "The UK inflation rate falls from 4% to 2.5% over a year. Which term describes this?", "o": ["Disinflation", "Stagflation", "Reflation", "Deflation"], "a": 0, "x": "Disinflation is a fall in the rate of inflation. Prices are still rising, just more slowly. Deflation would need a negative inflation rate, meaning the general price level is falling.", "w": ["", "Stagflation is high inflation with stagnant output; the question says nothing about output.", "Reflation is policy to expand demand after a downturn, not a fall in the inflation rate.", "Deflation needs the general price level to fall; a positive rate of 2.5% means prices still rise."]}
{"id": "econ-020", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of these is included in the RPI but excluded from the CPI?", "o": ["Restaurant meals", "Clothing prices", "Petrol prices", "Mortgage interest"], "a": 3, "x": "RPI includes housing costs such as mortgage interest and council tax; CPI leaves them out. Students often assume petrol or food differs, but both measures include them.", "w": ["Restaurant meals are in both baskets.", "Clothing is in both CPI and RPI.", "Petrol is in both measures, so it does not distinguish them.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-021", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A price index is 120 in 2023 and 123.6 in 2024. What is the annual inflation rate?", "o": ["3.6%", "2.9%", "103%", "3%"], "a": 3, "x": "Inflation = (123.6 − 120) ÷ 120 × 100 = 3%. Divide the change by the starting index, not the new one, and give a percentage, not the raw index change.", "w": ["3.6 is the raw change in index points, not a percentage of the starting value.", "2.9% divides by the new index (123.6) instead of the old one.", "103% is the index ratio (1.03) read as a rate, not the percentage change.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-022", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A basket gives food a weight of 40% and transport 60%. Food prices rise 5% and transport prices rise 2%. What is the weighted inflation rate?", "o": ["7%", "3.5%", "3.2%", "3.8%"], "a": 2, "x": "Weighted change = 0.4 × 5 + 0.6 × 2 = 2 + 1.2 = 3.2%. Using a simple average ignores weights, which reflect each item's share of household spending.", "w": ["7% adds the two changes without weighting them.", "3.5% is the simple average, ignoring the weights.", "", "3.8% swaps the weights, applying 60% to food and 40% to transport."]}
{"id": "econ-023", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is most likely to cause cost-push inflation in the UK?", "o": ["A fall in the pound raises imported raw material prices", "A cut in interest rates raises consumer spending", "Higher government spending raises aggregate demand", "A rise in consumer confidence boosts demand"], "a": 0, "x": "Cost-push inflation comes from higher costs of production, shifting SRAS left. Dearer imported raw materials after a depreciation do this. The other options raise aggregate demand, which is demand-pull inflation.", "w": ["", "Lower interest rates raise AD, so this is demand-pull, not cost-push.", "Extra government spending is a rise in AD, so it is demand-pull.", "Higher confidence raises consumption and AD; that is demand-pull."]}
{"id": "econ-024", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Workers' money wages rise 2% while CPI inflation falls from 5% to 3%. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Disinflation occurred and real wages fell by about 1%", "Deflation occurred and real wages fell by about 3%", "Disinflation occurred and real wages rose by about 1%", "Deflation occurred and real wages rose by about 1%"], "a": 0, "x": "Inflation fell but stayed positive, so it is disinflation. Real wage change ≈ 2% − 3% = −1%, so real wages fell. Prices were still rising faster than pay.", "w": ["", "Inflation is still 3%, so prices did not fall; this is not deflation, and the real fall is about 1%.", "Disinflation is right, but 2% pay against 3% inflation means real wages fell.", "A fall in the rate is not deflation, and real wages did not rise."]}
{"id": "econ-025", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 1, "q": "In an economy MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.2 and MPM = 0.2. What is the value of the multiplier?", "o": ["2", "10", "0.5", "5"], "a": 0, "x": "Marginal propensity to withdraw = 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.2 = 0.5, so $k=\\frac{1}{0.5}=2$. The trap is forgetting to invert the MPW.", "w": ["", "10 comes from using 1/MPS alone and ignoring taxation and imports.", "0.5 is the MPW itself; the multiplier is 1 divided by it, so you forgot to invert.", "5 comes from using only MPT or MPM; all three withdrawals must be added first."]}
{"id": "econ-026", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 1, "q": "The only withdrawal in an economy is saving, and MPC = 0.75. Investment rises by £3bn. What is the total increase in national income?", "o": ["£12bn", "£2.25bn", "£9bn", "£4bn"], "a": 0, "x": "$k=\\frac{1}{1-0.75}=4$, so the change in income is 4 × £3bn = £12bn. The trap is giving only the extra beyond the initial injection (£9bn).", "w": ["", "£2.25bn is 0.75 × £3bn, using MPC as the multiplier instead of 1/(1 − MPC).", "£9bn is the extra income beyond the first £3bn; the question asks for the total increase.", "£4bn is just the multiplier; you did not multiply it by the injection."]}
{"id": "econ-027", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which one change would increase the size of the multiplier?", "o": ["A rise in the marginal propensity to tax", "A rise in the marginal propensity to save", "A rise in the marginal propensity to import", "A fall in the marginal propensity to import"], "a": 3, "x": "The multiplier is 1/MPW. A lower MPM reduces withdrawals, so more of each extra £ is re-spent in the UK and the multiplier rises.", "w": ["Higher MPT raises withdrawals, which lowers the multiplier.", "Higher MPS raises withdrawals, which lowers the multiplier.", "Higher MPM means more income leaks abroad, which lowers the multiplier.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-028", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 2, "q": "MPS = 0.05, MPT = 0.2 and MPM = 0.15. Government spending rises by £6bn. What is the eventual rise in national income?", "o": ["£24bn", "£2.4bn", "£15bn", "£9bn"], "a": 2, "x": "MPW = 0.05 + 0.2 + 0.15 = 0.4, so k = 2.5. Rise in income = 2.5 × £6bn = £15bn. Ignoring imports would wrongly give k = 4.", "w": ["£24bn leaves out MPM, using k = 1/0.25 = 4, so imports are ignored as a withdrawal.", "£2.4bn is 0.4 × £6bn; you multiplied by MPW instead of dividing 1 by it.", "", "£9bn is £15bn minus the initial £6bn; the question asks for the total rise."]}
{"id": "econ-029", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 2, "q": "The multiplier is 4. Real GDP is £20bn below the level the government wants. By how much must it raise spending, assuming nothing else changes, to close the gap?", "o": ["£20bn", "£5bn", "£80bn", "£16bn"], "a": 1, "x": "Change in income = k × injection, so injection = £20bn ÷ 4 = £5bn. The trap is multiplying by k instead of dividing.", "w": ["£20bn ignores the multiplier, which makes a £5bn injection enough.", "", "£80bn multiplies by k; the multiplier means a smaller injection is needed.", "£16bn subtracts k from the gap, which is not how the multiplier works."]}
{"id": "econ-030", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 3, "q": "MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.2, MPM = 0.2. A tax change raises MPT to 0.3. How does the final effect of a £12bn rise in investment on national income change?", "o": ["Falls by £4bn", "Falls by £1.2bn", "Rises by £4bn", "Falls by £20bn"], "a": 0, "x": "Before: MPW 0.5, k = 2, rise £24bn. After: MPW 0.6, k = 1/0.6, rise £20bn. So the effect falls by £4bn. A higher MPT cuts the multiplier.", "w": ["", "£1.2bn is 12 × 0.1, using the change in MPT directly rather than recalculating the multiplier.", "Higher MPT increases withdrawals, so the multiplier and income effect fall, not rise.", "£20bn is the new total effect, not the change from the original £24bn."]}
{"id": "econ-031", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which body in the UK sets Bank Rate to meet the government's inflation target?", "o": ["The Competition and Markets Authority", "The Treasury", "The Office for Budget Responsibility", "The Monetary Policy Committee"], "a": 3, "x": "The MPC of the Bank of England sets Bank Rate and decides on QE to meet the CPI inflation target. The Treasury sets fiscal policy; the OBR only forecasts.", "w": ["The CMA regulates competition, which is microeconomic policy, not monetary policy.", "The Treasury runs fiscal policy, not interest rates. Mixing up fiscal and monetary authorities.", "The OBR produces forecasts of the public finances; it does not set interest rates. Confusing forecasting with policy-setting.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-032", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an example of expansionary fiscal policy?", "o": ["Reducing the budget deficit by cutting spending", "Cutting income tax and raising government spending", "Raising Bank Rate to reduce borrowing", "Selling government bonds to reduce the money supply"], "a": 1, "x": "Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxation. Lower taxes and higher spending raise aggregate demand. Bank Rate and bond sales are monetary tools, and cutting the deficit is contractionary.", "w": ["Cutting spending to reduce the deficit is contractionary (austerity), not expansionary.", "", "Bank Rate is a monetary tool, and raising it is contractionary, not expansionary.", "Selling bonds is a monetary tool that tightens money supply, so it is both the wrong type and the wrong direction."]}
{"id": "econ-033", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 2, "q": "The Bank of England buys government bonds from financial institutions with newly created electronic money. Which describes the main intended transmission of this quantitative easing?", "o": ["Bond prices rise and yields fall, cutting borrowing costs and raising AD", "Government spending rises directly, which raises AD", "Bank Rate is raised, which reduces inflationary pressure", "Bond prices fall and yields rise, encouraging saving and lowering AD"], "a": 0, "x": "Buying bonds raises their price and lowers yields, and adds liquidity to banks. Cheaper borrowing and higher asset prices should raise consumption and investment. Students often reverse price and yield.", "w": ["", "QE is carried out by the central bank, not government spending; the Treasury's budget is not directly changed.", "QE is used when more demand is needed, not to raise Bank Rate or reduce inflation.", "This reverses the effect: buying bonds raises demand for them, so prices rise and yields fall."]}
{"id": "econ-034", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 2, "q": "An economy has a large negative output gap and inflation below target. Which policy is most appropriate?", "o": ["Raise corporation tax and reduce the money supply", "Raise Bank Rate and raise income taxes", "Cut Bank Rate and raise government capital spending", "Cut government spending and sell bonds"], "a": 2, "x": "A negative output gap means spare capacity and weak AD, so expansionary policy is needed: lower rates and higher spending. The other options all tighten policy and would widen the gap.", "w": ["Higher corporation tax and a smaller money supply both reduce AD, the wrong direction.", "Both measures are contractionary and would worsen the negative output gap.", "", "Cutting spending and selling bonds both reduce AD, widening the gap."]}
{"id": "econ-035", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A government has a budget deficit of £40bn in one year and £30bn the next, while its total debt keeps rising. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["The deficit fell, but debt still grew because a deficit remains", "The government ran a budget surplus in the second year", "The national debt fell because the annual deficit fell", "The deficit and the national debt are exactly the same thing"], "a": 0, "x": "The deficit is an annual flow; the national debt is the accumulated stock. A smaller deficit still adds £30bn to debt. Students often confuse the flow with the stock.", "w": ["", "A £30bn deficit is still a deficit; a surplus would need tax revenue to exceed spending.", "A fall in the deficit only slows borrowing; debt falls only with a surplus. This confuses flow and stock.", "The deficit is yearly borrowing, whereas debt is total accumulated borrowing. They are different."]}
{"id": "econ-036", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Bank Rate is cut sharply during a recession but consumer spending barely rises. Which is the most likely explanation?", "o": ["The MPC has no influence over any market interest rates", "Lower rates always raise saving by more than they raise spending", "Cutting rates shifts AS left, which offsets the rise in AD", "Low confidence and weak bank lending blunt the effect of cuts"], "a": 3, "x": "Monetary policy can be weakened by low consumer and business confidence, banks restricting lending, and households repaying debt. Cuts are not automatically effective.", "w": ["Bank Rate strongly influences market rates, so the MPC does have influence. The issue is weakened transmission.", "\"Always\" is wrong; the usual effect of lower rates is less saving. Weak response comes from confidence, not a fixed rule.", "Rate cuts act mainly on AD; they do not shift AS left. This confuses the type of effect.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-049", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best describes a floating exchange rate system?", "o": ["The rate is set by demand for and supply of the currency", "The rate is fixed against gold by the government", "The central bank sets the rate and holds it by trading reserves", "The rate may move only within a narrow band set by government"], "a": 0, "x": "In a floating system the rate is determined by market demand and supply of the currency, with no official target. Targeting or holding a rate describes fixed or managed systems.", "w": ["", "Gold-linked rates are a fixed system and are not what floating means.", "This describes a fixed rate system, where the central bank intervenes to hold a target rate.", "A band around a target describes a managed (or semi-fixed) system, not a pure float."]}
{"id": "econ-050", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 1, "q": "The government of a country with a fixed exchange rate lowers the official rate against the dollar. What is this called?", "o": ["Appreciation", "Revaluation", "Devaluation", "Depreciation"], "a": 2, "x": "Devaluation is a deliberate lowering of a fixed or pegged exchange rate by the government. Depreciation is a market-driven fall under floating rates. Revaluation is a deliberate rise.", "w": ["Appreciation is a market-driven rise, and this is a fall.", "Revaluation is a deliberate rise in a fixed rate, the opposite direction to this.", "", "Depreciation is a market-driven fall in a floating rate, not an official decision."]}
{"id": "econ-051", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 2, "q": "The pound is floating. Foreign investors buy more UK government bonds. Which effect on the foreign exchange market for sterling follows, ceteris paribus?", "o": ["Demand for sterling shifts right, so the pound appreciates", "Supply of sterling shifts right, so the pound depreciates", "Demand for sterling shifts left, so the pound depreciates", "Supply of sterling shifts left, so the pound appreciates"], "a": 0, "x": "Foreign buyers need pounds to buy UK bonds, so demand for sterling rises and the rate appreciates. The trap is confusing whose currency is being demanded.", "w": ["", "Supply of sterling rises when UK residents buy foreign assets, not when foreigners buy UK ones.", "Demand would fall if foreigners sold UK assets, not bought them.", "Supply falls when UK residents buy fewer foreign assets, which is not the case here."]}
{"id": "econ-052", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 2, "q": "After a sterling depreciation the UK current account worsens for several months, then improves. What explains the initial worsening?", "o": ["Volumes respond slowly, so dearer imports raise import spending", "Demand for imports is highly elastic immediately after the fall", "Exports become dearer in foreign currency, so export volumes fall at once", "The Marshall-Lerner condition is always met in the short run"], "a": 0, "x": "In the short run demand is inelastic because contracts and habits take time to change. Import prices rise but volumes barely fall, so the balance worsens: the J-curve. Volumes adjust later.", "w": ["", "Short-run demand is inelastic, not elastic; elastic demand would improve the balance quickly.", "Depreciation makes exports cheaper in foreign currency, not dearer.", "If it were met immediately, there would be no initial worsening and no J-curve."]}
{"id": "econ-053", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 2, "q": "To hold a fixed rate when there is downward pressure on the currency, the central bank can do which of the following?", "o": ["Sell the domestic currency and lower interest rates", "Sell foreign reserves and lower interest rates", "Buy the domestic currency with reserves and raise rates", "Buy foreign currency and raise interest rates"], "a": 2, "x": "To support the rate the bank raises demand for its currency: it buys it with reserves and raises rates to attract hot money. Selling the currency increases supply and lowers the rate.", "w": ["Selling the domestic currency adds to supply and worsens the fall.", "Lower interest rates reduce demand for the currency, so this fails to support the rate even though selling reserves alone could.", "", "Buying foreign currency means selling domestic currency, which pushes the rate down further."]}
{"id": "econ-054", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 3, "q": "A country with a fixed rate devalues. Which is the most likely short-run effect, ceteris paribus, if demand for imports and exports is inelastic?", "o": ["The current account is unaffected as volumes are unchanged", "Export earnings are unchanged because prices are fixed", "The current account worsens as import spending outgrows exports", "The current account improves because exports are cheaper"], "a": 2, "x": "With inelastic demand (the Marshall-Lerner condition is not met), volumes barely change while import prices rise in domestic currency, so the deficit widens. This is the downward part of the J-curve.", "w": ["Volumes may be little changed, but import prices rise, so spending changes.", "Prices of exports in foreign currency do fall after devaluation, so earnings change.", "", "Cheaper exports help only if demand is elastic enough; here volumes hardly change."]}
{"id": "econ-055", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "A country's Gini coefficient for income falls from 0.42 to 0.38. What does this show?", "o": ["Income has become more equally distributed", "Income has become more unequally distributed", "The incomes of the poorest households have fallen", "Average income in the economy has fallen"], "a": 0, "x": "A lower Gini means the Lorenz curve is closer to the line of equality, so income is more equally distributed. The trap is reading a higher Gini as 'better'. Gini says nothing about average income.", "w": ["", "Reverses the scale: a falling Gini means less inequality, not more.", "The Gini measures relative shares, so it cannot show that the poorest are worse off in absolute terms.", "The Gini gives no information about the level of average income, only its distribution."]}
{"id": "econ-056", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an example of wealth, rather than income?", "o": ["Child benefit received by a parent each month", "Dividends paid to a shareholder over this year", "A household's house and savings balance", "A nurse's monthly salary from the NHS trust"], "a": 2, "x": "Wealth is a stock of assets owned at a point in time, such as property and savings. Salary, dividends and benefits are flows of income over a period. The trap is confusing a stock with a flow.", "w": ["Benefits are a flow of income, in this case a transfer payment.", "Dividends are income received over a period, even though they come from owning shares.", "", "A salary is a flow of income over time, not a stock of assets."]}
{"id": "econ-057", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "On a Lorenz diagram, the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve is 1,200 units. The area under the Lorenz curve is 3,800 units. What is the Gini coefficient?", "o": ["3.17", "0.24", "0.76", "0.32"], "a": 1, "x": "Gini = A ÷ (A+B), where A is the area between the line and the curve. Total area under the line = 1,200 + 3,800 = 5,000. So 1,200 ÷ 5,000 = 0.24. The trap is dividing by B alone.", "w": ["This is B ÷ A, which is upside down and cannot be a Gini value between 0 and 1.", "", "This is B ÷ (A+B), the area under the Lorenz curve, not the area between the curve and the line.", "Divides A by B (1,200 ÷ 3,800) instead of by the total area under the line of equality."]}
{"id": "econ-058", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Shares of national income by household quintile, poorest to richest, are 5%, 10%, 15%, 20% and 50%. What cumulative % of income does the poorest 60% of households receive?", "o": ["15%", "50%", "20%", "30%"], "a": 3, "x": "A Lorenz curve plots cumulative shares. The poorest 60% are the first three quintiles: 5 + 10 + 15 = 30%. The trap is using a single quintile's share rather than adding up.", "w": ["This is the third quintile's own share, not a cumulative figure.", "This is the richest quintile's share, not the cumulative share of the poorest 60%.", "This is the fourth quintile's own share, not a cumulative figure.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-059", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Which change is most likely to increase income inequality in the UK?", "o": ["Making income tax rates more progressive for high earners", "A rise in the wage premium for skilled workers from new technology", "Increasing means-tested benefits for low-income households", "A rise in the National Living Wage for low-paid workers"], "a": 1, "x": "If demand for highly skilled labour rises faster than for low-skilled labour, skilled wages rise relative to others, widening income inequality. The other three redistribute towards the poor.", "w": ["More progressive tax takes a larger share from high earners, reducing inequality in post-tax income.", "", "Means-tested benefits are targeted at the poor, so they reduce inequality.", "A higher minimum wage raises low pay, which tends to narrow inequality."]}
{"id": "econ-060", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The Lorenz curves of countries X and Y cross each other. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Inequality cannot be ranked by eye; a Gini hides where they differ", "The two must have identical Gini coefficients as the curves cross", "X is more equal, because its curve is closer to the line at the bottom", "Y is more equal, because its curve is closer to the line at the top"], "a": 0, "x": "When curves cross, one is not closer to equality throughout, so inspection cannot rank them. Gini gives one number, but hides whether the difference lies among the poor or the rich.", "w": ["", "Crossing curves do not mean equal areas; Gini values can differ.", "Looks at only one part of the curve; being closer at one point does not show greater equality overall.", "Looks at only one part of the curve; the other part favours X."]}
{"id": "econ-181", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "What is the key difference between nominal GDP and real GDP?", "o": ["Real GDP includes net property income from abroad", "Real GDP adjusts nominal GDP to remove inflation", "Real GDP divides total output by the population", "Real GDP excludes all government service output"], "a": 1, "x": "Real GDP measures output at constant prices by removing inflation. The trap is confusing real GDP with GNI or GDP per head.", "w": ["That describes Gross National Income (GNI), which adds net property income from abroad to GDP.", "", "That defines GDP per head (per capita), not the adjustment from nominal to real values.", "Both nominal and real GDP include public services such as state schools and NHS healthcare."]}
{"id": "econ-182", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "How is Gross National Income (GNI) calculated from Gross Domestic Product (GDP)?", "o": ["GDP plus net property income from abroad", "GDP minus depreciation of physical capital", "GDP minus indirect taxes plus subsidies", "GDP divided by the GDP deflator index"], "a": 0, "x": "GNI equals GDP plus net property income from abroad (the net flow of profits, interest and dividends earned on assets abroad). The trap is confusing GNI with net domestic product, real GDP or GDP at basic prices.", "w": ["", "Subtracting capital depreciation gives Net Domestic Product, not Gross National Income.", "Removing indirect taxes and adding subsidies moves between market prices and basic prices, not GDP to GNI.", "Dividing by the price index converts nominal GDP into real GDP rather than GNI."]}
{"id": "econ-183", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Why do economists use Purchasing Power Parities (PPPs) when comparing GDP per head between two countries?", "o": ["To remove the effect of differences in population size", "To adjust for differences in the cost of living", "To measure income inequality within each country", "To exclude profits sent home by foreign multinationals"], "a": 1, "x": "PPPs compare what a common basket of goods costs in each country, adjusting for cost-of-living gaps that market exchange rates miss. The trap is thinking PPPs adjust for population size, income flows abroad or inequality.", "w": ["Dividing GDP by population already removes differences in population size before any PPP adjustment.", "", "PPPs adjust average output for local prices and do not measure how income is shared.", "Adjusting for cross-border profits is what distinguishes GNI from GDP, not PPP exchange rates."]}
{"id": "econ-184", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In a made-up economy, nominal GDP rises from £500bn in Year 1 to £546bn in Year 2, while the price index rises from 100 to 105. What is real GDP growth?", "o": ["4.2%", "9.2%", "4.0%", "5.0%"], "a": 2, "x": "Year 2 real GDP is $\\frac{546}{105}\\times 100=£520\\text{bn}$. Growth is $\\frac{520-500}{500}\\times 100=4.0\\%$. Subtracting $5\\%$ from $9.2\\%$ is only an approximation.", "w": ["4.2% comes from simply subtracting 5% from 9.2%, which slightly overstates exact real growth.", "9.2% is nominal GDP growth, which fails to remove the 5% rise in the price level.", "", "5.0% is the rate of inflation shown by the price index, not the rise in real output."]}
{"id": "econ-185", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "According to the Easterlin paradox, why might higher real GDP per head fail to raise average happiness in a rich country?", "o": ["Higher real GDP always reduces leisure time to zero", "Real GDP per head excludes all private spending", "Inflation automatically cancels out real GDP gains", "People judge wellbeing relative to others' incomes"], "a": 3, "x": "Once basic needs are met, people compare their income with others', so a general rise in incomes may not lift average happiness. The trap is blaming inflation, which real GDP has already removed.", "w": ["Growth can coexist with shorter working hours, so leisure time does not inevitably fall to zero.", "Private household spending is the largest part of GDP, not an excluded item.", "Real GDP has already been adjusted for inflation, so price rises do not cancel it out.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-186", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 3, "q": "Country A has higher PPP-adjusted real GDP per head than Country B, yet lower average wellbeing. Which factor best explains this?", "o": ["Country A has wide inequality and high pollution", "Country A uses a weaker market exchange rate than B", "Country A has a much larger total population than B", "Country A has a higher rate of price inflation than B"], "a": 0, "x": "PPP-adjusted real GDP per head already controls for inflation, living costs and population, but misses inequality, pollution, leisure and health. The trap is choosing a factor the measure has already adjusted for.", "w": ["", "Using PPP exchange rates has already corrected for distortions in market exchange rates.", "Measuring output per head already divides total output by population size.", "Using real GDP has already stripped out differences in domestic price inflation."]}
{"id": "econ-187", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which worker is best described as underemployed?", "o": ["A retired teacher choosing not to look for work", "A worker claiming benefits while between jobs", "A part-time barista seeking full-time shifts", "A student applying for a first graduate role"], "a": 2, "x": "Underemployment covers workers wanting more hours or in jobs below their skill level. The trap is confusing it with unemployment or inactivity.", "w": ["A retired person not seeking work is economically inactive, not underemployed.", "Someone without a job who is claiming benefits is unemployed rather than underemployed.", "", "A student without a job who is seeking work and available is unemployed, not underemployed."]}
{"id": "econ-188", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "A UK software engineer leaves one firm and spends five weeks interviewing before starting a better-paid role. What type of unemployment is this?", "o": ["Cyclical unemployment", "Frictional unemployment", "Seasonal unemployment", "Structural unemployment"], "a": 1, "x": "Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers search between jobs. The trap is calling any job change structural.", "w": ["Cyclical unemployment is caused by a fall in aggregate demand during a downturn, not job search.", "", "Seasonal unemployment happens when demand for labour drops at regular times of the year.", "Structural unemployment reflects a long-term mismatch of skills or location, not a brief transition."]}
{"id": "econ-189", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In a made-up UK survey, 28.5m people are employed, 1.5m are unemployed and 10.0m are economically inactive. What is the unemployment rate?", "o": ["5.26%", "5.0%", "15.0%", "3.75%"], "a": 1, "x": "The labour force is $28.5+1.5=30.0\\text{m}$. The rate is $\\frac{1.5}{30.0}\\times 100=5.0\\%$. The trap is including economically inactive people in the denominator.", "w": ["5.26% divides unemployed by employed alone (28.5m) instead of the whole labour force (30.0m).", "", "15.0% divides unemployed by the economically inactive group, which is not the labour force.", "3.75% wrongly includes the 10.0m economically inactive people in the denominator (40.0m)."]}
{"id": "econ-190", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "After a UK steelworks closes, former workers lack digital skills for nearby service jobs and cannot afford housing in London. What type of unemployment results?", "o": ["Seasonal unemployment from winter closures", "Structural unemployment from labour immobility", "Cyclical unemployment from higher interest rates", "Frictional unemployment from slow recruitment"], "a": 1, "x": "Skill gaps create occupational immobility and high housing costs create geographical immobility, causing structural unemployment. The trap is blaming cyclical demand.", "w": ["Seasonal unemployment repeats annually in sectors like tourism or farming, unlike plant closure.", "", "Cyclical unemployment comes from an economy-wide slump in aggregate demand, not industry decline.", "Frictional unemployment is brief search time when workers already match available vacancies."]}
{"id": "econ-191", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Why does net immigration of working-age adults into the UK not automatically raise the unemployment rate?", "o": ["Immigrants are excluded from the Labour Force Survey", "Immigrants also spend wages, boosting demand for labour", "Immigrants always accept wages below the legal minimum", "Immigration automatically reduces the UK labour force"], "a": 1, "x": "Immigrants add to labour supply, yet their spending raises aggregate demand and creates jobs. The trap is treating the number of jobs as fixed.", "w": ["Resident immigrants seeking or holding work are included in the Labour Force Survey.", "", "Firms must legally pay at least the minimum wage, and many immigrants earn well above it, so 'always' is false.", "Net immigration of working-age adults increases the labour force rather than reducing it."]}
{"id": "econ-192", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 3, "q": "In a labour market diagram, a national minimum wage set above equilibrium causes unemployment. Which long-term risk deepens the harm if workers stay jobless?", "o": ["Seasonal jobs vanish across all UK industries", "The Claimant Count automatically rises above the LFS", "Excess demand for labour pushes wages higher still", "Hysteresis erodes skills and cuts productive capacity"], "a": 3, "x": "A wage floor above equilibrium causes excess labour supply (real-wage unemployment). Long spells out of work cause hysteresis, eroding skills and LRAS. The trap is choosing excess demand for labour, which a wage floor does not create.", "w": ["Real-wage unemployment affects low-wage labour markets broadly rather than removing seasonal work.", "A minimum wage does not push the Claimant Count above the LFS; the two measures simply count different groups.", "A minimum wage above equilibrium creates excess supply of labour, not excess demand.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-193", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Where on the UK current account is UK overseas development aid recorded?", "o": ["Secondary income", "Trade in goods", "Primary income", "Trade in services"], "a": 0, "x": "Secondary income records current transfers with nothing supplied in return, such as overseas aid and remittances. The trap is placing it in primary income.", "w": ["", "Trade in goods records physical imports and exports of merchandise, not aid transfers.", "Primary income records earnings on factors of production such as wages, interest and dividends.", "Trade in services covers invisible trade like tourism and finance, not unrequited transfers."]}
{"id": "econ-194", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "A UK pension fund receives £40m in dividends from shares it owns in a German carmaker. How is this recorded on the UK current account?", "o": ["A debit (outflow) in primary income", "A credit (inflow) in trade in services", "A credit (inflow) in primary income", "A credit (inflow) in secondary income"], "a": 2, "x": "Dividends are investment income, so they belong in primary income; money flowing into the UK is a credit. The trap is calling shares a service.", "w": ["Money entering the UK from abroad is a credit (inflow), not a debit (outflow).", "Financial advice fees are trade in services, whereas dividends are returns on capital in primary income.", "", "Secondary income covers transfers with nothing given in return, whereas dividends reward capital."]}
{"id": "econ-195", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "UK holidaymakers spend £500m on hotels and restaurants in Spain. How does this affect the UK current account?", "o": ["Credit in trade in services", "Debit in trade in goods", "Debit in secondary income", "Debit in trade in services"], "a": 3, "x": "Spending by UK residents abroad on hospitality is an import of services, causing an outflow (debit). The trap is thinking going abroad means exporting.", "w": ["A credit in services occurs when foreign visitors spend money in the UK, not vice versa.", "Hotel stays and restaurant meals are services rather than physical traded goods.", "Holiday spending pays for a service received, so it is not an unrequited transfer.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-196", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A made-up economy has nominal GDP of £2,000bn and a current account balance of −£60bn. What is its current account balance as a percentage of GDP?", "o": ["+3.0%", "−33.3%", "−3.0%", "−0.3%"], "a": 2, "x": "$\\frac{-60}{2000}\\times 100=-3.0\\%$, a deficit of 3.0% of GDP. The trap is dropping the minus sign or misplacing the decimal point.", "w": ["+3.0% reverses the sign and wrongly reports a deficit as a surplus.", "−33.3% divides GDP by the deficit instead of dividing the deficit by GDP.", "", "−0.3% misplaces the decimal point by a factor of ten."]}
{"id": "econ-197", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "What does global interconnectedness imply about current account balances across all countries combined?", "o": ["Current account balances must always equal zero for every nation", "Deficits in some economies are matched by surpluses in others", "Every country can run a surplus at the same time", "Deficits in one country always cause deficits elsewhere"], "a": 1, "x": "One country's export is another's import, so global surpluses and deficits theoretically sum to zero. The trap is thinking all nations can run surpluses.", "w": ["Individual nations can run large surpluses or deficits for years; only the world total balances.", "", "Since every export is another nation's import, it is impossible for every country to run a surplus.", "A deficit in one country means net inflows for trading partners, contributing to surpluses elsewhere."]}
{"id": "econ-198", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "lv": 3, "q": "Why might a UK current account deficit of 4% of GDP be less worrying if it is caused by importing machinery and financed by foreign direct investment (FDI)?", "o": ["Machinery boosts future exports and FDI is stable finance", "Importing machinery is excluded from trade in goods", "A 4% deficit always triggers an immediate currency rise", "FDI automatically turns the current account into a surplus"], "a": 0, "x": "Capital goods raise productive capacity and export competitiveness, while FDI is more stable than short-term hot money. The trap is thinking FDI changes the current account directly.", "w": ["", "Imported machinery is a physical good and is counted as a debit in trade in goods.", "A current account deficit tends to increase supply of sterling, which weakens rather than strengthens the currency.", "FDI enters the financial account to finance the deficit; it does not make the current account positive."]}
{"id": "econ-199", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which component normally makes up the largest share of UK aggregate demand?", "o": ["Government expenditure $(G)$", "Capital investment $(I)$", "Net trade $(X-M)$", "Household consumption $(C)$"], "a": 3, "x": "Household consumption (C) is by far the largest component of UK AD, typically around three-fifths. The trap is overestimating investment or net trade.", "w": ["Government spending is the second-largest part of UK AD, below household consumption.", "Investment is volatile and important for growth, yet much smaller than household spending.", "Net trade is usually a small negative figure in the UK because imports exceed exports.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-200", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 1, "q": "On an AD diagram with price level on the vertical axis and real output on the horizontal axis, what happens when the general price level rises, ceteris paribus?", "o": ["The AD curve becomes completely vertical", "The AD curve shifts to the right", "An upward movement along the AD curve", "The AD curve shifts to the left"], "a": 2, "x": "The price level is on the vertical axis, so a rise in it causes an upward movement along AD (a contraction). Only non-price changes in C, I, G or (X−M) shift AD. The trap is confusing a movement along AD with a shift.", "w": ["AD slopes downwards; it is the classical long-run aggregate supply curve that is vertical.", "A change in the price level moves the economy along AD; it does not shift AD in either direction.", "", "A leftward shift happens when C, I, G or (X−M) falls at every price level, not from price changes."]}
{"id": "econ-201", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In a made-up economy, $C=£900\\text{bn}$, $I=£250\\text{bn}$, $G=£320\\text{bn}$, $X=£410\\text{bn}$ and $M=£440\\text{bn}$. What is aggregate demand?", "o": ["£2,320bn", "£1,500bn", "£1,880bn", "£1,440bn"], "a": 3, "x": "$AD=900+250+320+(410-440)=1,470-30=£1,440\\text{bn}$. The trap is adding imports instead of subtracting them, or omitting imports.", "w": ["£2,320bn wrongly adds £440bn of imports instead of subtracting them from exports.", "£1,500bn adds £30bn instead of subtracting £30bn, reversing the sign of net trade.", "£1,880bn adds exports (£410bn) but forgets to subtract imports (£440bn).", ""]}
{"id": "econ-202", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A small UK toy shop buys £15,000 of UK-made display shelving and till equipment. Which component of UK AD rises directly?", "o": ["Consumption $(C)$", "Exports $(X)$", "Government expenditure $(G)$", "Investment $(I)$"], "a": 3, "x": "Spending by firms on capital goods such as shelving and tills is investment (I). The trap is calling any retail-sector purchase consumption.", "w": ["Consumption (C) is spending by households on consumer goods and services, not firms buying capital.", "Exports (X) are goods sold to overseas buyers, whereas the shop is based in the UK.", "Government expenditure (G) is spending by central and local government, not a private shop.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-203", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "UK households buy more imported electronics while exports stay unchanged, with no change in total household spending. How does UK AD shift?", "o": ["No shift, because higher imports cancel out exports", "Rightwards, because household spending is unchanged", "Leftwards, because net trade $(X-M)$ falls", "Down along the curve, because electronics are cheaper"], "a": 2, "x": "Imports are subtracted in $(X-M)$. Higher $M$ with unchanged $C$ and $X$ lowers net trade, shifting AD leftwards. The trap is thinking unchanged household spending means unchanged AD.", "w": ["Higher imports reduce $(X-M)$ when exports stay fixed, so AD cannot remain unshifted.", "Unchanged C with higher M means more spending leaks abroad, reducing rather than raising AD.", "", "A preference shift towards imports is a non-price factor that shifts the whole AD curve."]}
{"id": "econ-204", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 3, "q": "At a given price level, $C$ rises by £20bn, $G$ falls by £12bn and net trade $(X-M)$ worsens by £15bn, with $I$ unchanged. How does AD change?", "o": ["Shifts rightwards by £7bn", "Shifts leftwards by £47bn", "Shifts leftwards by £7bn", "Shifts rightwards by £23bn"], "a": 2, "x": "Net change is $+20-12-15=-£7\\text{bn}$. Total spending falls at the same price level, so AD shifts leftwards by £7bn. The trap is adding the worsening in net trade instead of subtracting it.", "w": ["£7bn rightwards gets the size right but reverses the direction of the net fall.", "£47bn leftwards adds up all three changes as if each reduced AD, ignoring that C rises.", "", "Adding £15bn instead of subtracting treats a worsening of net trade as an improvement."]}
{"id": "econ-205", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "How is household disposable income defined in UK macroeconomics?", "o": ["Gross income minus VAT and excise duties paid", "Gross income minus direct taxes plus cash benefits", "Total household savings plus property equity held", "Gross income minus mortgage and rent payments made"], "a": 1, "x": "Disposable income is what households have to spend or save once direct taxes (income tax, National Insurance) are deducted and state cash benefits added. The trap is deducting housing costs, which gives discretionary income.", "w": ["VAT and excise duties are indirect taxes on spending, not deducted when defining disposable income.", "", "Savings and property equity are stocks of household wealth, not a flow of disposable income.", "Income left after essential housing bills is discretionary income, not disposable income."]}
{"id": "econ-206", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In a simple model with disposable income only spent or saved, if the marginal propensity to consume ($MPC$) is 0.8, what is the marginal propensity to save ($MPS$)?", "o": ["−0.2", "0.2", "0.8", "1.25"], "a": 1, "x": "Because every extra £1 of disposable income is either consumed or saved, $MPC+MPS=1$, so $MPS=1-0.8=0.2$. The trap is inverting 0.8.", "w": ["−0.2 reverses the subtraction ($0.8-1$); households here save a positive 20p per extra £1.", "", "0.8 repeats the MPC instead of subtracting it from 1 to find the share saved.", "1.25 divides 1 by 0.8, which confuses MPS with a reciprocal ratio."]}
{"id": "econ-207", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A made-up UK household's monthly disposable income rises from £2,500 to £2,900, and its consumption rises from £2,100 to £2,400. What is its $MPC$?", "o": ["0.25", "0.84", "0.75", "0.83"], "a": 2, "x": "$\\Delta C=£300$ and $\\Delta Y=£400$, so $MPC=\\frac{300}{400}=0.75$. The trap is dividing total consumption by total income (APC) instead of using changes.", "w": ["0.25 is the marginal propensity to save (100/400), not the marginal propensity to consume.", "0.84 is the original average propensity to consume (2,100/2,500), not the marginal change.", "", "0.83 is the new average propensity to consume (2,400/2,900), not the marginal change."]}
{"id": "econ-208", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Fearing redundancies during a downturn, UK households keep a bigger share of their pay in bank accounts. What happens to the household saving ratio and AD?", "o": ["Saving ratio falls and AD shifts rightwards", "Saving ratio rises and AD shifts leftwards", "Saving ratio rises and AD shifts rightwards", "Saving ratio falls and AD shifts leftwards"], "a": 1, "x": "Weak consumer confidence triggers precautionary saving, raising the saving ratio and reducing consumption ($C$), which shifts AD leftwards. The trap is thinking money kept in bank accounts still boosts spending.", "w": ["Saving a larger share of income increases the saving ratio and cuts spending rather than raising AD.", "", "Higher saving reduces current household spending, shifting AD leftwards rather than rightwards.", "Setting aside a larger fraction of disposable income raises the saving ratio, not lowers it."]}
{"id": "econ-209", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Which of the following is an example of household wealth rather than household income?", "o": ["Quarterly interest paid on a savings bond", "£250,000 of equity owned in a UK house", "Weekly state pension payments received", "A teacher's monthly take-home salary"], "a": 1, "x": "Wealth is a stock of assets held at a point in time, such as housing equity or shares. Income is a flow received over time. The trap is the bond interest: it is income earned from wealth, not wealth itself.", "w": ["Quarterly interest is an income flow generated by an asset, not the asset stock itself.", "", "Weekly pension payments are a recurring flow of transfer income rather than a stock of assets.", "A monthly salary is a flow of earned income per period, not a stock of wealth."]}
{"id": "econ-210", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Why does redistributing £1bn of disposable income from the highest earners to the lowest-income UK households usually raise total consumption?", "o": ["Lower-income households always hold more housing wealth", "Higher-income households never pay indirect taxes", "Redistribution lowers the Bank of England Bank Rate", "Lower-income households typically have a higher MPC"], "a": 3, "x": "Low-income households spend a higher fraction of each extra £1 (higher MPC) than affluent households, who save more, so total $C$ rises. The trap is reaching for a Bank Rate link: redistribution works through differences in MPC.", "w": ["Lower-income households typically hold less housing wealth, not more, than high earners.", "High earners do pay VAT and other indirect taxes, so this does not explain the change in C.", "Fiscal redistribution is set by HM Treasury and does not automatically reduce Bank Rate.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-211", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which transaction counts as investment ($I$) in UK aggregate demand?", "o": ["A supermarket paying monthly wages to staff", "A student putting £500 into a UK savings account", "A UK bakery buying a new commercial oven", "An investor buying existing FTSE 100 shares"], "a": 2, "x": "Investment is spending by firms on new physical capital goods such as machinery or buildings. The trap is using the everyday financial meaning of investing.", "w": ["Paying wages is a current operating cost rather than expenditure on capital goods.", "Putting money in a bank account is household saving, a withdrawal rather than investment.", "", "Buying existing shares transfers ownership of financial assets without adding new capital goods."]}
{"id": "econ-212", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 1, "q": "What did John Maynard Keynes mean by 'animal spirits' when explaining business investment?", "o": ["The physical wear and tear on machinery each year", "Business instincts, emotions and confidence", "The minimum interest rate set by the central bank", "Government regulations restricting factory pollution"], "a": 1, "x": "Keynes used animal spirits to describe optimism, confidence and gut feelings that drive firms to invest when future returns are uncertain. The trap is mixing it with depreciation.", "w": ["Wear and tear on capital goods is called depreciation (capital consumption), not animal spirits.", "", "The central bank's policy rate is Bank Rate, whereas animal spirits describe business psychology.", "Environmental regulation is government intervention, not Keynes's concept of animal spirits."]}
{"id": "econ-213", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Why does a faster rate of UK economic growth typically encourage firms to increase investment?", "o": ["Faster growth guarantees Bank Rate will drop to zero", "Firms invest only when national output is falling", "Faster growth eliminates depreciation on existing machines", "Rising demand needs extra capacity for more orders"], "a": 3, "x": "Stronger GDP growth boosts sales expectations and shrinks spare capacity, encouraging firms to buy new capital goods (the accelerator effect). The trap is linking growth to zero rates.", "w": ["Faster growth often raises inflation pressure, making higher, not zero, interest rates more likely.", "Falling output leaves spare capacity and weak confidence, which discourages new investment.", "Machinery still wears out during booms; heavier use can even speed up depreciation.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-214", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In a made-up year, a UK haulage firm spends £18m on new electric vans while its existing fleet depreciates by £21m. What is its net investment?", "o": ["−£18m, so its capital stock shrinks", "+£39m, so its capital stock expands", "+£3m, so its capital stock expands", "−£3m, so its capital stock shrinks"], "a": 3, "x": "$\\text{Net investment}=£18\\text{m}-£21\\text{m}=-£3\\text{m}$. Because gross investment fails to replace worn-out capital, the capital stock shrinks. The trap is adding depreciation.", "w": ["−£18m ignores depreciation and simply puts a minus sign in front of gross investment.", "+£39m wrongly adds depreciation to gross investment instead of subtracting it.", "+£3m reverses the subtraction ($21-18$), turning a fall in capital stock into a rise.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-215", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 2, "q": "After a banking crisis, UK banks become risk-averse and turn down loans to small firms even though Bank Rate is low. Which investment determinant is at work?", "o": ["Reduced access to credit", "Stronger export growth abroad", "Higher corporation tax rates", "Lower capital depreciation"], "a": 0, "x": "Even at low policy rates, tighter bank lending restricts access to credit, stopping firms financing capital projects. The trap is blaming corporation tax.", "w": ["", "Stronger export growth would encourage rather than restrict investment by UK firms.", "Corporation tax is set by government fiscal policy, not by commercial banks refusing loans.", "Depreciation reflects wear and tear on machinery rather than banks withholding business finance."]}
{"id": "econ-216", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 3, "q": "How does a sustained rise in UK positive net investment affect both aggregate demand and long-run aggregate supply?", "o": ["Moves down along AD now and shifts LRAS leftwards", "Shifts AD rightwards now and leaves LRAS unchanged", "Shifts AD leftwards now and LRAS rightwards later", "Shifts AD and LRAS rightwards, the latter later"], "a": 3, "x": "Spending on capital goods boosts $I$, shifting AD rightwards immediately. Positive net investment expands the capital stock, shifting LRAS rightwards over time. The trap is forgetting the supply-side effect and treating investment as demand only.", "w": ["Investment is a component of AD, so it shifts AD rightwards rather than causing a movement along it; LRAS also shifts right.", "Positive net investment adds to the capital stock, which raises productive potential and shifts LRAS right.", "Higher investment is a component of AD, so it shifts AD rightwards rather than leftwards.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-217", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which item of UK public spending is counted directly in government expenditure ($G$) inside aggregate demand?", "o": ["Universal Credit payments to jobseekers", "Child Benefit paid to UK families", "Salaries paid to NHS hospital nurses", "State pension payments to retired residents"], "a": 2, "x": "$G$ includes spending on goods and services like NHS wages and roads. State pensions, Universal Credit and Child Benefit are transfer payments excluded from $G$. The trap is counting all public spending, including transfers, as $G$.", "w": ["Universal Credit is a welfare transfer payment, so counting it in G would double-count later spending.", "Child Benefit is a transfer payment redistributed to households rather than direct government purchases.", "", "State pensions are transfer payments with no current output exchanged; they enter AD only when spent as C."]}
{"id": "econ-218", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which example is discretionary fiscal policy rather than an automatic stabiliser?", "o": ["The Chancellor announces a £12bn rail building scheme", "Income tax revenue drops automatically during a recession", "VAT receipts rise naturally as consumers spend more in a boom", "Unemployment benefit claims fall automatically as hiring rises"], "a": 0, "x": "Discretionary fiscal policy is a deliberate government decision to change spending or tax rates. Automatic stabilisers react without any new policy announcement. The trap is treating tax and benefit changes that happen by themselves as discretionary.", "w": ["", "Falling income tax receipts in a recession are an automatic stabiliser built into the tax system.", "Higher VAT revenue during a boom happens automatically under existing tax rates, not via a new policy.", "Fewer welfare claims during growth happen automatically without the Chancellor changing rules."]}
{"id": "econ-219", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "In a made-up financial year, the UK government spends £1,015bn and collects £940bn in tax revenue. What does this represent?", "o": ["A national debt of £75bn", "A trade deficit of £75bn", "A fiscal surplus of £75bn", "A fiscal deficit of £75bn"], "a": 3, "x": "Government spending exceeds tax revenue by $£1,015\\text{bn}-£940\\text{bn}=£75\\text{bn}$, an annual fiscal (budget) deficit. The trap is confusing deficit with national debt.", "w": ["National debt is the cumulative total of all past borrowing, whereas £75bn is one year's deficit.", "A trade deficit measures exports minus imports, not government spending minus tax revenue.", "A fiscal surplus occurs when tax revenue exceeds government spending, the reverse of this case.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-220", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "To reduce demand-pull inflation during an overheating boom, the UK government cuts capital spending on new roads and raises income tax. What is this policy called and how does AD shift?", "o": ["Automatic stabilisation; AD moves up along the curve", "Contractionary monetary policy; AD shifts leftwards", "Expansionary fiscal policy; AD shifts rightwards", "Contractionary fiscal policy; AD shifts leftwards"], "a": 3, "x": "Cutting $G$ and raising taxes deliberately to cool AD is contractionary fiscal policy, shifting AD leftwards. The trap is calling tax and spending changes monetary policy.", "w": ["Deliberate changes to road budgets and tax rates are discretionary fiscal policy shifting AD.", "Monetary policy uses interest rates and money supply via the Bank of England, not roads and taxes.", "Expansionary fiscal policy raises G or cuts taxes to boost AD, the opposite of this policy.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-221", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Even when the UK economy grows steadily at its trend rate, why does an ageing population create upward pressure on government expenditure?", "o": ["Automatic increases in cyclical unemployment benefits", "Falling tax-free personal allowances for all earners", "Higher spending on primary schools for retired workers", "More demand for NHS care and adult social care"], "a": 3, "x": "An older population increases long-term demand for healthcare and social care, pushing up public spending independently of the trade cycle. The trap is blaming cyclical joblessness.", "w": ["Steady trend growth keeps cyclical unemployment low rather than driving up jobseeker claims.", "Changing tax allowances alters tax revenue rather than directly explaining higher healthcare spending.", "An ageing population shifts demand towards health and care rather than primary school places.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-222", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 3, "q": "A made-up economy is operating at full employment during a boom, yet the government still runs a £40bn fiscal deficit. What does this indicate?", "o": ["A zero national debt because output is at capacity", "A purely cyclical deficit that growth will soon erase", "A structural fiscal deficit needing policy reform", "That automatic stabilisers have raised welfare claims"], "a": 2, "x": "A cyclical deficit disappears at full employment. A deficit remaining in a boom is structural, needing discretionary spending cuts or tax rises to close. The trap is calling it cyclical and expecting growth to erase it.", "w": ["Running a £40bn deficit adds £40bn to national debt rather than making debt zero.", "A cyclical deficit occurs during a downturn and disappears once the economy reaches full employment.", "", "In a boom, automatic stabilisers lower welfare claims and lift tax revenue, shrinking the deficit."]}
{"id": "econ-223", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 1, "q": "What does the acronym SPICED help you remember about an appreciation of the pound (£)?", "o": ["Strong Pound: Interest Cuts, Exports Driven", "Strong Pound: Imports Cheaper, Exports Dearer", "Strong Pound: Inflation Climbs, Exports Double", "Strong Pound: Imports Costlier, Exports Depreciated"], "a": 1, "x": "A stronger pound buys more foreign currency, making imports cheaper in £ and UK exports dearer in foreign currency, which tends to worsen $(X-M)$. The trap is reversing the import effect: a strong pound makes imports cheaper, not costlier.", "w": ["SPICED summarises trade-price effects of exchange rates, not Bank Rate decisions.", "", "Cheaper imports tend to dampen rather than raise inflation, and dearer exports do not double.", "A stronger pound makes foreign goods cheaper in pounds, not costlier."]}
{"id": "econ-224", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 1, "q": "If the UK imposes a 15% tariff on imported steel, ceteris paribus, what is the immediate direct effect on UK imports of steel?", "o": ["Import volume rises because steel becomes cheaper", "Import volume falls as foreign steel gets dearer", "Import volume stays identical regardless of price", "UK exports of steel automatically double overseas"], "a": 1, "x": "A tariff is a tax on imports that raises their UK price, so buyers switch to UK suppliers and import volume falls. The trap is thinking a tariff makes imported steel cheaper, when it adds to its price.", "w": ["A tariff taxes imported steel, raising its UK price rather than making it cheaper.", "", "Unless demand is perfectly price-inelastic, a higher price reduces quantity demanded of imports.", "A UK import tariff does not automatically boost foreign demand for UK steel exports."]}
{"id": "econ-225", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "Why does rapid growth in UK real incomes often worsen UK net trade $(X-M)$ when UK households have a high marginal propensity to import?", "o": ["Higher UK incomes force UK exports down to zero", "Faster UK growth makes the pound fall by 50%", "UK households spend much extra income on imports", "Foreign countries ban UK exports in UK booms"], "a": 2, "x": "With a high marginal propensity to import, rising UK real incomes pull in more foreign goods and holidays ($M$ rises), worsening $(X-M)$ if $X$ lags behind. The trap is blaming the exchange rate or other countries instead of import demand.", "w": ["Higher UK incomes raise import spending; they do not cause foreign demand for UK exports to vanish.", "Nothing here implies a currency crash; the link is extra import spending from higher incomes, not a 50% fall in the pound.", "", "Trading partners do not ban UK goods simply because UK real incomes grow."]}
{"id": "econ-226", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "A UK engineering firm exports a machine priced at £100,000. If the exchange rate appreciates from £1 = €1.10 to £1 = €1.25, what happens to its euro price?", "o": ["Rises from €110,000 to €125,000", "Rises from €100,000 to €115,000", "Stays unchanged at €110,000 throughout", "Falls from €125,000 to €110,000"], "a": 0, "x": "At £1 = €1.10, £100,000 × 1.10 = €110,000. At £1 = €1.25, £100,000 × 1.25 = €125,000. Appreciation makes UK exports dearer abroad (SPICED). The trap is reversing the direction, as if a stronger pound cut export prices.", "w": ["", "This starts from €100,000 as if £1 equalled €1.00 initially instead of €1.10.", "A change in the exchange rate alters the foreign-currency price unless the UK firm cuts its £ price.", "This reverses the direction, treating an appreciation of sterling as a depreciation."]}
{"id": "econ-227", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "Despite a stronger pound, a UK maker of specialist medical scanners keeps increasing its overseas sales. Which factor best explains this?", "o": ["Global real incomes fell sharply across all markets", "Strong non-price competitiveness: quality and design", "UK inflation stayed much higher than foreign inflation", "UK tariffs on medical imports rose sharply"], "a": 1, "x": "Superior quality, innovation, brand reputation and after-sales service are non-price factors that make export demand less sensitive to higher prices. The trap is assuming price alone decides export sales.", "w": ["Falling world incomes reduce demand for UK exports rather than helping overseas sales expand.", "", "Higher UK inflation would worsen price competitiveness further on top of a stronger pound.", "UK import tariffs affect goods entering the UK rather than boosting foreign demand for UK scanners."]}
{"id": "econ-228", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 3, "q": "Country A puts high tariffs on UK exports, and the UK retaliates with tariffs on Country A's goods. What is the likely combined effect on UK net trade $(X-M)$?", "o": ["UK exports fall while UK imports automatically double", "Both $X$ and $M$ fall, leaving the net effect uncertain", "Net trade definitely improves because UK tariffs cut $M$", "Both $X$ and $M$ rise because tariffs stimulate trade"], "a": 1, "x": "Country A's tariffs cut UK exports ($X$) while UK tariffs cut imports ($M$). Because both fall, the net change in $(X-M)$ depends on which falls more. The trap is counting only the UK's own tariffs and ignoring lost exports.", "w": ["UK retaliatory tariffs raise the price of imports from Country A, cutting rather than doubling them.", "", "Focusing only on UK tariffs ignores the drop in UK exports caused by Country A's tariffs.", "Tariffs raise cross-border prices and shrink trade volumes rather than stimulating them."]}
{"id": "econ-337", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following causes a movement along, rather than a shift of, the short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) curve?", "o": ["A fall in world oil prices that cuts firms' costs", "A fall in the exchange rate that raises import prices", "A cut in employers' National Insurance contributions", "A rise in AD that raises the general price level"], "a": 3, "x": "A change in the price level, for example after an AD shift, moves the economy along SRAS. Costs, taxes and the exchange rate shift SRAS. The trap is treating a price level change as a shift.", "w": ["Lower oil prices cut firms' costs and shift SRAS right; the price level is not the cause.", "Dearer imports raise firms' costs and shift SRAS left; it is a shift, not a movement.", "Lower employment taxes reduce costs and shift SRAS right; this is a cost change, not a price level change.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-338", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following would shift the long-run aggregate supply (LRAS) curve to the right in the neoclassical view?", "o": ["A rise in aggregate demand caused by lower interest rates", "Better education and training that raises labour quality", "A rise in the general price level across the economy", "A fall in the price of raw materials for many firms"], "a": 1, "x": "LRAS shifts when potential output rises: more or better factors of production, or higher productivity. Better human capital does this. Demand changes and temporary cost falls do not alter potential output.", "w": ["Higher AD moves the economy along SRAS; it does not raise potential output, so LRAS does not shift.", "", "A higher price level is a movement along SRAS; it has no effect on the position of LRAS.", "Cheaper raw materials shift SRAS right but do not change productive capacity, so LRAS is unchanged."]}
{"id": "econ-339", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Aggregate demand is unchanged. A sharp rise in firms' energy costs shifts SRAS to the left. What is the short-run effect on the equilibrium price level and real GDP?", "o": ["The price level falls and real GDP rises", "The price level and real GDP both rise", "The price level rises and real GDP falls", "The price level rises and real GDP is unchanged"], "a": 2, "x": "A leftward SRAS shift with AD fixed moves equilibrium up along the AD curve to a higher price level and lower real output: cost-push inflation. Real GDP does not stay fixed in the short run.", "w": ["This describes a rightward SRAS shift, such as lower costs; the slip is reversing the direction of the shift.", "Both rising needs an AD shift right; a leftward SRAS shift cannot raise output along a downward-sloping AD curve.", "", "Unchanged output applies only on a vertical LRAS in the long run; this is a short-run shift, so output falls."]}
{"id": "econ-340", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The economy has a large amount of spare capacity and its AS curve is L-shaped (Keynesian). Aggregate demand increases. What is the most likely result?", "o": ["Real output rises, with little change in prices", "Mainly a higher price level, with real output unchanged", "Real output falls because LRAS shifts left", "Both the price level and potential output rise sharply"], "a": 0, "x": "On the flat section of the Keynesian AS curve, spare capacity means firms raise output without raising prices much. Only near full capacity does extra AD mainly raise the price level.", "w": ["", "This is the neoclassical vertical LRAS result at full employment, not the Keynesian result with spare capacity.", "AD does not shift LRAS left, and extra demand with spare capacity raises output rather than lowering it.", "Higher AD does not raise potential output; the flat section means output rises with little price change."]}
{"id": "econ-341", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Provisional ONS data released on 18 November 2026 showed UK producer input prices rose 10.8% in the year to October 2026. Other things equal, what does this suggest for AS?", "o": ["SRAS shifts to the right", "SRAS shifts to the left", "A movement up along SRAS", "LRAS shifts to the left permanently"], "a": 1, "x": "Higher input prices raise firms' costs of production, so at every price level they supply less: SRAS shifts left. One year of input price rises does not show a permanent fall in productive capacity.", "w": ["A right shift needs lower costs; rising input prices do the opposite.", "", "A movement along SRAS needs a change in the price level caused by AD; input prices are a cost factor, so the curve itself shifts.", "LRAS depends on capacity and productivity; a cost rise that may be temporary shifts SRAS, not LRAS."]}
{"id": "econ-342", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 3, "q": "The OBR's October 2026 assessment judged the 2026 energy shock would not permanently lower UK potential output. In AS analysis, the shock is best shown as:", "o": ["A leftward shift of SRAS with LRAS unchanged", "A rightward shift of LRAS and a leftward shift of SRAS", "A leftward shift of both SRAS and LRAS", "A movement along SRAS with no curve shifting"], "a": 0, "x": "Energy costs rise, so SRAS shifts left. If potential output is not permanently lowered, LRAS stays where it is. Confusing a temporary cost shock with a loss of productive capacity is the trap.", "w": ["", "LRAS moving right means higher potential output; an energy shock gives no reason for that.", "A left LRAS shift means permanently lower potential output, which the OBR judged would not happen.", "The shock changes firms' costs at every price level, so SRAS shifts; it is not caused by a price level change from AD."]}
{"id": "econ-343", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which one of the following would shift the UK short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) curve to the right?", "o": ["A rise in the world price of oil", "A rise in the rate of corporation tax", "Falling imported raw material costs", "A rise in the general price level"], "a": 2, "x": "Lower raw material costs reduce firms' costs at every price level, so SRAS shifts right. A price level rise is only a movement along SRAS.", "w": ["Dearer oil raises firms' costs, so SRAS shifts left, not right.", "A higher business tax raises costs, so SRAS shifts left.", "", "A change in the price level moves along SRAS; it does not shift the curve."]}
{"id": "econ-344", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In the year to October 2026, provisional ONS figures showed manufacturers' crude oil input prices surged 88.4%. Other things equal, what is the effect on SRAS?", "o": ["A movement up along SRAS to a higher price level", "SRAS shifts right, lowering the price level and raising real output", "SRAS shifts left, raising the price level and lowering real output", "AD shifts left because firms' costs have risen"], "a": 2, "x": "Dearer energy inputs raise firms' costs at every price level. SRAS shifts left (cost-push): the price level rises and real output falls. Cost changes shift curves; they do not cause movements along them.", "w": ["Confuses a shift with a movement: a cost change shifts the whole SRAS curve.", "Rising input prices cut supply; a rightward shift would need falling costs.", "", "Input costs are a supply-side factor and shift SRAS, not AD."]}
{"id": "econ-345", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "AHDB reported that sterling strengthened from €1.1850 on 16 October to €1.1943 on 23 October 2026. For a UK firm importing euro-priced inputs, what is the direct effect on SRAS?", "o": ["SRAS shifts left because UK exports become dearer abroad", "No shift, because exchange rates only affect aggregate demand", "SRAS shifts right because imported inputs cost fewer pounds", "A movement along SRAS because the price level has changed"], "a": 2, "x": "A stronger pound means each euro of imports costs fewer pounds, so unit costs fall at every price level and SRAS shifts right. Dearer exports affect AD, not this supply effect.", "w": ["Dearer exports affect net exports and AD, not the cost of imported inputs.", "Exchange rates also change imported input costs, so SRAS is affected as well as AD.", "", "Input costs changed, not the price level, so the whole curve shifts."]}
{"id": "econ-346", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Employer National Insurance rose from 13.8% to 15% from April 2025. Suppose a firm's pay bill subject to it is £1,000,000 (ignore thresholds). What is the effect?", "o": ["£12,000 extra cost; SRAS shifts right", "£12,000 extra cost; SRAS shifts left", "£150,000 extra cost; SRAS shifts left", "£1,200 extra cost; SRAS shifts left"], "a": 1, "x": "The rise is 15% − 13.8% = 1.2 percentage points; 1.2% of £1,000,000 = £12,000. Higher business costs shift SRAS left. £150,000 is the whole new bill, not the increase.", "w": ["The sum is right, but higher costs shift SRAS left, not right.", "", "£150,000 is the total bill at 15%, not the extra cost.", "Decimal slip: 1.2% of £1,000,000 is £12,000, not £1,200."]}
{"id": "econ-347", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Which pair of events would both shift UK SRAS to the left, other things equal?", "o": ["A fall in oil prices and a rise in employer National Insurance", "A cut in corporation tax and an appreciation of sterling", "A rise in income tax and a fall in consumer confidence", "A rise in gas prices and a depreciation of sterling"], "a": 3, "x": "Dearer gas and a weaker pound (dearer imported inputs) both raise firms' costs, shifting SRAS left. Income tax and confidence mainly affect AD.", "w": ["Cheaper oil would shift SRAS right; only the tax rise shifts it left.", "A corporation tax cut and a stronger pound both lower costs, shifting SRAS right.", "Income tax and confidence mainly affect household spending, so they shift AD, not SRAS.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-348", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Suppose £1 = €1.20 falls to £1 = €1.10. A firm buys €240,000 of euro-priced components. What happens to its cost in pounds?", "o": ["Rises by about £18,200, shifting SRAS left", "Rises by £24,000, shifting SRAS left", "Rises by £20,000, shifting SRAS left", "Falls by about £18,200, shifting SRAS right"], "a": 0, "x": "Before: 240,000 ÷ 1.20 = £200,000. After: 240,000 ÷ 1.10 ≈ £218,182. Cost rises by about £18,200 because sterling has depreciated. Higher costs shift SRAS left.", "w": ["", "£24,000 multiplies by 0.10 in euros instead of converting both costs into pounds.", "£20,000 takes 10% of £200,000, but the fall is 10 cents on a base of 1.20, not 10%.", "Fewer euros per pound means sterling is weaker, so costs rise, not fall."]}
{"id": "econ-349", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "In the classical model, the long-run aggregate supply (LRAS) curve is drawn as:", "o": ["Downward sloping as productivity rises", "Horizontal at the current price level", "Upward sloping throughout its length", "Vertical at full employment output"], "a": 3, "x": "Classical economists see the economy returning to full employment in the long run, so output is fixed by capacity and independent of the price level. The curve is vertical. A horizontal curve is only the Keynesian spare-capacity section.", "w": ["Downward sloping has no place in AS analysis; this confuses AS with demand.", "Horizontal is the Keynesian section with spare capacity, not the classical view that capacity fixes output.", "Upward sloping describes short-run AS or the Keynesian middle range, not classical LRAS.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-350", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which change would shift the LRAS curve to the right?", "o": ["A rise in consumer confidence about the economy", "A fall in the exchange rate against the euro", "A cut in the basic rate of income tax to boost demand", "A larger working-age population"], "a": 3, "x": "A larger working-age labour force raises the quantity of a factor of production and so potential output. Consumer confidence and tax cuts aimed at demand shift AD. Exchange rate falls mainly affect demand and costs short-run.", "w": ["Confidence raises consumption, which shifts AD, not capacity.", "An exchange rate fall mainly shifts AD through exports, and is not a change in capacity.", "A tax cut for demand shifts AD; only an incentive effect on labour supply would affect LRAS.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-351", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "On a Keynesian LRAS curve, an increase in aggregate demand from a level with large spare capacity will mainly lead to:", "o": ["A rise in real output with little price change", "A large rise in the price level with no change in output", "A fall in real output and a rise in prices", "A rightward shift of the LRAS curve itself"], "a": 0, "x": "The Keynesian curve is flat at low output because spare capacity lets firms raise output without raising costs. AD rises move along the curve, not shift it. Price rises dominate only near full capacity.", "w": ["", "This describes the vertical section near full capacity, not the spare-capacity section.", "Falling output with rising prices is stagflation, caused by a leftward supply shift, not AD rising.", "AD changes move the economy along the curve; they never shift LRAS."]}
{"id": "econ-352", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a new training programme raises the average skills of UK workers and productivity rises, with no change in the number of workers. Which is the most accurate effect on LRAS?", "o": ["LRAS shifts left because training costs rise sharply", "No shift, since the quantity of labour is unchanged", "LRAS shifts right as labour quality improves", "Movement along LRAS to a higher price level overall"], "a": 2, "x": "Productive capacity depends on quality as well as quantity of factors. Higher skills raise output per worker, so potential output rises and LRAS shifts right. Quantity unchanged is irrelevant here.", "w": ["Training costs are short-run and capacity still rises; the result is a rightward shift, not leftward.", "Slip: only quantity matters. Quality of labour also shifts LRAS.", "", "A price level change causes movement along AD or SRAS, not a change in LRAS."]}
{"id": "econ-353", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In 2025 the ONS estimated UK net capital stock grew 1.5% (to £5.9 trillion). Other things equal, what does growth of the capital stock do to the economy's productive potential?", "o": ["Shifts SRAS left through higher costs only", "Moves the economy along the vertical LRAS curve", "Raises AD only, leaving LRAS unchanged", "Raises potential output, shifting LRAS right"], "a": 3, "x": "Capital is a factor of production. More capital raises the economy's productive capacity, shifting LRAS right. It also raises AD through investment, but the lasting effect is on supply, so LRAS is not unchanged.", "w": ["Capital accumulation raises capacity; it does not cut supply, even if some costs rise.", "A shift is not a movement along the curve; capacity itself has changed.", "Investment is part of AD, but it also adds to capacity, so LRAS does not stay unchanged.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-354", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 3, "q": "An economy is on the vertical section of a Keynesian LRAS curve. The government raises AD with higher spending. Which outcome is most likely?", "o": ["Real GDP rises with prices unchanged", "Unemployment rises as real output falls", "LRAS shifts right as demand expands", "Prices rise but real output does not"], "a": 3, "x": "On the vertical section the economy is at full capacity, so extra AD cannot raise real output; it raises the price level. LRAS is determined by supply factors, not demand.", "w": ["Unchanged prices with rising output fits the flat spare-capacity section, not the vertical one.", "Higher AD does not lower output; the problem here is inflation.", "AD does not shift LRAS; only supply-side improvements do.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-355", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is a flow rather than a stock?", "o": ["A worker's wages earned during one month", "UK national net worth at the end of 2025", "A firm's value of machinery at the start of a year", "A household's savings balance on 31 December"], "a": 0, "x": "Income is a flow: it is measured over a period of time. Savings balances, net worth and the value of machinery are stocks, measured at one point in time. The trap is confusing wealth with income.", "w": ["", "Net worth is a stock measured at a point in time, not a flow.", "The value of capital at one date is a stock, not a flow.", "A balance on one date is a stock of wealth, not income earned over a period."]}
{"id": "econ-356", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the circular flow of income, which is an injection?", "o": ["Households saving in a bank", "Households paying income tax", "Government spending on hospitals", "Households buying imported cars"], "a": 2, "x": "Injections are investment, government spending and exports. Taxes, saving and imports are withdrawals. The trap is thinking any spending is an injection, when imports are spending leaving the flow.", "w": ["Saving is a withdrawal (S): income not passed on as spending on domestic output.", "Taxation is a withdrawal (T) because it removes income from the flow.", "", "Imports are a withdrawal (M); spending goes abroad, not to UK firms."]}
{"id": "econ-357", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose in a year saving is £40bn, taxes £90bn and imports £60bn, while investment is £50bn, government spending £95bn and exports £40bn. What is the effect on national income?", "o": ["It rises, because injections exceed withdrawals by £5bn", "It is unchanged, because injections equal withdrawals", "It falls, because withdrawals exceed injections by £35bn", "It falls, because withdrawals exceed injections by £5bn"], "a": 3, "x": "Withdrawals: 40 + 90 + 60 = £190bn. Injections: 50 + 95 + 40 = £185bn. Withdrawals exceed injections by £5bn, so national income falls.", "w": ["Injections total £185bn, below withdrawals of £190bn, so income does not rise.", "Totals are £185bn and £190bn, which are not equal, so the flow is not in equilibrium.", "The gap is 190 − 185 = £5bn, not £35bn; this comes from an addition slip.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-358", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Which statement correctly distinguishes income from wealth for a household?", "o": ["Income is received over a period; wealth is a stock of net assets", "Both are flows, but wealth is measured after tax is paid", "A rise in house prices raises the household's income that year", "Wealth is earned each month; income is the value of assets owned"], "a": 0, "x": "Income is a flow over a period; wealth is a stock of assets (net of liabilities) at a point in time. The trap is reversing them, or treating a rise in asset values as income.", "w": ["", "Wealth is a stock, not a flow; tax treatment does not define the difference.", "A house price rise increases wealth (a stock), not income earned in the period.", "This reverses the two terms: income is the flow, wealth is the stock."]}
{"id": "econ-359", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In 2025 the ONS put UK national net worth at £13.3 trillion and UK Gross National Income at £3,009.3 billion. Which description is correct?", "o": ["Both are flows measured over 2025", "Both are stocks measured at the end of 2025", "Net worth is a flow; Gross National Income is a stock", "Net worth is a stock; Gross National Income a flow"], "a": 3, "x": "Net worth is the stock of assets less liabilities at a point in time. Gross National Income is the income earned over the year, a flow. The trap is treating the bigger figure as income.", "w": ["Net worth is measured at a point in time, so it is not a flow.", "Gross National Income is earned over a period, so it is a flow.", "The labels are reversed: income is the flow, net worth the stock.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-360", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 3, "q": "In a closed economy with no government, households save more while firms' planned investment is unchanged. What does the circular flow predict, other things equal?", "o": ["Income rises until investment equals saving", "Income is unchanged because saving stays in the economy", "Income falls until saving again equals investment", "Investment rises automatically to match the extra saving"], "a": 2, "x": "Higher saving is a bigger withdrawal with no matching injection, so J < W and national income falls. It keeps falling until W = J (saving equals investment again). The trap is assuming saved money is automatically re-spent.", "w": ["Higher withdrawals relative to injections reduce income; they do not raise it.", "Saving is a withdrawal; it is not automatically re-injected as investment.", "", "Nothing in the model makes investment rise automatically; planned investment is given here."]}
{"id": "econ-361", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an injection into the circular flow of income?", "o": ["Household saving in a bank account", "Income tax paid to the government", "Households buying imported cars", "Government spending on a new hospital"], "a": 3, "x": "Government spending (G) adds to the flow, so it is an injection. Saving, imports and taxation all take income out of the flow, so they are withdrawals.", "w": ["Saving is a withdrawal (leakage) because income is not passed on as spending.", "Taxation is a withdrawal; only government spending is the injection.", "Imports are a withdrawal: spending goes abroad, not to UK firms.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-362", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is a withdrawal from the circular flow of income?", "o": ["Taxation paid by households and firms", "Government spending on new roads", "Investment by a firm in new machinery", "Exports of goods sold to Germany"], "a": 0, "x": "Taxation (T) is a withdrawal. Along with saving (S) and imports (M), it removes income from the domestic flow. Exports, G and investment are injections.", "w": ["", "Government spending is an injection (G).", "Investment is an injection (I); it is firms' spending, not a leakage.", "Exports bring in spending from abroad, so they are an injection, not a withdrawal."]}
{"id": "econ-363", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose planned withdrawals in an economy are £400bn and planned injections are £430bn. Other things equal, what happens to national income?", "o": ["It falls, because withdrawals are leakages from the flow", "It rises until withdrawals have risen to equal injections", "It stays the same, because both injections and withdrawals are positive", "It rises permanently, as long as injections are positive"], "a": 1, "x": "Injections exceed withdrawals by £30bn, so income rises. Higher income raises S, T and M until W = J. The trap is thinking any positive injection keeps income rising forever.", "w": ["This reverses the comparison: withdrawals are smaller than injections here, so income rises.", "", "Equilibrium needs J = W, not both being positive; here J is greater.", "Growth stops once rising income lifts withdrawals to equal injections."]}
{"id": "econ-364", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "ONS data for Q3 2026 (released 12 November 2026) recorded UK imports of £255.9 billion and exports of £245.5 billion. What was trade's net effect on the circular flow?", "o": ["A net injection of £501.4 billion", "A net injection of £10.4 billion", "A net withdrawal of £501.4 billion", "A net withdrawal of £10.4 billion"], "a": 3, "x": "Imports (a withdrawal) exceed exports (an injection): 255.9 − 245.5 = 10.4. So trade is a net withdrawal of £10.4 billion. Adding the two figures is a slip.", "w": ["Adding the figures is wrong, and imports are a withdrawal, so the direction is also wrong.", "This mixes up the two: imports are the withdrawal, and they are larger than exports.", "Adding imports and exports (255.9 + 245.5) is wrong; the net effect is the difference.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-365", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose investment is £50bn, government spending £120bn, exports £90bn, saving £60bn, taxation £110bn and imports £100bn. What happens to national income?", "o": ["It rises, because injections are £260bn", "It falls, as withdrawals of £270bn exceed injections of £260bn", "It stays the same, because the economy is in equilibrium", "It rises, because injections exceed withdrawals by £10bn"], "a": 1, "x": "J = 50 + 120 + 90 = £260bn. W = 60 + 110 + 100 = £270bn. Withdrawals exceed injections by £10bn, so national income falls until J = W.", "w": ["A large J alone does not matter; it must be compared with W (£270bn).", "", "Equilibrium needs J = W; here they differ by £10bn.", "Withdrawals are £270bn, so it is withdrawals that exceed injections, not the reverse."]}
{"id": "econ-366", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Starting from equilibrium, government spending rises £10bn, imports rise £15bn and saving falls £3bn; other items are unchanged. What is the pressure on national income?", "o": ["Downward: withdrawals rise £12bn, injections only £10bn", "Upward, because government spending is an injection", "Downward, because withdrawals rise £15bn and injections £10bn", "Upward, because saving falls so withdrawals fall overall"], "a": 0, "x": "Change in J = +£10bn. Change in W = +15 − 3 = +£12bn. Withdrawals now exceed injections by £2bn, so income falls. Ignoring the fall in saving gives £15bn, which is wrong.", "w": ["", "This looks only at G; all changes in J and W must be netted off.", "The fall in saving offsets part of the rise in imports, so W rises by £12bn, not £15bn.", "Imports rise by more than saving falls, so total withdrawals rise."]}
{"id": "econ-367", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 1, "q": "In the AD/AS model, where is short-run macroeconomic equilibrium found?", "o": ["Where AD intersects the vertical LRAS curve", "Where aggregate demand equals potential output", "Where SRAS intersects the LRAS curve", "Where AD intersects the SRAS curve"], "a": 3, "x": "Short-run equilibrium is where AD meets SRAS, fixing real output and the price level. It need not be at potential output, so the LRAS curve is not required.", "w": ["LRAS shows potential output, not the short-run equilibrium; the short-run equilibrium can be away from it.", "Potential output is shown by LRAS; AD can equal output below or above it, so this is not the definition.", "SRAS and LRAS crossing says nothing about demand; equilibrium needs AD, so this leaves out a curve.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-368", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 1, "q": "In November 2026 the Bank of England projected that Middle East energy shocks would push UK CPI inflation to around 5% by mid-2027. Which shift best shows an energy cost shock?", "o": ["AD shifts right, raising output and the price level", "SRAS shifts left, cutting output and raising prices", "SRAS shifts right, lowering prices and raising output", "LRAS shifts right, raising potential output"], "a": 1, "x": "Higher energy prices raise firms' costs, so SRAS shifts left. At the new equilibrium, real output falls and the price level rises. The trap is treating a cost shock as a change in demand.", "w": ["Energy costs hit supply, not demand; this treats a cost shock as an AD change.", "", "This is the effect of lower costs; it has the direction of the shift reversed.", "An energy price rise does not raise productive capacity; LRAS does not shift right here."]}
{"id": "econ-369", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "The Keynesian LRAS curve has a horizontal section at low output. Suppose AD shifts right while the economy is on this section. What happens to real output and the price level?", "o": ["Real output rises; the price level stays the same", "Real output stays the same; the price level rises", "Real output and the price level both rise sharply", "Real output falls; the price level rises"], "a": 0, "x": "On the horizontal section there is plenty of spare capacity, so firms raise output without raising prices. Applying the classical vertical result here is the trap.", "w": ["", "This is the classical vertical LRAS result; it ignores the spare capacity on the horizontal section.", "Prices rise only as capacity runs short; on the horizontal section they do not.", "A rise in AD cannot reduce output; this is the effect of an SRAS shift left."]}
{"id": "econ-370", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "On the classical model the economy is at full capacity on a vertical LRAS curve. AD shifts right. What is the long-run effect?", "o": ["Real output is unchanged and the price level is higher", "Real output rises and the price level falls", "Real output falls and the price level rises", "Real output rises and the price level is unchanged"], "a": 0, "x": "A vertical LRAS means output is fixed at potential. More AD only raises the price level. The trap is expecting AD to raise long-run real output.", "w": ["", "A rise in AD pushes the price level up, not down; this also wrongly raises output.", "Output cannot fall because AD increased; this is an SRAS shift left result.", "This is the Keynesian horizontal result; classical LRAS is vertical so output cannot rise permanently."]}
{"id": "econ-371", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Falling consumer confidence shifts AD left. At the same time, lower oil prices shift SRAS right. What is the certain effect at the new equilibrium?", "o": ["Real output rises; the price level rises", "The price level rises; real output falls", "Real output falls; the price level change is uncertain", "The price level falls; the output change is uncertain"], "a": 3, "x": "Both shifts lower the price level (AD left, SRAS right). Output falls with the AD shift but rises with the SRAS shift, so the net effect depends on the sizes.", "w": ["AD left lowers both output and prices, so output rising with prices is not a combined result.", "Neither shift raises the price level here; AD left and SRAS right both lower it.", "Output has opposing effects, so it is the uncertain one; the price level is the certain fall.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-372", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 3, "q": "Classical economists say a fall in AD leaves output below potential. What do they predict happens in the long run if wages and prices are flexible?", "o": ["SRAS shifts right as wages fall, restoring potential output at lower prices", "LRAS shifts left until it meets the new, lower AD curve", "Output stays low unless the government raises AD itself", "AD shifts back right on its own, with the price level unchanged"], "a": 0, "x": "Classical economists expect self-correction: spare capacity pushes wages down, SRAS shifts right and output returns to potential at a lower price level. Keynesians dispute this, as wages can be sticky.", "w": ["", "LRAS shows potential output, and weak AD does not reduce it; the adjustment is in SRAS.", "This is the Keynesian view that intervention is needed; classical economists expect self-correction.", "Classical self-correction operates through SRAS and wages, not by AD rebounding; the price level falls."]}
{"id": "econ-373", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which event is an example of potential economic growth?", "o": ["A fall in unemployment as firms hire more workers", "A rise in consumer spending after a cut in interest rates", "An outward PPF shift caused by improved technology", "A rise in exports using previously spare capacity"], "a": 2, "x": "Potential growth is a rise in productive capacity, shown by an outward PPF or LRAS shift. Technology improves productivity. The others use spare capacity, so they are actual growth.", "w": ["Using spare labour is a move towards the PPF, so it is actual growth, not potential.", "Higher demand raises actual output only; capacity is unchanged, a common AD confusion.", "", "Exports using spare capacity move the economy towards the PPF, so this is actual growth."]}
{"id": "econ-374", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the AD/AS model, which change would cause actual growth without any change in potential output?", "o": ["A rightward shift of the LRAS curve", "AD rises when there is spare capacity", "A rise in the size of the labour force", "A rise in the stock of capital goods"], "a": 1, "x": "With spare capacity, higher AD raises real GDP along the short-run AS curve. Potential output (LRAS) is unchanged. Labour and capital changes shift LRAS, so they raise potential output.", "w": ["LRAS shifting right is potential growth by definition.", "", "A larger labour force raises productive capacity, so it is potential growth.", "More capital raises productive capacity, so it shifts LRAS and is potential growth."]}
{"id": "econ-375", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose real GDP is £1,800bn in Year 1 and £1,854bn in Year 2. What is the actual growth rate?", "o": ["5.4%", "3.3%", "3.0%", "2.9%"], "a": 2, "x": "Growth = change ÷ original × 100 = 54 ÷ 1,800 × 100 = 3.0%. Using the new figure as the base gives 2.9%, the usual slip.", "w": ["5.4% divides by 1,000, not by the original £1,800bn.", "3.3% is a miscalculation; 54 ÷ 1,800 gives exactly 3.0%.", "", "2.9% divides by the new GDP (1,854) instead of the original value."]}
{"id": "econ-376", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A country's real GDP grows 2.5% a year while its potential growth is 1.5% a year. Which statement about the output gap is correct?", "o": ["Any negative gap widens, or a positive gap narrows", "Any negative gap narrows, or a positive gap widens", "The output gap is unchanged, as both are growing", "Any gap closes, whether negative or positive"], "a": 1, "x": "Actual growth above potential growth means actual output gains on capacity. A negative gap narrows towards zero, and a positive gap widens.", "w": ["This reverses the result: actual growth above potential growth uses up spare capacity, so a negative gap narrows.", "", "The gap changes whenever actual and potential growth rates differ, even if both are positive.", "A positive gap widens when actual growth exceeds potential growth; only a negative gap moves towards zero."]}
{"id": "econ-377", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In the 12 months to September 2026, UK exports rose 6.2% to £956.8 billion (ONS/GOV.UK). Why might this support export-led growth?", "o": ["Higher exports always raise potential output permanently", "Exports are part of AD, so higher exports raise real GDP", "Exports reduce the need for imports, so AD falls", "Exports raise AS directly by lowering firms' costs"], "a": 1, "x": "Exports (X) are a component of AD: AD = C + I + G + (X − M). A rise shifts AD right and raises actual growth. Potential growth needs capacity to rise too.", "w": ["Exports lift AD; they only affect capacity if they encourage investment, so 'always' is wrong.", "", "Exports do not reduce imports, and higher X raises AD, not lowers it.", "Exports are demand-side. They do not directly shift AS."]}
{"id": "econ-378", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 3, "q": "A country relies on export-led growth. Which is the most relevant risk?", "o": ["Exports increase the money supply and so cause deflation", "Higher exports always worsen the current account balance", "A recession in main trading partners cuts export demand", "Export growth cannot raise real GDP in the short run"], "a": 2, "x": "Export-led growth depends on foreign demand, so a downturn in trading partners reduces X and AD. Exports raise real GDP in the short run and improve, not worsen, the trade balance.", "w": ["Exports do not cause deflation by themselves; this is an invented link.", "Higher exports improve the trade balance, so this reverses the effect.", "", "Higher X raises AD and real GDP in the short run, so this is false."]}
{"id": "econ-379", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement correctly describes a negative output gap?", "o": ["Actual output is above potential output, with firms stretched", "Actual output is below potential, so there is spare capacity", "Potential output is falling faster than actual output does", "Real GDP is falling in the current period, quarter on quarter"], "a": 1, "x": "A negative output gap means actual output is below potential, leaving spare capacity. It is a level comparison, not a growth rate, so it can exist while GDP is rising.", "w": ["This describes a positive output gap, not a negative one; the sign is reversed.", "", "A gap compares the levels of actual and potential output, not their rates of change.", "Confuses a negative gap with a recession; GDP can grow while the gap stays negative."]}
{"id": "econ-380", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose actual real GDP is £1,764bn and potential output is £1,800bn. What is the output gap as a percentage of potential output?", "o": ["-2.04%", "-2.0%", "+2.0%", "-36%"], "a": 1, "x": "Gap = 1,764 − 1,800 = −£36bn. Divide by potential: −36 ÷ 1,800 × 100 = −2.0%. Using actual GDP as the denominator is the trap.", "w": ["Divides by actual GDP (36 ÷ 1,764) instead of potential output.", "", "Gets the sign wrong; actual output is below potential, so the gap is negative.", "Gives the £bn gap as a percentage without dividing by potential output."]}
{"id": "econ-381", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is the most likely consequence of a positive output gap?", "o": ["Unemployment rising above the natural rate", "Falling wage growth as workers become scarcer", "Rising spare capacity in most firms", "Upward pressure on wages and the price level"], "a": 3, "x": "With actual output above potential, firms compete for scarce labour and resources, so wages and costs rise and demand-pull inflation pressure builds. Spare capacity and cyclical unemployment go with a negative gap.", "w": ["Unemployment above the natural rate goes with a negative gap, not a positive one.", "Scarce workers push wage growth up, not down.", "Spare capacity appears when actual output is below potential, the opposite case.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-382", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "On an AD/AS diagram with a vertical LRAS at potential output Y*, which shows a negative output gap?", "o": ["AD and SRAS intersect to the right of LRAS, above Y*", "AD and SRAS intersect exactly on the LRAS curve at Y*", "LRAS shifts right while AD and SRAS stay fixed", "AD and SRAS intersect to the left of LRAS, below Y*"], "a": 3, "x": "The equilibrium output is read from where AD meets SRAS. If that is left of the vertical LRAS, real output is below Y*, which is a negative gap.", "w": ["This shows output above Y*, which is a positive output gap.", "Equilibrium on LRAS means actual equals potential, so the gap is zero.", "Shifting LRAS right is growth in potential output; it would widen a gap, but it is not itself the diagram of one.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-383", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "ONS analysis published in 2026 found a mean absolute revision of 0.27 percentage points between first quarterly UK GDP estimates and final estimates since Q1 2000. Why does this matter for output gaps?", "o": ["It shows potential output can be observed directly", "First GDP estimates may change, so the measured gap may too", "It shows output gaps are always close to zero", "It affects the price level but not the size of the gap"], "a": 1, "x": "Actual output is itself revised, and potential output cannot be observed, only estimated. Real-time gap estimates are therefore uncertain and policy may respond to a wrong figure.", "w": ["Potential output is never directly observed; it is estimated, which is the problem.", "", "Revisions say nothing about the gap being near zero; the gap's size is simply uncertain.", "The gap is calculated from output, so revisions to GDP change the gap directly."]}
{"id": "econ-384", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Suppose actual GDP grows from £500bn to £505bn and potential output grows from £500bn to £510bn. The gap began at zero. What is the new output gap, to one decimal place?", "o": ["-1.0%", "+1.0%", "-0.5%", "-5.0%"], "a": 0, "x": "Gap = 505 − 510 = −£5bn. As a percentage of potential: −5 ÷ 510 × 100 = −0.98%, which is −1.0% to one decimal place. Actual grew more slowly than potential.", "w": ["", "Wrong sign; actual output is below potential, so the gap is negative.", "Halves the answer, perhaps by dividing by the wrong total; the calculation gives −0.98%.", "Reads the £5bn gap as a percentage without dividing by potential output."]}
{"id": "econ-385", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which combination is most typical of the boom phase of the trade cycle?", "o": ["Rising unemployment and falling business confidence", "Falling investment and rising government borrowing", "Falling real GDP and falling consumer spending", "Fast growth, falling unemployment, rising prices"], "a": 3, "x": "In a boom, aggregate demand grows quickly, firms hire and invest, unemployment falls and inflation pressure builds. The other options describe recession features, a common mix-up of the two phases.", "w": ["Rising unemployment and falling confidence are recession features, not boom features.", "Falling investment and higher borrowing fit a recession; in a boom firms invest and borrowing falls.", "Falling real GDP and falling spending define a recession, the opposite phase.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-386", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 1, "q": "In the UK, a 'technical recession' is usually defined as:", "o": ["One quarter of falling real GDP", "Two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP", "Two consecutive quarters of growth below trend", "Falling real GDP over a full calendar year"], "a": 1, "x": "A technical recession is two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth. Growth that is merely below trend, or a single negative quarter, does not meet the definition.", "w": ["One negative quarter is not enough; the definition needs two in a row.", "", "Growth below trend can still be positive, so this describes a slowdown, not a recession.", "The definition uses quarters, not a full year of falling output."]}
{"id": "econ-387", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "ONS data (Blue Book 2026, 20 August 2026): UK real GDP fell 0.2% in Q3 2023 and 0.3% in Q4 2023, then rose 0.7% in Q1 2024. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["The UK was in recession only in Q4 2023 because the fall was larger", "The UK avoided recession because the falls were small", "A technical recession occurred in late 2023, recovery from Q1 2024", "The UK entered recession in Q1 2024 when growth turned positive"], "a": 2, "x": "Two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP (Q3 and Q4 2023) meet the technical recession definition. Size of fall is irrelevant. Positive growth in Q1 2024 marks the start of recovery.", "w": ["Both quarters count; the definition needs consecutive falls, not the largest fall.", "Size does not matter; any two consecutive quarterly falls make a technical recession.", "", "Positive growth signals recovery, not recession; the downturn was in Q3 and Q4 2023."]}
{"id": "econ-388", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose an economy's quarterly real GDP growth is +0.4%, −0.1%, −0.3%, 0.0%. At the end of which quarter was a technical recession first confirmed?", "o": ["Quarter 2, because GDP first fell", "It was never confirmed, because the falls were small", "Quarter 4, because growth reached zero", "Quarter 3, as the second consecutive fall"], "a": 3, "x": "Quarters 2 and 3 are consecutive falls, so the definition is first met at the end of quarter 3. One fall alone is not enough, and size of fall is irrelevant.", "w": ["One fall is not a recession; a second consecutive fall is needed.", "Small falls still count; the definition concerns direction, not size.", "Zero growth is not a fall, and the condition was already met in quarter 3.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-389", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 3, "q": "Suppose growth falls from 3% a year to 1% a year. Unemployment is still edging down and inflation is still above target. Which phase best describes this?", "o": ["The trough of the cycle, because growth is low", "A recession, because the growth rate has fallen sharply", "A slowdown after a boom: output still rises, more slowly", "The recovery phase, because unemployment is still falling"], "a": 2, "x": "Real GDP still rises, so there is no recession. Growth falling from a high rate is a downturn or slowdown. A fall in the growth rate is not the same as a fall in output.", "w": ["The trough is where output is lowest, with falling GDP; here GDP is still growing.", "A recession needs falling real GDP; 1% growth is still positive output growth.", "", "Recovery follows a recession with accelerating growth; here growth is slowing after high growth."]}
{"id": "econ-390", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "During a recession, what is the most likely effect on UK government finances, other things equal?", "o": ["Tax revenue rises and benefit spending falls, so borrowing falls", "Tax revenue rises as unemployment rises, so borrowing falls", "Tax revenue and benefit spending both fall, so borrowing is unchanged", "Tax revenue falls and benefit spending rises, so borrowing rises"], "a": 3, "x": "Lower incomes, spending and profits cut income tax, VAT and corporation tax receipts, while more unemployment raises benefit payments. The budget deficit widens, so borrowing rises. These are automatic stabilisers.", "w": ["This is the boom pattern; in recession the effects reverse.", "Rising unemployment means fewer taxpayers and lower incomes, so tax revenue falls.", "Benefit spending rises with unemployment, so the effects do not both fall.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-391", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is a benefit of economic growth for the government?", "o": ["Lower tax revenue because incomes are higher", "Higher tax revenue and lower unemployment benefit spending", "A guaranteed fall in income inequality across households", "A fall in the demand for public services such as health"], "a": 1, "x": "Growth raises incomes and employment, so tax receipts rise and benefit spending tends to fall, improving the fiscal position. Growth does not guarantee lower inequality, a common trap.", "w": ["Higher incomes and spending raise income tax and VAT receipts, not lower them; this reverses the link.", "", "Growth can widen inequality if gains go mainly to high earners; 'guaranteed' is wrong.", "Demand for health and education often rises with incomes and population; this is unsupported."]}
{"id": "econ-392", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is a likely cost of rapid economic growth?", "o": ["A fall in the average price level in every case", "A fall in firms' profits as consumer demand rises", "More strain on non-renewables and more pollution", "Lower purchasing power for consumers as incomes rise"], "a": 2, "x": "Faster growth raises output and resource use, which can deplete non-renewables and raise pollution (negative externalities). Growth tends to raise, not lower, profits and purchasing power.", "w": ["Rapid growth, if demand outpaces capacity, tends to raise inflation, not guarantee falling prices.", "Rising demand usually lifts firms' sales and profits, so this is the wrong direction.", "", "Growth normally raises real incomes and so purchasing power, unless inflation outpaces wages."]}
{"id": "econ-393", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "lv": 2, "q": "UK real GDP per head rises but a few high earners gain most of the extra income. Which is the best conclusion about living standards?", "o": ["Everyone's living standards must have risen equally", "Living standards fell because GDP is only an average", "Average living standards rose, but inequality may be wider", "Nothing can be said, as GDP does not measure living standards"], "a": 2, "x": "Real GDP per head is an average, so it can rise while gains are unevenly shared. It is still a useful but imperfect indicator. Do not assume equal gains.", "w": ["Averages hide distribution; equal gains is the trap of treating the mean as everyone's experience.", "A rising average does not mean living standards fell; this misreads the limitation.", "", "GDP per head is an imperfect but still relevant guide, so 'nothing can be said' is too extreme."]}
{"id": "econ-394", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A toy shop's sales rise 6% as incomes grow, but it must pay higher wages as labour becomes scarce. What is the best description of growth for this firm?", "o": ["Only a benefit, since costs cannot rise in a boom", "Only a cost, since wages rise as labour gets scarcer", "Higher demand benefits it, partly offset by higher labour costs", "No effect at all, because prices are fixed in the economy"], "a": 2, "x": "Growth raises demand and profit opportunities, but tight labour markets can raise wage costs. The net effect depends on whether revenue gains exceed cost increases.", "w": ["Ignores that growth can raise wage and input costs; one-sided analysis.", "Ignores the rise in sales and profit; one-sided analysis.", "", "Prices are not fixed in the economy; this ignores both demand and cost effects."]}
{"id": "econ-395", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "lv": 2, "q": "DESNZ provisional data recorded UK territorial emissions falling 1.8% in 2025 while the economy grew. This is best described as:", "o": ["Growth causing a fall in output", "Evidence that growth always harms the environment", "Decoupling of growth from emissions", "Evidence of a rise in negative externalities"], "a": 2, "x": "Output rising while emissions fall shows decoupling, helped by renewables. It challenges the idea that growth must raise pollution, though one year's data is not proof for all environmental harm.", "w": ["Output was growing, so growth did not cause output to fall; this confuses the variables.", "The data show emissions fell, contradicting 'always harms'; this overgeneralises.", "", "Emissions fell, so this particular negative externality declined, not rose."]}
{"id": "econ-396", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "lv": 3, "q": "UK real household disposable income per head rose 1.0% in Q2 2026. Which best evaluates the benefit to consumers?", "o": ["Every consumer gains, since the average rose 1.0%", "Only firms gain from higher disposable income", "Consumers always lose whenever growth occurs", "It depends on distribution and on price changes"], "a": 3, "x": "An average rise in real income raises purchasing power overall, but individual gains depend on who receives income and on price changes. Evaluation should consider distribution, not just the average.", "w": ["Treats an average as applying to everyone; ignores groups whose incomes lag prices.", "Consumers gain purchasing power directly; firms also gain from demand, but not only firms.", "Growth generally raises real incomes; 'whenever' is wrong and unsupported.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-397", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best describes the macroeconomic objective of balance of payments equilibrium on the current account?", "o": ["Government spending equals tax revenue over the whole year", "Trade in goods, services, income and transfers roughly balances", "The exchange rate stays fixed against all other currencies", "The value of visible exports equals the value of visible imports only"], "a": 1, "x": "The current account records trade in goods and services plus income and transfers. Equilibrium means the balance is roughly zero over time. Government spending and tax describe the budget, a different objective.", "w": ["This describes a balanced government budget, not the current account. Mixing up the two 'balances' is the classic slip.", "", "The objective concerns the balance of payments flows, not fixing the exchange rate. Exchange rate stability is a separate idea.", "The current account includes services, primary income and secondary income as well as goods. Visible trade alone is too narrow."]}
{"id": "econ-398", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "lv": 1, "q": "DWP data published on 26 March 2026 showed a UK Gini coefficient of 33% before housing costs and 37% after housing costs in FYE 2025. What does this suggest?", "o": ["Average incomes fell by 4% once housing costs were counted", "Income was more equally distributed after housing costs than before", "Housing costs had no effect on the distribution of income", "Income was less equal after housing costs than before"], "a": 3, "x": "A higher Gini coefficient means greater inequality. 37% after housing costs is above 33% before, so income was less equally distributed once housing costs were counted. The Gini is not an income level.", "w": ["The Gini coefficient measures inequality, not average income. A 4 point gap is not a 4% fall in income.", "Reverses the scale. A higher Gini coefficient means more inequality, not more equality.", "The two figures differ (33% and 37%), so housing costs did change the measured distribution.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-399", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Bank of England data showed UK CPI inflation at 3.6% in October 2026, against the official 2% CPI target. How does inflation compare with the target?", "o": ["It was 1.8 percentage points above the target", "It was 1.6 percentage points above the target", "It was 1.6 percentage points below the target", "It met the target, as inflation was positive and stable"], "a": 1, "x": "3.6 − 2 = 1.6 percentage points above the target, so the low inflation objective was not being met. Dividing 3.6 by 2 is the wrong operation for comparing two rates.", "w": ["Divides 3.6 by 2 to get 1.8 instead of subtracting. The gap between two rates is found by subtraction.", "", "The size is right but the direction is wrong. 3.6% is higher than 2%, so inflation is above target.", "The target is 2%, not any positive rate. Inflation of 3.6% is well above it."]}
{"id": "econ-400", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "lv": 2, "q": "ONS data for July to September 2026 showed unemployment at 4.6% and employment (ages 16 to 64) at 75.4%. Why can the unemployment rate not be found as 100% − 75.4%?", "o": ["Because 24.6% of people aged 16 to 64 are unemployed", "Because some of the 24.6% are economically inactive, not unemployed", "Because unemployment is measured as a share of the whole population", "Because the two rates are measured over different time periods"], "a": 1, "x": "The unemployed are those without a job who are seeking and available for work. Students, carers and others not looking are inactive, so they are neither employed nor unemployed. Unemployment is a share of the economically active.", "w": ["This treats everyone not in work as unemployed. It ignores the economically inactive.", "", "Unemployment is a share of the economically active (labour force), not the whole population.", "Both figures cover the same period. Timing is not the problem."]}
{"id": "econ-401", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The OBR's October 2026 forecast projected a £41 billion UK current budget deficit in 2026–27. What does a current budget deficit mean?", "o": ["The accumulated national debt stands at £41 billion in total", "Total government borrowing, including investment, is £41 billion", "Day-to-day spending exceeds tax revenue, excluding investment", "Tax revenue exceeds current spending by £41 billion"], "a": 2, "x": "The current budget compares revenue with current (day-to-day) spending, excluding capital investment. A deficit means spending is higher. A deficit is a yearly flow, not the stock of debt.", "w": ["Confuses a yearly flow (deficit) with the stock of accumulated debt.", "The current budget excludes capital investment, so this describes total borrowing, not the current deficit.", "", "This describes a surplus, not a deficit. The direction is reversed."]}
{"id": "econ-402", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "lv": 3, "q": "Suppose exports are £380bn and imports £400bn (ignore income and transfers). Growth raises imports by 10%, exports unchanged. What happens to the current account?", "o": ["The deficit widens from £20bn to £60bn", "The deficit narrows because higher GDP raises exports", "The deficit widens from £20bn to £40bn", "The deficit disappears as growth improves competitiveness"], "a": 0, "x": "Imports rise by 10% of £400bn = £40bn, to £440bn. The balance moves from −£20bn to 380 − 440 = −£60bn. This shows growth conflicting with current account balance, as higher incomes pull in imports.", "w": ["", "Exports were unchanged and imports rose, so the deficit cannot narrow. Growth often sucks in imports.", "Takes £40bn as the new deficit, but the deficit rises by £40bn on top of £20bn, giving £60bn.", "Exports are unchanged and imports rise, so the gap grows. Nothing here improves competitiveness."]}
{"id": "econ-403", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an example of a market-based supply-side policy?", "o": ["Government funding for a new rail link", "Government-funded apprenticeship training", "Privatising a state-owned industry", "Increasing R&D grants to universities"], "a": 2, "x": "Privatisation reduces the state's role and exposes firms to competition and the profit motive, so it is market-based. Spending on rail, training and R&D is interventionist: a common slip is treating all supply-side policy as market-based.", "w": ["Government-funded infrastructure is interventionist, because the state spends directly to raise capacity.", "Government-funded training is interventionist, because the state pays for human capital rather than leaving it to the market.", "", "R&D grants are interventionist subsidies; they are not about freeing markets."]}
{"id": "econ-404", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 1, "q": "On an AD/AS diagram, a successful supply-side policy is shown by which of the following, with AD unchanged?", "o": ["A rightward shift of AD, raising the price level", "A leftward shift of SRAS, as costs rise for firms", "A movement up along the existing LRAS curve", "A rightward shift of LRAS, lowering prices and raising output"], "a": 3, "x": "Supply-side policies raise productive capacity, so LRAS shifts right. With AD unchanged, the equilibrium price level falls and real output rises. Confusing this with an AD shift is the usual slip.", "w": ["A rightward AD shift is a demand-side effect; supply-side policy works through aggregate supply.", "A leftward SRAS shift means higher costs or lower capacity, the opposite of the policy's aim.", "LRAS is vertical at potential output; the policy shifts the curve itself rather than moving along it.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-405", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 2, "q": "The UK made full expensing (100% first-year allowance on qualifying plant and machinery) permanent in the Autumn Finance Bill 2023. How is it best classified?", "o": ["Market-based: it lowers the after-tax cost of investment", "Interventionist: the government directly provides the capital to firms", "Demand-side: it only affects the level of consumer spending", "Interventionist: it is a subsidy to households to raise labour supply"], "a": 0, "x": "Full expensing is a tax incentive. It lets firms deduct qualifying investment spending in year one, boosting capital and potential output. It works through incentives, not direct state provision, so it is market-based.", "w": ["", "The state does not supply the capital; firms invest their own funds, encouraged by a tax incentive.", "The policy targets business investment, not consumption, so it works on aggregate supply.", "It is aimed at firms' capital spending, not households' labour supply, and it is a tax allowance."]}
{"id": "econ-406", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose potential GDP is £2,000 billion and supply-side policies raise it by 0.5%. What is the new level of potential GDP?", "o": ["£2,010 billion", "£2,005 billion, a 0.25% rise", "£2,001 billion, a 0.05% rise", "£2,100 billion, a 5% rise"], "a": 0, "x": "0.5% of £2,000 billion is £10 billion, so potential GDP rises to £2,010 billion. Slips include misplacing the decimal point or treating 0.5% as 5%.", "w": ["", "That is 0.25%, as if 0.5% had been halved.", "That is only 0.05%, from moving the decimal point one place too far.", "That is a 5% rise, from treating 0.5% as 5%."]}
{"id": "econ-407", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is the best evaluative point about government-funded training schemes as a supply-side policy?", "o": ["They raise AD at once, so inflation falls immediately", "They cut labour productivity by raising the cost of hiring", "They are always cheaper than any market-based policies", "Benefits come after long lags and depend on skills matching need"], "a": 3, "x": "Training takes years to raise productivity, costs public money, and may not match skills that firms need. Time lags and opportunity cost are standard evaluation of interventionist policies.", "w": ["Training works mainly on supply, not AD, and extra AD would raise rather than lower inflation pressure.", "Training is meant to raise productivity; the claim reverses the intended effect.", "Training costs taxpayers money, which has an opportunity cost, so 'always cheaper' is unsupported.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-408", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 3, "q": "In Budget 2026 (28 October 2026) HM Treasury set out regulatory reforms aiming to save firms almost £800 million a year in administrative burdens. Which is the strongest limitation?", "o": ["Lower admin costs reduce AD, so deflation is certain to follow", "Weaker rules may raise external costs; economy-wide gains may be small", "Deregulation always raises tax revenue and shrinks the budget deficit", "Fewer rules mean firms stop investing, so LRAS shifts left over time"], "a": 1, "x": "Cutting regulation can lower firms' costs and ease entry, but it may weaken protection and create negative externalities, and the saving may be modest relative to the economy. Claims of certainty are a slip.", "w": ["Lower costs mainly affect supply, not AD, and 'certain' deflation is unsupported.", "", "Revenue effects are uncertain; 'always' overstates, and the policy's main aim is lower firm costs.", "Lower costs usually encourage investment and entry; a leftward LRAS shift is the wrong direction."]}
{"id": "econ-409", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 1, "q": "What does the short-run Phillips curve show?", "o": ["An inverse link between inflation and unemployment", "A positive link between inflation and economic growth rates", "An inverse link between the inflation rate and the exchange rate", "A positive link between unemployment and the budget deficit"], "a": 0, "x": "The short-run Phillips curve slopes downwards: lower unemployment is associated with higher inflation, because rising demand raises output, employment and wage and price pressures. Do not confuse it with the long run.", "w": ["", "Growth and inflation may move together, but the Phillips curve plots inflation against unemployment, not growth.", "The curve plots unemployment against inflation, not the exchange rate. This mixes up different macroeconomic variables.", "Unemployment and the deficit are linked, but that is not the Phillips curve relationship."]}
{"id": "econ-410", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 1, "q": "The government cuts income tax to reduce unemployment. Which other objective is most likely to be harmed?", "o": ["Cutting the budget deficit by raising tax revenue", "Keeping inflation above the target rate", "A balanced current account, as higher incomes raise imports", "Reducing income inequality through progressive taxation"], "a": 2, "x": "Tax cuts raise disposable income and AD. Part of the extra spending goes on imports, so the current account worsens. Inflation may also rise, but 'above target' is not an objective.", "w": ["Tax cuts reduce revenue, so the deficit is more likely to widen; this option is worded as if revenue rises.", "Keeping inflation above target is not an objective; the aim is to stay near the target.", "", "Inequality might change either way, but the clearest conflict from higher spending is with the current account."]}
{"id": "econ-411", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 2, "q": "On 4 November 2026 the Bank of England voted 8–1 to raise Bank Rate to 4% as forecast CPI inflation moved towards 5%. Which trade-off does this show?", "o": ["Lower unemployment is sought at the cost of a bigger budget deficit", "Higher growth is sought at the cost of a worse current account", "Lower inflation is sought at the cost of slower growth and higher unemployment", "Higher inflation is sought in order to improve income equality"], "a": 2, "x": "Higher Bank Rate raises borrowing costs and reduces consumption and investment, lowering AD. This helps meet the inflation target but slows growth and may raise unemployment.", "w": ["Bank Rate is not mainly about deficits or cutting unemployment; a rate rise tends to raise it.", "A rate rise reduces AD, so it does not aim to raise growth.", "", "Inflation is the problem here, and the Bank's target is stable low inflation, not higher inflation."]}
{"id": "econ-412", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Workers and firms come to expect higher inflation. What happens to the short-run Phillips curve, other things equal?", "o": ["A movement down along the curve to lower unemployment", "The curve shifts left, giving lower inflation at each unemployment rate", "The curve shifts right, giving higher inflation at each unemployment rate", "The curve is unchanged because only unemployment affects inflation"], "a": 2, "x": "Higher expected inflation raises wage claims and price-setting, so each level of unemployment now comes with higher inflation. This is a shift of the curve outwards, not a movement along it.", "w": ["A movement along the curve needs a change in AD, not a change in expectations.", "Left would mean a better trade-off; higher expectations worsen it.", "", "Expectations of inflation feed into wage bargaining, so they do shift the curve."]}
{"id": "econ-413", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 2, "q": "On a short-run Phillips curve, unemployment falls from 6% to 4% while inflation rises from 1% to 3%. How much extra inflation is given up per 1 percentage point fall in unemployment?", "o": ["0.5 percentage points", "3 percentage points", "2 percentage points", "1 percentage point"], "a": 3, "x": "Unemployment falls 2 percentage points; inflation rises 2 percentage points. Dividing, $\\frac{2}{2}=1$ percentage point of inflation per 1 point of unemployment.", "w": ["This inverts or miscalculates the ratio; the changes are both 2 points, so the ratio is 1.", "This is the new inflation rate, not the change per unit.", "The total rise in inflation is 2 points, but the question asks per 1 point of unemployment.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-414", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 3, "q": "In November 2026 Bank of England staff judged that fiscal tightening was weighing on the UK output gap. Which statement best explains the conflict involved?", "o": ["Cutting the deficit raises AD, so inflation rises above target", "Deficit reduction always raises growth by crowding in investment at once", "A smaller deficit shifts the long-run Phillips curve to the right", "Lower spending or higher taxes cut AD, so output and jobs fall short run"], "a": 3, "x": "Fiscal tightening cuts government spending or raises taxes, lowering AD. Output falls and cyclical unemployment may rise, so the deficit objective conflicts with short-run growth and employment.", "w": ["Tightening reduces AD, so it lowers rather than raises inflationary pressure.", "Crowding in is possible over time, but 'always' and 'immediately' are unrealistic; the short-run effect is a fall in AD.", "Deficit changes do not shift a Phillips curve this way; this option is wrong.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-415", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is the best description of globalisation?", "o": ["A rise in a country's exports relative to its imports over time", "Closer integration of economies through trade and capital flows", "Governments raising tariffs to protect domestic firms and jobs", "Growth in the size of a single country's national GDP each year"], "a": 1, "x": "Globalisation is increasing economic integration and interdependence between countries through flows of goods, services, capital, labour, technology and information. It is not just exports, and protectionism works against it.", "w": ["Describes a trade surplus, not integration; confuses a trade balance with globalisation.", "", "Tariffs are protectionism, which reverses globalisation; this is the opposite idea.", "Growth is national; globalisation concerns links between countries."]}
{"id": "econ-416", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is most clearly a driver of globalisation?", "o": ["Falling costs of communication through the internet", "Increased use of import quotas by major economies", "Rising transport costs for container shipping firms", "Stricter limits on capital flows between countries"], "a": 0, "x": "Cheaper communication lets firms coordinate production across borders and reach global markets. Rising transport costs, quotas and capital controls all hinder integration, so they slow globalisation rather than drive it.", "w": ["", "Quotas are protectionist and restrict trade, so they work against globalisation.", "Higher shipping costs would discourage trade; the drivers are falling transport costs.", "Capital controls restrict flows, reducing integration; liberalised capital flows drive globalisation."]}
{"id": "econ-417", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A UK car firm moves assembly to a lower-wage country and imports the cars. Which effect is most likely for UK car workers in the short run?", "o": ["Higher real wages because import prices fall", "Structural unemployment as jobs in the industry disappear", "No effect because workers move instantly to new jobs", "Cyclical unemployment only, ending when growth returns"], "a": 1, "x": "Loss of an industry to overseas competition causes structural unemployment: workers' skills and locations do not match new vacancies. Occupational and geographical immobility slow adjustment. It is not cyclical, as demand is not the cause.", "w": ["Lower import prices help consumers but do not raise these workers' wages; they lose jobs.", "", "Labour is immobile, so adjustment is slow; assuming perfect mobility is the slip.", "Job loss from industry decline is structural, not caused by the business cycle."]}
{"id": "econ-418", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "At end-2024, ONS recorded UK outward FDI stock of £1,856.1 billion and inward FDI stock of £2,127.6 billion. How much larger was the inward stock?", "o": ["£271.5 billion", "£281.5 billion", "£3,983.7 billion", "£261.5 billion"], "a": 0, "x": "Subtract: 2,127.6 − 1,856.1 = 271.5. Adding the two gives total FDI stock, which is not the difference asked for. Check carefully when subtracting decimals.", "w": ["", "Arithmetic slip: 2,127.6 − 1,856.1 is 271.5, not 281.5.", "This adds the two stocks instead of finding the difference.", "Arithmetic slip when borrowing; the correct difference is 271.5."]}
{"id": "econ-419", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is a likely benefit of globalisation for consumers in the UK?", "o": ["Higher prices because of more imports into the UK", "Fewer products available because firms leave the UK", "Guaranteed rise in domestic employment in all sectors", "Lower prices and wider choice from competition"], "a": 3, "x": "Specialisation, economies of scale and greater competition lower prices and widen choice. Jobs are not guaranteed, since some industries decline. Importing more does not by itself raise prices.", "w": ["Import competition tends to lower prices; this reverses the effect.", "Globalisation usually widens choice, not narrows it.", "Some sectors lose jobs, so employment gains are not guaranteed.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-420", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "lv": 3, "q": "In Q2 2026 the ONS reported Middle East conflict disrupting fuel supply through the Strait of Hormuz. Which concept does this best illustrate?", "o": ["Gains from comparative advantage in trade", "Risks of interdependence in supply chains", "Benefits of tariff cuts and liberalisation", "A fall in the value of inward FDI flows"], "a": 1, "x": "Reliance on international supply chains exposes economies to shocks abroad, a cost of interdependence. It does not show comparative advantage gains or tariff cuts, and the fact gives no evidence about FDI values.", "w": ["Comparative advantage concerns efficiency gains; the disruption is a risk, not a gain.", "", "No tariff change is involved; the issue is supply disruption.", "Nothing stated concerns FDI; this adds an unsupported claim."]}
{"id": "econ-421", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In Country X a worker can produce either 40 units of wheat or 20 units of cloth per week. What is the opportunity cost of one unit of cloth?", "o": ["0.5 units of wheat", "40 units of wheat", "20 units of wheat", "2 units of wheat"], "a": 3, "x": "Producing 20 cloth gives up 40 wheat, so 1 cloth costs 40 ÷ 20 = 2 wheat. The trap is inverting the ratio and giving 0.5, which is the opportunity cost of wheat in cloth.", "w": ["This inverts the ratio: 0.5 cloth is the opportunity cost of one wheat, not 0.5 wheat for a cloth.", "This is the total wheat given up for all 20 cloth, not the cost of one unit.", "This uses the cloth output (20) as the cost and ignores dividing the wheat given up by the cloth gained.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-422", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement correctly defines absolute advantage?", "o": ["Producing a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country", "Using fewer resources per unit than another country", "Exporting more of a good than the country imports", "Selling a good at a lower price because of a weaker currency"], "a": 1, "x": "Absolute advantage means higher output from the same resources, or fewer resources per unit. Lower opportunity cost defines comparative advantage, and the two are often confused.", "w": ["This defines comparative advantage, not absolute advantage; it is the usual mix-up.", "", "This describes a trade surplus in that good, not a productivity measure.", "Price changes from the exchange rate are not about resource use or productivity."]}
{"id": "econ-423", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "With the same resources, Country A can make 120 laptops or 60 phones. Country B can make 40 laptops or 40 phones. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["A has a comparative advantage in phones because it can make more phones", "B has a comparative advantage in laptops because it has a lower laptop output", "Neither country gains from trade because A has an absolute advantage in both", "B has a comparative advantage in phones: it gives up 1 laptop per phone, A gives up 2"], "a": 3, "x": "A's opportunity cost of a phone is 120 ÷ 60 = 2 laptops. B's is 40 ÷ 40 = 1 laptop. B's is lower, so B has the comparative advantage in phones. A has absolute advantage in both, which does not stop gains.", "w": ["This confuses absolute with comparative advantage; A's phone opportunity cost is higher at 2 laptops.", "B's laptop opportunity cost is 1 phone, A's is 0.5 phone, so A has the comparative advantage in laptops.", "Gains depend on differing opportunity costs, not on absolute advantage; A's and B's costs differ, so both can gain.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-424", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A's opportunity cost of one phone is 2 laptops. B's is 1 laptop. B specialises in phones and A in laptops. At which price per phone, in laptops, do both gain?", "o": ["0.5 laptops", "1 laptop", "2 laptops", "1.5 laptops"], "a": 3, "x": "Both gain if the terms of trade lie strictly between the two opportunity costs: more than 1 and less than 2 laptops per phone. 1.5 is inside that range. At either end one country gains nothing.", "w": ["This is below B's own cost of 1 laptop, so B would lose by selling phones.", "At exactly 1 laptop B gains nothing compared with making laptops itself.", "At exactly 2 laptops A gains nothing, as it could make a phone at that cost itself.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-425", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is an assumption of the basic theory of comparative advantage?", "o": ["Opportunity costs are constant as output changes", "Countries have identical factor endowments and technology", "Transport costs are high but fixed for all goods", "Countries impose tariffs on all imports of goods"], "a": 0, "x": "The basic model assumes constant opportunity costs (a straight-line PPF), no transport costs, no trade barriers, and mobile factors. Identical endowments would remove the differences that create comparative advantage.", "w": ["", "Identical endowments would mean identical opportunity costs and no gains; differences are what create comparative advantage.", "The model assumes zero transport costs; high costs would reduce the gains.", "The model assumes free trade with no tariffs or other barriers."]}
{"id": "econ-426", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 3, "q": "In Q3 2026 (July to September) the ONS recorded a UK goods deficit of £61.5 billion (excluding precious metals) and a services surplus of £51.1 billion. What does this best show?", "o": ["Comparative advantage theory is disproved, because the UK runs a goods deficit", "The UK lacks an absolute advantage in goods, so it should stop trading them", "The UK has an absolute advantage in services because its surplus is positive", "The UK may have a comparative advantage in services; a goods deficit does not disprove it"], "a": 3, "x": "Comparative advantage concerns opportunity cost, not the balance of trade. A services surplus is consistent with specialising in services, while a goods deficit just means importing goods. A surplus does not prove absolute advantage.", "w": ["The theory predicts specialisation and trade, not balanced trade in every good, so a deficit does not disprove it.", "Even without an absolute advantage in goods, gains from trade can exist; the UK need not stop trading.", "A surplus reflects the trade balance, not output per unit of resource, so it does not prove absolute advantage.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-427", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which description fits a customs union?", "o": ["Members trade freely and apply a common external tariff to non-members", "Members trade freely but each sets its own tariffs on non-members", "Members share one currency and one central bank for all trade", "Members remove tariffs and so need no rules on trade with non-members"], "a": 0, "x": "A customs union combines free trade between members with a common external tariff. A free trade area lacks the common tariff, so students often confuse the two.", "w": ["", "This describes a free trade area, not a customs union, because there is no common external tariff.", "A shared currency is a monetary union, a different and deeper arrangement than a customs union.", "Customs unions do set a common tariff on non-members; free internal trade does not remove external barriers."]}
{"id": "econ-428", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In a day, country X can make 100 cars or 200 tonnes of steel; country Y can make 60 cars or 180 tonnes of steel. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["X has a comparative advantage in both goods because it makes more of each", "X has a comparative advantage in cars and Y in steel", "Y has a comparative advantage in neither, so trade cannot benefit both", "X has a comparative advantage in steel and Y in cars"], "a": 1, "x": "Opportunity cost of a car: X gives up 2 tonnes of steel, Y gives up 3. For steel, X gives up 0.5 car, Y gives up 1/3. Lowest cost wins, so X cars, Y steel.", "w": ["X has an absolute advantage in both, but both cannot have a comparative advantage; this confuses the two ideas.", "", "Opportunity costs differ (2 against 3 steel per car), so mutually beneficial specialisation exists.", "This reverses the opportunity costs: X's car costs 2 steel, lower than Y's 3."]}
{"id": "econ-429", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "At first £1 = €1.20. The pound then depreciates to £1 = €1.10. A UK product is priced at £50. What is its new euro price and the likely effect on euro-area demand?", "o": ["€55, so demand is likely to rise", "€60, so demand is likely to be unchanged", "€45.45, so demand is likely to rise", "€55, so demand is likely to fall"], "a": 0, "x": "£50 × 1.10 = €55, down from €60. A cheaper price abroad raises the quantity demanded of UK exports. Dividing by the rate instead of multiplying is a common slip.", "w": ["", "€60 was the price before depreciation; the new price is £50 × 1.10 = €55.", "This divides £50 by 1.10, which converts the wrong way; you multiply pounds by the rate.", "The price does fall to €55, but a lower price raises, not lowers, quantity demanded."]}
{"id": "econ-430", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "The ONS reported July 2026 recorded the highest monthly level of UK car imports from China. Which best explains emerging economies gaining share of world trade in manufactures?", "o": ["They have lost comparative advantage in labour-intensive goods", "Industrialisation and FDI raised productivity and comparative advantage", "Trading blocs have banned all manufactured imports from them", "Their currencies must always appreciate, making exports cheaper abroad"], "a": 1, "x": "Emerging economies industrialise, attract foreign direct investment and raise productivity, so their comparative advantage shifts towards manufactures. Appreciation would make exports dearer, not cheaper.", "w": ["Low-cost labour has typically given them an advantage in manufactures, not removed it.", "", "Trading blocs may raise barriers to non-members but do not ban imports; this does not explain growing share.", "An appreciation raises export prices abroad, so it would reduce, not increase, price competitiveness."]}
{"id": "econ-431", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Before a customs union, country A imports a good from non-member Z at £8 plus a 25% tariff. Member B can supply at £9. After the union A buys from B. This is:", "o": ["Trade creation, as A is now buying more imports in total", "Trade diversion, as A switches from cheaper Z to dearer member B", "An improvement in A's terms of trade, as import prices fall", "Free trade with all countries, as A now imposes no tariffs at all"], "a": 1, "x": "Z is the lower-cost producer (£8 against £9) but the common external tariff makes it dearer. Buying shifts to B: trade diversion, a loss of efficiency.", "w": ["Trade creation replaces high-cost domestic output with member imports; here A switches between foreign suppliers.", "", "Nothing here changes export prices relative to import prices, so terms of trade are not the point.", "A still taxes imports from Z; removing tariffs only on members is not free trade with everyone."]}
{"id": "econ-432", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 3, "q": "In the year to June 2026, non-EU markets took 59.0% (£553.3 billion) and the EU 41.0% (£385.0 billion) of UK exports; the EU deficit was £98.2 billion, non-EU surplus £48.3 billion. Which is supported?", "o": ["The EU took most UK exports, so the UK still trades mainly within the EU", "The UK ran a £98.2 billion trade surplus with the EU", "The UK ran a £48.3 billion trade deficit with non-EU markets", "Most exports went outside the EU, yet there was a deficit with the EU"], "a": 3, "x": "Non-EU markets took 59.0%, a majority. The EU balance was a deficit (£98.2 billion) and the non-EU balance a surplus (£48.3 billion). Reading a deficit as a surplus is the slip.", "w": ["The EU took 41.0%, under half, so this misreads the shares.", "A £98.2 billion figure was a deficit with the EU, not a surplus.", "£48.3 billion was a surplus with non-EU markets, not a deficit; the sign is reversed.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-433", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "In one year a country's export price index is 110 and its import price index is 125. What is its terms of trade index?", "o": ["113.6", "88", "135", "-15"], "a": 1, "x": "Terms of trade = export price index ÷ import price index × 100 = 110 ÷ 125 × 100 = 88. Inverting the fraction gives 113.6, and subtracting gives a difference, not an index.", "w": ["Inverts the formula (imports ÷ exports); the export index must be the numerator.", "", "Adds the two indices; the terms of trade is a ratio, not a sum.", "Subtracts the indices; the terms of trade is a ratio of price indices, not a difference."]}
{"id": "econ-434", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which change, other things equal, would improve a country's terms of trade?", "o": ["Export prices fall by 4% while import prices stay unchanged", "Import prices rise by 8% while export prices rise by 5%", "Import prices fall by 3% while export prices are unchanged", "Export prices and import prices both rise by 6%"], "a": 2, "x": "The terms of trade improves when export prices rise relative to import prices. Falling import prices with unchanged export prices raises the ratio, so each unit of exports buys more imports.", "w": ["Lower export prices reduce the numerator, so the terms of trade worsens.", "Import prices rose faster than export prices, so the ratio falls: a deterioration.", "", "Equal percentage rises leave the ratio unchanged, so there is no improvement."]}
{"id": "econ-435", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "In the year to October 2026 the UK Import Price Index rose 11.1% and the Export Price Index rose 8.3% (ONS, provisional). What does this suggest about the UK terms of trade?", "o": ["They improved, because both price indices rose overall", "They were unchanged, because both indices rose overall", "They worsened, as import prices outpaced export prices", "They improved, because 11.1% is larger than 8.3%"], "a": 2, "x": "The terms of trade is export prices relative to import prices. Import prices rose faster, so the ratio fell. Each unit of exports buys fewer imports. Both indices rising does not matter; the relative change does.", "w": ["Looks only at the direction of both indices; the relative size of the rises decides the outcome.", "Wrongly treats any two rises as cancelling; unequal rises change the ratio.", "", "Confuses the larger number with a better outcome; a bigger import rise lowers the ratio."]}
{"id": "econ-436", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Starting from a terms of trade index of 100, export prices rise by 6% and import prices rise by 2%. What is the new terms of trade index, to one decimal place?", "o": ["103.9", "106.0", "108.0", "96.2"], "a": 0, "x": "New export index 106, new import index 102. Terms of trade = 106 ÷ 102 × 100 = 103.9. Subtracting or adding the percentages gives the wrong answer, and 96.2 comes from inverting the ratio.", "w": ["", "Uses the export rise only and ignores the import price change.", "Adds the percentages (6 + 2) instead of dividing the indices.", "Inverts the ratio (102 ÷ 106); exports must be on top."]}
{"id": "econ-437", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Sterling appreciates. UK exports are priced in pounds and UK imports are priced in foreign currency. What happens to the UK terms of trade, other things equal?", "o": ["They improve, as import prices in pounds fall and export prices stay fixed", "They worsen, because import prices in pounds rise as sterling strengthens", "They worsen, because UK exports become dearer abroad in foreign currency", "They are unchanged, because both prices change by the same amount"], "a": 0, "x": "A stronger pound means each unit of foreign currency costs fewer pounds, so import prices in £ fall. Export prices in £ are unchanged, so the ratio rises. Lower export competitiveness is a separate volume effect.", "w": ["", "Reverses the effect; appreciation makes foreign goods cheaper in pounds, not dearer.", "Confuses price competitiveness abroad with the terms of trade, which compares pound prices of exports and imports.", "Only import prices in pounds change here, so the ratio does change."]}
{"id": "econ-438", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "lv": 3, "q": "UK export prices rise 5%, improving the terms of trade. Suppose export demand has PED of -2, so quantity demanded falls 10%. By how much does export revenue change?", "o": ["It falls by 10%", "It rises by 5%", "It falls by 5%", "It falls by 5.5%"], "a": 3, "x": "Revenue = price × quantity = 1.05 × 0.90 = 0.945 of the original, a fall of 5.5%. A better terms of trade does not guarantee higher export revenue when demand is elastic.", "w": ["Counts only the fall in quantity and ignores the 5% higher price.", "Assumes a price rise always raises revenue; with elastic demand the quantity fall outweighs it.", "Adds the percentages (5 - 10); the changes must be multiplied: 1.05 × 0.90.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-439", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 1, "q": "Which feature distinguishes a customs union from a free trade area?", "o": ["Members share a single currency", "Members apply a common external tariff to non-members", "Members may move labour freely between countries", "Members remove tariffs on trade between themselves"], "a": 1, "x": "A customs union has free internal trade plus a common external tariff. A free trade area lets each member keep its own tariffs on non-members. Removing internal tariffs is common to both.", "w": ["A single currency is monetary union, a deeper stage, not what separates a customs union from a free trade area.", "", "Free movement of labour is a feature of a common market, not a customs union.", "Both a free trade area and a customs union remove internal tariffs, so this does not distinguish them."]}
{"id": "econ-440", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 1, "q": "Which best defines trade diversion?", "o": ["Buying switches from a higher-cost domestic firm to a lower-cost member", "Buying switches from a cheaper non-member to a dearer member", "Total trade between the bloc and the rest of the world rises", "A member leaves the bloc and trades under WTO rules instead"], "a": 1, "x": "Trade diversion occurs when the external tariff makes a more efficient non-member's goods dearer than a less efficient member's. Resources are used less efficiently. Do not confuse it with trade creation.", "w": ["This is trade creation, where a member's efficient producer replaces a dearer domestic one.", "", "This does not define diversion; diversion shifts the source of trade, and may reduce trade with non-members.", "This describes leaving a bloc, not trade diversion."]}
{"id": "econ-441", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "A country has a 50% tariff on all imports. Toys cost £8 from non-member C and £10 from member B (pre-tariff). It joins a free trade area with B. What is the outcome?", "o": ["Trade diversion; real resource cost per toy rises by £2", "Trade creation, as consumers now buy cheaper toys", "Trade diversion; real resource cost per toy rises by £4", "Trade diversion; the consumer price of toys rises by £2"], "a": 0, "x": "Before, C's toys cost £12 with tariff, B's £15. After, B's cost £10 tariff-free, below C's £12, so buying switches to B. Resource cost rises from £8 to £10: £2. Tariff-inclusive prices are not real costs.", "w": ["", "Consumers pay less, but the switch is from a lower-cost non-member, so it is diversion, not creation.", "£4 comes from using the tariff-inclusive £12 as the resource cost; the tariff is a transfer, not a resource cost.", "Consumer price falls from £12 to £10, so it does not rise; the £2 rise is in resource cost."]}
{"id": "econ-442", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "Which WTO principle do preferential trading blocs most directly challenge?", "o": ["Settling trade disputes through a formal panel process", "Monitoring members' trade policies through regular reviews", "Negotiating multilateral trade rounds between all members", "Non-discrimination between trading partners"], "a": 3, "x": "The WTO's most-favoured-nation principle says members should not discriminate between trading partners. Blocs give members preferential access over non-members, which conflicts with this, though WTO rules allow blocs under conditions.", "w": ["Dispute settlement is a WTO function and does not conflict with blocs; blocs may even use it.", "Monitoring is a WTO role, not a principle that blocs undermine.", "Multilateral rounds are a WTO activity; blocs may weaken their appeal, but the clearest conflict is discrimination.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-443", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "The CPTPP entered into force between the UK and all 11 other member nations on 1 September 2026, after the UK joined on 15 December 2024. Treated as a free trade area, what follows for the UK?", "o": ["The UK must allow free movement of labour with all members of the deal", "The UK must adopt a common external tariff with all members of the deal", "UK exporters face lower tariffs in members, but the UK sets its own external tariffs", "The UK must replace sterling with a common currency used by all members"], "a": 2, "x": "A free trade area removes tariffs between members, but each keeps its own external tariffs. A common external tariff means a customs union; free labour movement a common market; a common currency monetary union.", "w": ["Free movement of labour is a feature of a common market, a deeper stage than a free trade area.", "A common external tariff defines a customs union, not a free trade area.", "", "A common currency is monetary union, not part of a free trade area."]}
{"id": "econ-444", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 3, "q": "A customs union cuts its common external tariff from 20% to 5%. Other things equal, what is the likely effect on trade diversion?", "o": ["More likely, because members now trade more with each other", "Less likely, because non-member goods lose less price advantage", "No change, because the tariff is common to all members", "More likely, because non-members now face lower prices"], "a": 1, "x": "Diversion happens when the external tariff makes efficient non-member goods dearer than member goods. A lower tariff narrows that gap, so fewer purchases switch to higher-cost members.", "w": ["Internal trade does not depend on the external tariff in this way; a lower tariff reduces the bias towards members.", "", "A common tariff does not mean its level is irrelevant; the size of the tariff determines how much non-member goods are penalised.", "Lower tariffs make non-member goods relatively cheaper, which reduces diversion, not increases it."]}
{"id": "econ-445", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is a tariff?", "o": ["A tax placed on imported goods", "A limit on the quantity of a good that can be imported", "A payment to domestic firms to lower their costs", "A rule that imports must meet UK safety standards"], "a": 0, "x": "A tariff is a tax on imports. A limit on quantity is a quota. A payment to firms is a subsidy. Safety rules are a non-tariff (regulatory) barrier. Students often mix up tariffs and quotas.", "w": ["", "This describes a quota, which limits quantity rather than taxing imports.", "This describes a subsidy to domestic producers, not a tax on imports.", "This is a regulatory non-tariff barrier, not a tax."]}
{"id": "econ-446", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "World price is £10. A £2 per unit tariff makes the domestic price £12. At £10, demand is 80 and supply 20; at £12, demand is 60 and supply 40. What is the tariff revenue?", "o": ["£120", "£160", "£80", "£40"], "a": 3, "x": "Revenue = tariff × imports after the tariff. Imports at £12 = 60 − 40 = 20 units, so 2 × 20 = £40. The trap is using the pre-tariff imports of 60 units.", "w": ["£120 uses pre-tariff imports (80 − 20 = 60); the tariff only raises revenue on imports that remain.", "£160 multiplies the tariff by the pre-tariff quantity demanded (80), ignoring domestic supply.", "£80 multiplies the tariff by domestic supply (40), which is not imported.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-447", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "A quota cuts imports and raises the domestic price. If the import licences are given free to foreign firms, who receives the extra revenue per unit that a tariff would have given government?", "o": ["The UK government, as it would with a tariff", "The foreign firms, who sell at the higher price", "UK consumers, through lower prices in shops", "UK producers, through the licence fees paid"], "a": 1, "x": "With free licences, the gap between the higher domestic price and the world price is kept by those selling the limited imports. Government gets nothing, unlike a tariff. The trap is assuming quotas raise revenue.", "w": ["Government only collects revenue if licences are sold or a tariff is charged; free licences give it nothing.", "", "A quota raises the domestic price, so consumers pay more, not less.", "Free licences generate no fees, and domestic producers do not receive the price gap on imports."]}
{"id": "econ-448", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "On 18 November 2026 the UK applied anti-dumping tariffs of 7.04% to 23.07% on South Korean hot-rolled steel plate up to 2,100mm wide. What is the main economic aim of such tariffs?", "o": ["To offset steel sold below fair value and protect UK producers", "To raise government revenue as the main aim of the policy", "To raise the price paid by UK consumers for steel plate", "To make South Korean firms more efficient over time"], "a": 0, "x": "Anti-dumping duties aim to counter exports sold at unfairly low prices, so domestic firms are not undercut. Higher prices and revenue are side effects, not the aim. The trap is calling it simple revenue-raising.", "w": ["", "Revenue may arise, but the stated purpose is correcting unfair pricing.", "Higher prices are a cost of the policy, not its aim.", "The tariff protects UK firms; it does not aim to improve foreign efficiency."]}
{"id": "econ-449", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 1, "q": "A government subsidises domestic producers who compete with imports. Which is the most likely effect?", "o": ["The domestic price rises and the government gains revenue", "Domestic supply shifts right, imports fall, taxpayers pay", "Imports rise because domestic firms' costs have fallen", "Consumers pay the world price plus the value of the subsidy"], "a": 1, "x": "A subsidy lowers costs, shifting domestic supply right. Domestic output rises, imports fall, and the subsidy is paid from taxation. Unlike a tariff, there is no price rise for consumers and no revenue.", "w": ["That describes a tariff; a subsidy brings no revenue and does not raise price.", "", "Lower costs raise domestic output, so imports fall, not rise.", "Consumers still face roughly the world price; the subsidy is not added to it."]}
{"id": "econ-450", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 3, "q": "World price £10; a £3 tariff makes the domestic price £13. Demand falls from 100 to 70, and domestic supply rises from 30 to 50. Assuming straight-line curves, what is the deadweight loss?", "o": ["£75", "£45", "£30", "£150"], "a": 0, "x": "Two triangles: ½ × 3 × 20 = £30 (extra costly domestic output) and ½ × 3 × 30 = £45 (lost consumption). Total £75. The trap is using rectangles or only one triangle.", "w": ["", "£45 counts only the consumption triangle and omits the supply-side triangle.", "£30 counts only the production triangle and omits the lost consumption triangle.", "£150 uses full rectangles (3 × 20 + 3 × 30), forgetting the ½ for triangles."]}
{"id": "econ-451", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 1, "q": "Which transaction is recorded under primary income in the UK current account?", "o": ["A UK firm builds a new factory in Spain", "A UK resident receives dividends on shares held in a US company", "The UK government pays a contribution to an international organisation", "A UK firm sells machinery to a German customer"], "a": 1, "x": "Primary income covers income from employment and investment abroad, such as profits, interest and dividends. Factory building is foreign direct investment (financial account); transfers are secondary income; machinery sales are trade in goods.", "w": ["Building a factory abroad is foreign direct investment, which belongs in the financial account, not current account income.", "", "Payments to international organisations are secondary income (current transfers), not primary income.", "Selling machinery is trade in goods, not primary income. This confuses income flows with trade flows."]}
{"id": "econ-452", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 1, "q": "Which policy to reduce a current account deficit is an example of expenditure switching?", "o": ["Raising the basic rate of income tax", "Imposing a tariff on imported goods", "Raising the interest rate", "Cutting government spending on public services"], "a": 1, "x": "Expenditure switching shifts spending from imports to domestic goods, for example through tariffs or a lower exchange rate. Higher taxes, lower spending and higher interest rates are expenditure-reducing: they cut total demand, including for imports.", "w": ["Higher income tax lowers disposable income and total spending. That is expenditure reducing, not switching.", "", "Higher interest rates reduce borrowing and total spending. This reduces expenditure rather than switching it to domestic goods.", "Cutting government spending reduces aggregate demand overall, which is expenditure reducing."]}
{"id": "econ-453", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a country has: trade in goods −£30bn, trade in services +£22bn, primary income −£6bn, secondary income −£4bn, capital account +£1bn, errors zero. What is the financial account?", "o": ["Net inflow of £19bn", "Net inflow of £18bn", "Net inflow of £17bn", "Net outflow of £17bn"], "a": 2, "x": "Current account = −30 + 22 − 6 − 4 = −£18bn. Current + capital + financial = 0, so financial = −(−18 + 1) = +£17bn net inflow. The trap is forgetting the capital account.", "w": ["This subtracts the capital account instead of adding it, a sign error.", "This ignores the +£1bn capital account, which reduces the amount the financial account must offset.", "", "The deficit must be financed by a net inflow, not an outflow. The sign is the wrong way round."]}
{"id": "econ-454", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 2, "q": "In Q2 2026 the ONS reported a UK current account deficit of 2.5% of GDP: 1.6% on trade, 0.5% on primary income, 0.4% on secondary income. What share of GDP was the combined income deficit?", "o": ["0.5% of GDP, the primary income deficit", "0.9% of GDP, primary plus secondary income", "1.6% of GDP, the deficit on trade", "2.5% of GDP, the whole current account"], "a": 1, "x": "Primary income and secondary income are both income balances: 0.5% + 0.4% = 0.9% of GDP. The trade deficit of 1.6% is separate. The total of 2.5% includes all three parts.", "w": ["This is primary income only; the question asks for primary and secondary income combined.", "", "This is the trade deficit, which is goods and services, not income.", "This is the whole current account deficit, including trade, so it overstates the income balances."]}
{"id": "econ-455", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 3, "q": "A country's PED for exports is 0.3 and its PED for imports is 0.4. Its currency depreciates. According to the Marshall-Lerner condition, what happens to the current account?", "o": ["It worsens, because the sum of the PEDs is below 1", "It improves after a short delay, as in the J-curve", "It improves, because exports become cheaper abroad", "It is unchanged, because export and import changes cancel"], "a": 0, "x": "Marshall-Lerner says depreciation improves the current account only if PEDx + PEDm > 1. Here the sum is 0.7, so demand is too inelastic and the trade balance worsens. Lower export prices alone are not enough.", "w": ["", "The J-curve assumes elasticities rise over time to meet the condition. Here the sum stays below 1, so no later improvement is implied.", "Cheaper exports help only if demand is elastic enough. With inelastic demand, export revenue barely rises.", "Changes do not cancel to zero. With inelastic demand the higher import prices dominate, so the balance worsens."]}
{"id": "econ-456", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 3, "q": "Which current account deficit is least likely to cause concern for a government?", "o": ["One from households importing consumer goods, financed by short-term portfolio inflows", "One from firms importing machinery, financed by long-term foreign direct investment", "One from falling export competitiveness, financed by selling foreign currency reserves", "One from rising debt interest payments abroad, financed by borrowing from overseas"], "a": 1, "x": "A deficit is less worrying if it builds productive capacity and is financed by stable, long-term investment. Imported capital goods raise future output and exports. Consumption, reserve sales and borrowing are less sustainable.", "w": ["Consumption imports add no future capacity, and short-term inflows can reverse quickly, so this is a greater concern.", "", "Reserves are finite and falling competitiveness is a structural weakness, so this is not sustainable.", "Borrowing to pay interest on past borrowing builds debt without adding capacity, so this is a greater concern."]}
{"id": "econ-457", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 1, "q": "Which expression gives a firm's unit labour cost?", "o": ["Output ÷ total labour cost", "Wage rate × number of workers × output", "Total labour cost ÷ output", "Total revenue ÷ number of workers"], "a": 2, "x": "Unit labour cost is labour cost per unit of output: total labour cost ÷ output. Output per worker is productivity and revenue per worker is a different measure.", "w": ["This is inverted: it gives output per £ of labour cost, not cost per unit.", "Multiplying by output gives nonsense; it overstates cost per unit instead of dividing by output.", "", "This is revenue per worker, not a cost measure."]}
{"id": "econ-458", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 2, "q": "A toy factory pays £90,000 in total labour costs and makes 30,000 toys. Next year it pays £99,000 and makes 33,000 toys. What happens to unit labour cost?", "o": ["It rises to £3.30 per toy", "It stays at £3 per toy", "It falls to £2.70 per toy", "It rises by £9,000"], "a": 1, "x": "Year 1: £90,000 ÷ 30,000 = £3. Year 2: £99,000 ÷ 33,000 = £3. Labour costs and output both rose 10%, so unit labour cost is unchanged. The trap is looking only at the wage bill.", "w": ["Looks only at the 10% rise in the wage bill and ignores the 10% rise in output.", "", "Wrongly assumes more output must cut unit cost; here cost rose in proportion.", "£9,000 is the rise in total labour cost, not the change in cost per unit."]}
{"id": "econ-459", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 1, "q": "UK firms keep their prices in pounds unchanged, but the pound appreciates against the euro. What is the most likely effect on UK relative export prices for euro-area buyers?", "o": ["They fall, so UK exports become more price competitive", "Unit labour costs fall because wages fall", "They are unchanged because pound prices are unchanged", "They rise, so UK exports are less price competitive"], "a": 3, "x": "A stronger pound makes each £ price cost more in euros. Relative export prices rise and price competitiveness falls. The trap is ignoring the exchange rate when prices are fixed in pounds.", "w": ["This is the effect of a depreciation, not an appreciation.", "An exchange rate change does not alter wages or unit labour costs directly.", "Relative prices are compared in a common currency, so the exchange rate matters.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-460", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 2, "q": "In an economy, labour cost per worker rises 20% and output per worker rises 25%. Ignoring other factors, what happens to unit labour cost?", "o": ["It rises by 5%, the gap between the two", "It falls by about 4%", "It falls by 5%, the gap between the two", "It rises by 45%, the sum of the two"], "a": 1, "x": "Unit labour cost = labour cost per worker ÷ output per worker. $\\frac{1.20}{1.25}=0.96$, so it falls 4%. Subtracting the percentages gives 5%, which is a slip.", "w": ["Wrong direction: output per worker rose faster than pay, so unit costs fall.", "", "Subtracting the percentages (25 − 20) is not exact; divide the indices: 1.20 ÷ 1.25.", "Adding the two changes is wrong; the measure is a ratio, so you divide."]}
{"id": "econ-461", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 3, "q": "ONS figures released on 17 November 2026 showed UK labour compensation per hour rose 8.0% in 2025 and whole-economy unit labour costs rose 5.5%. What can be concluded?", "o": ["Output per hour rose, as unit labour costs rose more slowly than pay", "UK firms became more competitive, because unit labour costs rose", "Output per hour fell in 2025, because unit labour costs rose", "Pay per hour was unchanged in real terms, as both figures are positive"], "a": 0, "x": "Unit labour cost = pay per hour ÷ output per hour. Costs rising more slowly than pay means output per hour rose. A rise in unit labour costs does not itself improve competitiveness.", "w": ["", "A rise in unit labour costs, other things equal, worsens price competitiveness.", "Falling productivity would make unit costs rise faster than pay, not slower.", "Real pay needs inflation data, which is not given; both figures being positive proves nothing about real pay."]}
{"id": "econ-462", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 3, "q": "Provisional ONS figures showed the UK Export Price Index rose 8.3% in the year to October 2026. Why does this alone not prove UK price competitiveness worsened?", "o": ["It depends on UK export prices relative to rivals' prices in a common currency, not given", "The Export Price Index measures the prices of imports, not the prices of exports", "Higher export prices always increase the quantity of exports that overseas buyers demand", "Rising prices automatically reduce unit labour costs, so competitiveness must improve"], "a": 0, "x": "Price competitiveness is about relative export prices, so rivals' prices and the exchange rate are needed. A rise in UK prices matters less if rivals' prices rose as much.", "w": ["", "The index measures export prices; import prices have a separate index.", "Higher prices usually reduce quantity demanded, not raise it, for normal goods.", "Higher prices do not reduce unit labour costs; the two are different measures."]}
{"id": "econ-463", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In UK statistics, a household is usually classed as in relative poverty if its income is below:", "o": ["60% of mean household income", "half of the national minimum wage", "the income needed for basic necessities, fixed in real terms", "60% of median household income"], "a": 3, "x": "The UK relative poverty line is 60% of median household income. Using the mean is a slip, because the mean is pulled up by very high incomes. A fixed real line is the absolute measure.", "w": ["The mean is distorted by very high incomes; the standard measure uses the median.", "The minimum wage is not the benchmark; relative poverty is tied to median income.", "This describes absolute poverty, a fixed real threshold, not relative poverty.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-464", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose median household income in a country is £32,000. What is the relative poverty line, defined as 60% of median income?", "o": ["£19,200", "£53,333", "£16,000", "£12,800"], "a": 0, "x": "60% of £32,000 = 0.6 × 32,000 = £19,200. Taking 40% gives £12,800, taking 50% gives £16,000, and dividing by 0.6 gives £53,333, which is the wrong operation.", "w": ["", "This divides £32,000 by 0.6 instead of multiplying by 0.6.", "This is 50% of the median, not 60%.", "This is 40% of the median, not 60%."]}
{"id": "econ-465", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Every household's real income rises by exactly 10%. The absolute poverty line is fixed in real terms. What is the most likely effect?", "o": ["Absolute poverty rises; relative poverty falls", "Both absolute and relative poverty fall together", "Absolute poverty is unchanged; relative poverty falls", "Absolute poverty falls; relative poverty is unchanged"], "a": 3, "x": "Higher real incomes lift some households above the fixed absolute line. The median also rises 10%, so the 60% line rises 10% and relative poverty is unchanged. The trap is thinking growth cuts relative poverty.", "w": ["Higher real incomes cannot raise absolute poverty; the fixed real line stays put.", "Relative poverty does not fall, because the 60% of median line rises by the same 10%.", "Absolute poverty is the measure that falls when real incomes rise, so the measures are muddled.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-466", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In 2024/25, 13.4 million UK residents (20%) were in relative low income after housing costs, against 10.9 million (16%) before. Why is the after-housing-costs figure higher?", "o": ["Housing costs take a bigger share of low incomes than of the median", "The after-housing-costs measure uses a fixed absolute line", "Housing costs raise median income by more than they raise low incomes", "Housing costs are paid only by households already in poverty"], "a": 0, "x": "Deducting housing costs lowers incomes, and low-income households tend to spend a bigger share on housing. Their incomes fall further relative to the median, so more fall below 60% of it.", "w": ["", "Both measures here are relative, using 60% of median income, not a fixed line.", "Deducting costs lowers median income too; it does not raise it, and the effect on low incomes is bigger.", "Many households pay housing costs; the point is that the share is higher for low-income households."]}
{"id": "econ-467", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In a recession, median income falls from £30,000 to £28,000. A household's income stays at £17,500. What happens to that household's relative poverty status?", "o": ["It stays in relative poverty because its income is unchanged", "It moves into absolute poverty because the median has fallen", "It moves above the relative poverty line, though its income is unchanged", "It stays in relative poverty because the line is £18,000 either way"], "a": 2, "x": "The line falls from 60% × £30,000 = £18,000 to 60% × £28,000 = £16,800. Income of £17,500 was below the old line but is above the new one, so measured relative poverty falls.", "w": ["The line moves with the median, so unchanged income can cross it.", "The absolute line is fixed and does not depend on the median.", "", "The line falls to £16,800 when the median is £28,000; it does not stay at £18,000."]}
{"id": "econ-468", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "lv": 3, "q": "Over a year the real incomes of the poorest households rise by 1% while median real income rises by 3%. The absolute line is fixed in real terms. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Absolute poverty must rise, because the poorest gained less than the median", "Absolute poverty can fall while relative poverty rises", "Both measures must fall, because all real incomes rose", "Relative poverty cannot change because all incomes rose"], "a": 1, "x": "The fixed line stays put, so a 1% gain can lift some households above it. The relative line rises 3%, so households near it may fall behind the line. Gains differ, so the measures can diverge.", "w": ["The absolute line is fixed in real terms, so any real rise cannot increase absolute poverty.", "", "The relative line rises 3% with the median, so poor households gaining 1% may fall below it.", "Relative poverty depends on the gap to the median, not on whether incomes rose."]}
{"id": "econ-469", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which set lists the three dimensions combined in the Human Development Index?", "o": ["Life expectancy, literacy rate and real GDP growth", "Infant mortality, unemployment and GDP per capita", "Life expectancy, years of schooling and the Gini coefficient", "Life expectancy, years of schooling and GNI per capita (PPP)"], "a": 3, "x": "HDI covers health (life expectancy), education (mean and expected years of schooling) and living standards (GNI per capita, PPP). The trap is to add growth, unemployment or inequality, which are not in it.", "w": ["Literacy rate and GDP growth are not HDI components; growth is a change, not a living-standards level.", "Infant mortality and unemployment are not used; HDI uses life expectancy and schooling plus income.", "The Gini coefficient measures inequality, which HDI does not include. This is the commonest misconception.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-470", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement about the Human Development Index is correct?", "o": ["It takes a value between 0 and 100, and a higher value means a larger informal economy", "It is measured in £ per person, and a higher value means greater inequality", "It takes a value between 0 and 1, and a higher value means greater human development", "It measures the annual growth rate of real GNI per capita in each country"], "a": 2, "x": "HDI is an index from 0 to 1; a higher value indicates higher human development. It is a level of development, not a growth rate and not a money amount.", "w": ["HDI is scaled 0 to 1, and it says nothing about the informal economy.", "HDI is an index, not a sum of money, and it does not measure inequality.", "", "HDI compares levels, not growth rates. Confusing the two is a common slip."]}
{"id": "econ-471", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Two countries both have an HDI of 0.80. Country A has a Gini coefficient of 0.25 and Country B has 0.55 (made-up figures). What is the best conclusion?", "o": ["HDI does not capture income distribution, so typical households may differ", "Country B must have the higher GNI per capita, since inequality is greater", "Country A must have the longer life expectancy, since it is more equal", "Both countries have identical living standards for every household in each"], "a": 0, "x": "HDI uses national averages, so it ignores how income is shared. Equal HDI values can hide very different experiences for typical households. The trap is reading HDI as a measure of equality.", "w": ["", "Higher inequality does not imply higher GNI per capita; the figures give no such link.", "Equality does not determine life expectancy, and HDI alone does not show it.", "Equal HDI values are averages; they do not mean every household is the same."]}
{"id": "econ-472", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In the UNDP's Human Development Report 2025 (2023 data), the UK and Singapore were joint 13th, HDI 0.946. Singapore's GNI per capita was far higher. How can this be?", "o": ["HDI uses GDP per head, which is similar in both countries", "HDI leaves out income, so GNI per capita has no effect on it", "HDI is a composite, and extra income adds less and less to it", "HDI converts income into £, which lowers Singapore's figure"], "a": 2, "x": "HDI combines health, education and income, so a lead in one can be offset by the others. Income enters with diminishing returns, so Singapore's extra income adds little. HDI does not ignore income.", "w": ["HDI uses GNI per capita at PPP, not GDP, and the question says Singapore's GNI per capita is far higher.", "Income (GNI per capita) is one of the three dimensions of HDI; it is not left out.", "", "HDI measures income in PPP dollars, not £, and currency conversion does not explain the tie."]}
{"id": "econ-473", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The Global MPI 2026 (OPHI and UNDP, 12 October 2026) found 1.2 billion people (18.9%) in acute multidimensional poverty. What does the MPI add to an income-based measure?", "o": ["It counts only people living below a single daily income line", "It shows people deprived in health, schooling and living standards at once", "It ranks countries by GDP per capita in PPP terms alone", "It measures inequality between rich and poor using the Lorenz curve"], "a": 1, "x": "The MPI looks at overlapping deprivations in health, education and living standards for households. It captures poverty that an income line alone can miss. The trap is to treat it as another income measure.", "w": ["That describes an income poverty line, which the MPI goes beyond.", "", "The MPI is not a GDP ranking; it counts deprivations in households.", "The Gini coefficient and Lorenz curve show inequality; the MPI counts deprivation."]}
{"id": "econ-474", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "lv": 3, "q": "A country's HDI rises from 0.55 to 0.60 over ten years. Which conclusion can be drawn from this information alone?", "o": ["Poverty fell because the poorest households gained most", "Income inequality narrowed because HDI rose", "At least one of life expectancy, schooling or income per head rose", "Environmental damage per person fell, since living standards improved"], "a": 2, "x": "A higher composite index needs at least one component to have improved. HDI says nothing about who gained, inequality or the environment, so those claims go beyond the evidence.", "w": ["HDI uses averages, so it cannot show who gained most.", "HDI excludes inequality, so a rise does not show it narrowed.", "", "HDI has no environmental measure; higher living standards may even raise damage."]}
{"id": "econ-475", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is the main reason heavy dependence on primary product exports can hold back a developing country?", "o": ["Export prices are often volatile, so export revenue is unstable", "Primary products cannot be sold to other countries at all", "Primary products always have price elastic demand on world markets", "Primary production never uses any labour, so jobs are not created"], "a": 0, "x": "Primary prices swing with world supply and demand, making revenue, investment and planning unstable. The trap is thinking primaries cannot be traded or that demand is elastic; it is typically price inelastic.", "w": ["", "Primary products are widely traded; the problem is price instability, not inability to sell.", "Demand for primaries is typically price inelastic, so this misstates the elasticity and the real problem.", "Primary production often uses much labour; the issue is low value added and volatility."]}
{"id": "econ-476", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In the Harrod-Domar model, a savings gap exists when:", "o": ["Imports are greater than exports, leaving a trade deficit", "Domestic saving is too low to fund the investment needed", "The government spends more than it collects in tax each year", "Households save more than firms invest in the economy"], "a": 1, "x": "The savings gap is the shortfall of domestic saving relative to the investment needed for a target growth rate. Confusing it with the trade or budget deficit is the common slip.", "w": ["This describes a trade deficit, which relates to the foreign currency gap, not the savings gap.", "", "This describes a budget deficit, a different concept from the savings gap.", "This is a surplus of saving, the opposite of a gap in funding investment."]}
{"id": "econ-477", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A country needs investment of 24% of GDP for its target growth, but domestic saving is 15% of GDP. What is the savings gap, and how could it be filled?", "o": ["39% of GDP; by cutting imports sharply", "9% of GDP; by FDI or foreign aid", "9% of GDP; by cutting investment further", "1.6% of GDP; by raising taxes only"], "a": 1, "x": "Gap = 24% − 15% = 9% of GDP. It can be filled by inflows such as FDI, aid or borrowing. Adding the figures or dividing them are the slips.", "w": ["Adding 24 and 15 is wrong; the gap is the difference, and cutting imports does not fill it.", "", "The gap is 9%, but cutting investment would lower growth rather than close the gap.", "Dividing 24 by 15 gives a ratio, not the gap; the gap is the difference."]}
{"id": "econ-478", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a country's export prices for its main commodity fall while import prices of manufactures rise. What happens to its terms of trade and why does this matter?", "o": ["They are unchanged, as the two price moves cancel out", "They improve, so it can import more per unit exported", "They worsen, so more exports are needed per import", "They worsen, but only the exchange rate is affected"], "a": 2, "x": "Terms of trade = export price index ÷ import price index × 100. Falling export prices and rising import prices lower it, so more exports are needed per import. Reversing the ratio is the slip.", "w": ["Prices moved in opposite directions, which lowers the ratio rather than cancelling out.", "A fall in the ratio is a worsening; the direction has been reversed.", "", "Terms of trade directly affect real income and the volume of imports, not just the exchange rate."]}
{"id": "econ-479", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a developing country spends 30% of government revenue on debt service. Which is the most likely effect on development?", "o": ["No effect, as debt service is a transfer abroad only", "More revenue for health and education, as debt is repaid", "Less revenue for health, education and infrastructure", "Higher savings, as debt service raises domestic investment"], "a": 2, "x": "Debt service has an opportunity cost: money paid to creditors cannot fund public services or infrastructure, slowing development. Calling it harmless because it is 'just repayment' ignores this.", "w": ["Payments abroad still use revenue that could have funded spending, so there is an opportunity cost.", "Repayments go to creditors, so less, not more, is available for public services.", "", "Debt service reduces funds available for investment; it does not raise domestic saving."]}
{"id": "econ-480", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 3, "q": "A country's imports of capital goods exceed its export earnings and it lacks the foreign currency to pay. Which describes the problem and a likely effect?", "o": ["A budget deficit, which makes imported capital goods cheaper", "A savings gap, which directly lowers the country's exchange rate", "A foreign currency gap, which limits investment and growth", "A foreign currency gap, which raises export prices automatically"], "a": 2, "x": "A foreign currency gap means insufficient foreign exchange for needed imports such as machinery, restricting investment and growth. It is distinct from the savings gap, which concerns domestic funds.", "w": ["A budget deficit concerns government finances, and it does not make imported capital goods cheaper.", "The savings gap concerns domestic saving, not foreign exchange needed to pay for imports.", "", "A lack of foreign exchange does not automatically raise export prices; it restricts imports."]}
{"id": "econ-481", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an example of a market-based strategy for growth and development?", "o": ["Import tariffs to protect infant industries", "Public investment in rural roads and ports", "Privatisation of state-owned enterprises", "A buffer stock scheme for a key export crop"], "a": 2, "x": "Privatisation moves resources from the state to the private sector and relies on market forces. Tariffs, public investment and buffer stocks are interventionist because the government directly shapes markets or provides the service.", "w": ["Tariffs are government intervention in trade, so they are interventionist; the slip is thinking protection is market-based.", "Government-funded infrastructure is interventionist; the slip is assuming anything that helps business is market-based.", "", "Buffer stocks need government buying and selling to stabilise prices, so this is interventionist."]}
{"id": "econ-482", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best describes the role of the International Monetary Fund (IMF)?", "o": ["It makes long-term loans for projects such as dams and schools", "It lends to countries with payments problems, often with conditions", "It runs local development projects funded by voluntary donations", "It sets the tariffs that member countries must charge on imports"], "a": 1, "x": "The IMF promotes macroeconomic and financial stability and lends short term, often with conditionality. Project lending is the World Bank's role; NGOs rely on donations. The IMF does not set members' tariffs.", "w": ["This describes the World Bank; the slip is confusing the IMF's short-term stability role with long-term project finance.", "", "This describes NGOs such as charities; the IMF is an international institution funded by member countries.", "The IMF cannot dictate tariffs; trade rules belong to bodies like the WTO, so this confuses institutions."]}
{"id": "econ-483", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In December 2024 the World Bank Group announced a record USD 100 billion IDA21 replenishment for 78 low-income countries. IDA funding is 'concessional'. This means it is:", "o": ["Tied to buying goods and services from donor countries", "Lent at commercial interest rates for rapid repayment", "Grants or loans on easier terms than the market", "Raised from private banks and repaid on demand"], "a": 2, "x": "Concessional finance is grants or loans with low or zero interest and long repayment periods. Confusing it with tied aid or commercial loans is the usual slip.", "w": ["Tying aid is a separate feature; concessional describes the generous terms, not a requirement to buy from donors.", "Commercial rates are the opposite of concessional; the slip is ignoring the favourable terms.", "", "IDA relies on donor pledges, not private banks, and its loans have long repayment periods rather than on-demand repayment."]}
{"id": "econ-484", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "An IMF loan is conditional on a country removing food subsidies and cutting public spending. Which is the most likely short-run cost?", "o": ["A larger government budget deficit as subsidy payments rise", "A larger role for the state in allocating scarce resources", "Lower real incomes for foreign investors in the country", "Higher food prices hitting poor households hardest"], "a": 3, "x": "Removing subsidies raises prices paid by consumers, hurting low-income households most in the short run. Subsidy removal reduces, not increases, government spending and the state's role.", "w": ["Removing subsidies cuts spending, so the deficit tends to fall; this reverses the direction of the policy.", "These reforms shrink the state's role; the slip is confusing conditionality with intervention.", "Nothing in the policy targets foreign investors' incomes; the main burden falls on domestic poor households.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-485", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "A microfinance scheme lends small sums to women in rural areas to start businesses. Which is the best description of its main benefit?", "o": ["It gives credit to people excluded from banks", "It reduces the government's budget deficit directly", "It replaces the need for any private sector activity", "It raises tariffs to protect new small rural firms"], "a": 0, "x": "Microfinance is a market-based strategy that widens access to credit for those without collateral, allowing small enterprises and higher incomes. It does not replace the private sector, cut deficits or involve tariffs.", "w": ["", "The scheme is not aimed at government finances; the slip is confusing development finance with fiscal policy.", "Microfinance supports private enterprise rather than replacing it; the slip is reversing its purpose.", "Tariffs are a trade policy; microfinance is about credit, so this mixes up different strategies."]}
{"id": "econ-486", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "lv": 3, "q": "A country imports a machine at a world price of £50,000 with a 20% tariff. It removes the tariff. Assuming the world price is unchanged, what is the tariff revenue lost per machine and the % fall in domestic price?", "o": ["£60,000; 16.7%", "£10,000; 20%", "£10,000; 16.7%", "£8,333; 20.0%"], "a": 2, "x": "Tariff is 20% × £50,000 = £10,000. Old price £60,000, new £50,000, so the fall is 10,000 ÷ 60,000 = 16.7%. The trap is dividing by the new price.", "w": ["£60,000 is the old domestic price, not the tariff revenue; revenue is only the £10,000 tax.", "Dividing by £50,000 gives 20%, but the % change must use the original price of £60,000.", "", "£8,333 comes from dividing £50,000 by 6; the tariff is 20% of the world price, £50,000, giving £10,000."]}
{"id": "econ-487", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an example of a forward market being used?", "o": ["A household pays £200 a month into a cash ISA at a high street bank", "A firm issues new shares on the stock market to raise money for a factory", "A tourist buys euros at today's rate at an airport bureau before a trip", "A UK exporter agrees now to sell euros in three months at today's rate"], "a": 3, "x": "A forward market fixes today the price of a currency or commodity for delivery at a future date. Buying euros today is a spot transaction, a common trap.", "w": ["This is saving, which financial markets facilitate, but it is not a contract to trade at a fixed future price.", "This is the equity market, a different role: raising finance, not fixing a future price.", "This is a spot transaction at today's rate, not a contract for future delivery. Students confuse exchange with forward trading.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-488", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "A shareholder sells 100 shares in a UK listed company to another investor through the stock exchange. Who receives the money paid for the shares?", "o": ["The company that issued the shares", "The government, as stamp duty receipts replace the price", "The company's bank, to reduce its borrowing", "The shareholder who sold the shares"], "a": 3, "x": "This is a secondary market trade, so the money goes to the seller. The company only receives funds when it issues new shares in the primary market.", "w": ["Wrong: the firm only gains funds from a new issue, not from resale of existing shares between investors.", "Wrong: tax may be paid on the trade, but the price paid goes to the seller, not the government.", "Wrong: the company is not party to the trade, so its borrowing is unaffected by the payment.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-489", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A UK firm must pay USD 50,000 for machinery in three months. It agrees a forward rate of USD 1.25 per £1. The spot rate then is USD 1.20 per £1. How much cheaper in £ is the forward deal?", "o": ["£40,000, the forward cost", "£2,500, from 50,000 × 0.05", "About £1,667", "£41,667, the spot cost"], "a": 2, "x": "Forward cost: 50,000 ÷ 1.25 = £40,000. Spot cost: 50,000 ÷ 1.20 = £41,666.67. Saving is about £1,667. Divide dollars by the rate to get pounds; do not multiply.", "w": ["Wrong: this is the forward cost in £, not the saving compared with the spot cost.", "Wrong: this multiplies USD 50,000 by 0.05, the rate difference. Dollars must be divided by the rate to give pounds.", "", "Wrong: this is the spot cost in £, not the difference between the two costs."]}
{"id": "econ-490", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In its FY 2025 results (published November 2025), easyJet reported it had hedged 77% of H1 FY26 US dollar exposure at USD 1.30 per pound. What does this mainly show?", "o": ["It guarantees easyJet pays less than it would at the later spot rate", "It has removed all currency risk on every dollar cost it faces", "It has bought shares in US firms to gain from dollar movements", "It has fixed the pound cost of that share of dollar spending"], "a": 3, "x": "Hedging with forward contracts fixes the rate, so costs become certain for the hedged share. It cannot guarantee a better rate: if the pound later strengthens, the firm misses out.", "w": ["Wrong: a forward rate may turn out better or worse than the later spot rate. Hedging gives certainty, not savings.", "Wrong: only 77% was hedged, so about 23% of this exposure stays open to exchange rate changes.", "Wrong: hedging uses forward contracts in currency markets, not equity purchases.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-491", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Bank of England data for September 2026 recorded £5.0 billion of household ISA deposits and £7.3 billion of corporate borrowing. Together, which role of financial markets do these illustrate?", "o": ["Channelling savings from households to borrowers", "Allowing firms to hedge against future price changes", "Enabling the exchange of one currency for another", "Raising capital by issuing new equity"], "a": 0, "x": "ISA deposits are saving and corporate borrowing is lending. Financial markets and institutions link savers with borrowers. Neither figure involves forward contracts, currency exchange or share issues.", "w": ["", "Wrong: hedging uses forward contracts. Deposits and loans do not fix future prices.", "Wrong: no currency is being exchanged in a savings deposit or a loan to a firm.", "Wrong: corporate borrowing is debt finance. Equity means selling ownership shares, which is different."]}
{"id": "econ-492", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "lv": 3, "q": "A growing firm needs £2 million. Compared with taking a bank loan, which is a likely feature of raising it by issuing new shares on the stock market?", "o": ["The £2 million must be repaid in full plus fixed interest", "Existing owners keep full control of the firm and all its profits", "No repayment is due, but ownership is diluted and dividends expected", "The firm's costs rise because of compulsory interest payments"], "a": 2, "x": "Equity finance has no fixed repayment or interest. But new shareholders gain a share of ownership and profits, which dilutes control. Repayment with interest describes a loan, a common mix-up.", "w": ["Wrong: this describes a loan (debt finance), not equity. Shares carry no repayment duty.", "Wrong: selling new shares gives others voting rights and a claim on profit, so control is diluted.", "", "Wrong: shares do not carry interest payments. Dividends are paid from profit and are not a fixed obligation."]}
{"id": "econ-493", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "A trader buys shares in a firm using confidential information about a takeover that the public does not yet know. Which type of market failure does this best illustrate?", "o": ["A speculative bubble", "Moral hazard", "Asymmetric information", "Negative externality of consumption"], "a": 2, "x": "Insider dealing exploits one party knowing more than others, which is asymmetric information. Moral hazard needs risk shifted to others after a protection; no such protection is described here.", "w": ["No sustained price rise driven by expectations of further rises is described, so it is not a bubble.", "Moral hazard is riskier behaviour once protected from the costs; nothing here involves protection or bearing others' losses.", "", "This is not a consumption spillover on third parties; the problem is unequal knowledge between traders."]}
{"id": "econ-494", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best defines moral hazard in the financial sector?", "o": ["Prices rise because buyers expect further rises to follow", "Firms collude to fix a benchmark interest rate between them", "A party takes greater risks because others bear the cost of failure", "A seller knows much more about a product than the buyer does"], "a": 2, "x": "Moral hazard is increased risk-taking because the costs of failure fall on others, such as taxpayers in a bailout. The other options describe bubbles, rigging and asymmetric information.", "w": ["This describes a speculative bubble, driven by expectations, not moral hazard.", "This describes market rigging, such as manipulating a benchmark rate, not risk-taking.", "", "This is asymmetric information, which concerns unequal knowledge, not behaviour after risk is shifted."]}
{"id": "econ-495", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "In 2025/26 the FCA cancelled 1,264 firms' authorisations. Which market failure is this regulatory action MOST directly intended to reduce?", "o": ["Unemployment caused by weak aggregate demand in the economy", "The lack of competition in the housing market for buyers", "Misconduct that exploits customers' lack of information", "Inflation caused by rising import prices from abroad"], "a": 2, "x": "Removing authorisation stops firms that mislead or exploit customers who cannot judge financial products. This tackles asymmetric information and misconduct. The other options are unrelated to the FCA's role.", "w": ["Unemployment from weak demand is a macro policy issue, not an FCA conduct matter.", "The FCA regulates financial firms; it does not target housing market competition.", "", "Imported inflation is dealt with through monetary policy, not by cancelling authorisations."]}
{"id": "econ-496", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a share's price rises from £4 to £10 over a year while the firm's profits are unchanged. Buyers say they expect to resell at higher prices. Which is the best explanation?", "o": ["Higher profits have raised the fundamental value of the share", "A rising supply of shares has lowered the price of the share", "Moral hazard, because shareholders face limited liability", "A speculative bubble, with price driven by expected rises"], "a": 3, "x": "Price rising far above fundamentals because buyers expect to resell dearer is a speculative bubble. Unchanged profits rule out a fundamental rise. Bubbles eventually burst, causing sharp falls.", "w": ["Profits are unchanged, so fundamental value has not risen.", "More supply would lower, not raise, the price.", "Moral hazard concerns risk-taking when costs are shifted; it does not explain expectation-driven price rises.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-497", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A bank expects the government to rescue it if its risky loans fail, so it lends more riskily. A student calls this asymmetric information. What is the correct analysis?", "o": ["Correct: the bank knows more than the government does", "Moral hazard: the bank takes more risk as taxpayers may bear losses", "Market rigging: the bank is manipulating prices or rates", "A speculative bubble: loan prices are rising on expectations"], "a": 1, "x": "Expecting a bailout shifts costs to taxpayers, so the bank takes more risk. That is moral hazard, a behavioural response, not unequal knowledge. Nothing suggests price manipulation or a bubble.", "w": ["The issue is behaviour after protection, not unequal knowledge; this confuses the two terms.", "", "Rigging means manipulating prices or benchmarks; none is described.", "No asset price boom driven by expectations is described."]}
{"id": "econ-498", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The Bank of England's July 2026 Financial Stability Report flagged stretched valuations in a narrow set of AI-related stocks. Which is the most likely economic consequence if a bubble in them burst?", "o": ["Wealth effects would raise consumption and aggregate demand", "Asymmetric information would be eliminated by the lower prices", "Moral hazard would disappear as investors learn about risk", "Lower asset prices could cut wealth and confidence and hit banks"], "a": 3, "x": "A burst bubble cuts asset values, household and firm wealth and confidence, and can create bank losses, reducing spending and credit. A negative wealth effect lowers, not raises, consumption.", "w": ["The wealth effect works in reverse: falling asset values reduce consumption and aggregate demand.", "Lower prices do not give everyone equal information; asymmetry persists.", "Falling prices do not remove moral hazard; protection from losses can still encourage risk-taking.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-499", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "lv": 1, "q": "What does the Bank of England do when it acts as lender of last resort?", "o": ["Lends money to households that cannot get a mortgage", "Provides emergency liquidity to banks short of funds", "Buys shares in failing banks to take them over for good", "Sets the tax rates that fund any bank rescues needed"], "a": 1, "x": "A lender of last resort supplies emergency liquidity to banks that cannot meet withdrawals, helping prevent a loss of confidence and a bank run. It lends to banks, not households, and does not set taxes.", "w": ["Lender of last resort lends to banks, not to households; confusing it with ordinary commercial lending.", "", "The role is temporary lending of liquidity, not permanent ownership of banks.", "Taxes are set by the government, not the central bank; this confuses fiscal and monetary roles."]}
{"id": "econ-500", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "lv": 1, "q": "On 4 November 2026 the MPC voted 8–1 to raise Bank Rate to 4%, to meet the 2% CPI target. Which role of the Bank of England is this?", "o": ["Microprudential regulation of single banks", "Acting as banker to the government", "Conducting monetary policy", "Acting as lender of last resort"], "a": 2, "x": "Changing Bank Rate to meet the inflation target is monetary policy, carried out by the Monetary Policy Committee. It is not regulation or emergency lending.", "w": ["Regulation sets rules for banks' capital and risk; Bank Rate is a policy instrument.", "The banker role covers holding government accounts, not setting interest rates.", "", "Last-resort lending is emergency help for banks, not a routine rate decision."]}
{"id": "econ-501", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In July 2026 the Financial Policy Committee held the Countercyclical Capital Buffer at 2%. What is the main purpose of this buffer?", "o": ["To let banks absorb shocks without cutting lending", "To guarantee depositors' savings up to a fixed limit", "To raise Bank Rate when inflation is above target", "To fund government borrowing through gilt purchases"], "a": 0, "x": "The CCyB is macroprudential regulation: banks hold extra capital that can be released in a downturn, so they can absorb losses while still lending. It is not a rate tool or deposit guarantee.", "w": ["", "Deposit protection is a separate scheme; confusing capital requirements with deposit insurance.", "Bank Rate is a monetary policy tool set by the MPC, not the FPC's buffer.", "The buffer is about bank capital, not financing the government."]}
{"id": "econ-502", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is the best example of the Bank of England acting as banker to commercial banks?", "o": ["Setting the 2% CPI target for the MPC each year", "Collecting income tax on behalf of the Treasury", "Holding bank reserve accounts to settle debts", "Telling banks which firms they must lend to"], "a": 2, "x": "Commercial banks keep reserve accounts at the Bank of England, which settle payments between banks and provide liquidity. The inflation target is set by government, and the Bank does not direct individual lending.", "w": ["The target is set by the government and relates to monetary policy, not banking services.", "Tax collection is done by HMRC; this confuses the Bank with the government's own departments.", "", "The Bank regulates risk but does not direct lending to particular firms."]}
{"id": "econ-503", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Inflation is forecast above target and the MPC raises Bank Rate. Which chain of reasoning is correct?", "o": ["Higher Bank Rate, dearer borrowing, lower spending, AD falls", "Higher Bank Rate, weaker pound, higher export demand, AD rises", "Higher Bank Rate, cheaper borrowing, higher investment, AD rises", "Higher Bank Rate, lower government spending, so AD falls"], "a": 0, "x": "Higher Bank Rate raises market interest rates, so borrowing costs and saving rewards rise. Consumption and investment fall, AD shifts left and inflationary pressure eases. Do not reverse the direction of effects.", "w": ["", "Higher rates tend to attract hot money and strengthen the pound, reducing export demand.", "Higher Bank Rate makes borrowing dearer, not cheaper; reversing the direction.", "Government spending is fiscal policy; Bank Rate works through private borrowing and saving."]}
{"id": "econ-504", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "lv": 3, "q": "Which is the strongest argument AGAINST a central bank acting as lender of last resort?", "o": ["It reduces the liquidity banks choose to hold in normal times", "It raises Bank Rate whenever a bank fails in the economy", "It makes bank runs more likely by alarming depositors", "It may create moral hazard, as banks expect rescue and gamble"], "a": 3, "x": "If banks expect emergency support, they may take greater risks, because losses are partly borne by others. This is moral hazard. Last-resort lending is meant to reduce, not increase, bank runs.", "w": ["No direct link; the facility concerns emergency lending, not normal-time liquidity holdings.", "Last-resort lending does not change Bank Rate; this confuses two separate roles.", "The aim is to prevent runs by restoring confidence, so this reverses the usual effect.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-505", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is an example of UK government capital expenditure?", "o": ["Paying the state pension to retired people", "Paying the wages of NHS nurses", "Paying interest on government debt", "Building a new railway line"], "a": 3, "x": "Capital spending creates long-lasting assets, such as a railway. Wages and debt interest are current spending; pensions are transfer payments. The trap is calling all large spending 'capital'.", "w": ["A pension is a transfer payment, not spending on an asset.", "Wages are current spending on day-to-day running of services, not asset creation.", "Debt interest is current spending; it does not create a new asset.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-506", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is a transfer payment?", "o": ["Spending on building a new bypass", "The purchase of new computers for a state school", "The salary of a state school teacher", "A Universal Credit payment to a jobseeker"], "a": 3, "x": "A transfer payment redistributes income with no good or service produced in return. Universal Credit fits. Computers and the bypass are purchases or capital spending; teacher pay is current spending on a service.", "w": ["A bypass is capital spending on an asset, not a transfer.", "Buying computers is spending on goods, which is not a transfer.", "Teacher pay buys a service (education), so it is current spending, not a transfer.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-507", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Country A spends a higher share of GDP on public spending than country B. Which is the most likely explanation?", "o": ["A has a larger share of its population of working age", "A's voters prefer a smaller state and lower taxes", "A has an older population needing pensions and healthcare", "A has a larger private sector providing healthcare"], "a": 2, "x": "An older population raises transfer payments (pensions) and current health spending, so public spending is a larger share of GDP. Demographics is one reason; political preferences and stage of development are others.", "w": ["A larger working-age share usually means lower pension and health demands, so lower spending.", "A preference for a smaller state would lower spending, not raise it.", "", "More private healthcare would reduce, not raise, public spending."]}
{"id": "econ-508", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The government increases its borrowing to fund extra spending. Which best describes crowding out?", "o": ["Higher taxes reduce consumer spending, offsetting the extra spending", "Extra spending reduces imports, which cuts the current account deficit", "A lower exchange rate raises exports, adding to the AD rise", "Higher interest rates cut private investment, offsetting some AD rise"], "a": 3, "x": "Crowding out occurs when government borrowing raises demand for loanable funds and interest rates, so private investment falls. This offsets some of the rise in AD. The trap is confusing it with tax effects.", "w": ["This describes a tax effect, not crowding out through borrowing and interest rates.", "Imports are not the channel; crowding out works through the private sector's borrowing costs.", "A fall in the exchange rate is not the crowding-out mechanism; borrowing tends to push rates up.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-509", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose government spending is £500bn: £60bn capital, £180bn transfer payments, the rest current spending on goods and services. GDP is £1,250bn. Current spending on goods and services is what % of GDP?", "o": ["20.8%", "35.2%", "25.6%", "40.0%"], "a": 0, "x": "£500bn − £60bn − £180bn = £260bn. $\\frac{260}{1250}\\times100=20.8\\%$. The traps are forgetting to remove one of the other categories, or using total spending.", "w": ["", "£440bn is 35.2% of GDP, but it forgets to subtract transfer payments.", "£320bn is 25.6% of GDP, but it forgets to subtract capital spending.", "40.0% is total spending as a share of GDP, not the required category."]}
{"id": "econ-510", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 3, "q": "Which situation makes crowding out of private investment LEAST likely after a rise in borrowed government spending?", "o": ["Large spare capacity, and firms' investment is insensitive to interest rates", "The economy is near full capacity, and firms' investment is very interest-sensitive", "The government borrows heavily while private demand for loans is rising", "Financial markets lose confidence and demand higher yields on gilts"], "a": 0, "x": "With spare capacity and interest-insensitive investment, borrowing barely raises rates or competes for resources, so little private investment is lost. Near full capacity, rising loan demand or higher gilt yields all raise crowding out.", "w": ["", "Full capacity and interest-sensitive investment make crowding out more likely, not less.", "Rising private loan demand plus heavy public borrowing pushes rates up, increasing crowding out.", "Higher gilt yields raise borrowing costs across the economy, increasing crowding out."]}
{"id": "econ-511", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement correctly defines a progressive tax?", "o": ["The tax rate falls as income rises", "Everyone pays the same percentage of income", "The average tax rate rises as income rises", "Everyone pays the same £ amount"], "a": 2, "x": "A progressive tax takes a higher proportion of income as income rises, so the average tax rate rises. Paying more £ is not enough; the trap is ignoring the percentage.", "w": ["This describes a regressive tax, where the share of income taken falls as income rises.", "This describes a proportional tax, where the average rate stays constant.", "", "A flat £ amount is a lump-sum tax, which is regressive because it is a smaller share of higher incomes."]}
{"id": "econ-512", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A tax takes £2,000 from a person earning £20,000 and £4,000 from a person earning £50,000. What type of tax is it?", "o": ["Progressive, because the richer person pays more £", "Regressive, because the average rate falls from 10% to 8%", "Proportional, because both pay a positive amount", "Progressive, because the marginal rate is above 8%"], "a": 1, "x": "Average rates are £2,000 ÷ £20,000 = 10% and £4,000 ÷ £50,000 = 8%. The average rate falls as income rises, so the tax is regressive. The trap is looking at £ paid.", "w": ["Judging by £ paid, not by percentage of income. The share of income taken actually falls.", "", "Proportional needs the same percentage for both, but 10% and 8% differ.", "Marginal rate is not the test here, and the average rate falls, so it is not progressive."]}
{"id": "econ-513", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "In 2026–27 UK Income Tax has a £12,570 Personal Allowance, a 20% basic rate, a 40% higher rate and a 45% additional rate above £125,140. Why is it progressive?", "o": ["Because the 20% rate applies to all income, so everyone pays equally", "Because the allowance and higher rates make the average rate rise", "Because it is a direct tax, and all direct taxes are progressive", "Because higher earners pay more £ simply by earning more"], "a": 1, "x": "Income below the allowance is untaxed and higher slices face higher rates, so the average tax rate rises with income. Paying more £ alone would also fit a proportional tax.", "w": ["The 20% rate does not apply to all income; the allowance and higher bands change the average rate.", "", "Not all direct taxes are progressive; it is the rate structure that matters.", "Even a proportional tax takes more £ from higher earners, so this does not show progression."]}
{"id": "econ-514", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "On a Laffer curve the government is at a tax rate above the revenue-maximising rate. What is the likely effect of cutting the rate to the optimum?", "o": ["Revenue rises only if the economy is in recession", "Tax revenue falls, as every cut loses revenue", "Tax revenue is unchanged", "Tax revenue rises"], "a": 3, "x": "Above the optimum the economy is on the downward-sloping part of the curve. A lower rate improves incentives and reduces avoidance, so revenue rises. The trap is assuming cuts always reduce revenue.", "w": ["The Laffer effect depends on the rate relative to the optimum, not on the economic cycle.", "Cuts only lose revenue when the rate is below the optimum, on the upward-sloping section.", "Revenue changes along the curve; it is only unchanged at the peak.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-515", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a tax has no charge on the first £12,000 of income and 20% on income above that. What is the average tax rate on an income of £30,000?", "o": ["12%", "20%", "18%", "40%"], "a": 0, "x": "Taxable income is £30,000 − £12,000 = £18,000. Tax is 20% × £18,000 = £3,600. Average rate is £3,600 ÷ £30,000 = 12%. The trap is quoting the marginal rate of 20%.", "w": ["", "20% is the marginal rate on the top slice, not the average rate on total income.", "£18,000 is the taxable income, not the tax; 18% mixes up income with the tax rate.", "There is no step that doubles the rate; 40% has no basis in the data."]}
{"id": "econ-516", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The top marginal rate of income tax rises from 40% to 50%. Why might hours worked by high earners not fall?", "o": ["Leisure becomes more expensive, so people want to work more", "Income effect may dominate, so they work longer for income", "Take-home pay per hour rises, so they choose to work more", "The Laffer curve shows tax revenue must always rise at 50%"], "a": 1, "x": "A higher marginal rate cuts the reward for work (substitution effect, favouring leisure) but also cuts income, which may push people to work more (income effect). The net effect is ambiguous.", "w": ["A higher tax lowers the reward for work, so leisure becomes cheaper, not more expensive.", "", "A higher marginal rate reduces take-home pay per hour, not raises it.", "The Laffer curve says revenue can fall at high rates; it does not guarantee a rise."]}
{"id": "econ-517", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is an example of an automatic stabiliser in the UK?", "o": ["The Chancellor announces a temporary VAT cut to boost demand in a recession", "The government brings forward a planned road-building programme", "The Bank of England lowers Bank Rate to stimulate spending", "Income tax receipts fall automatically as incomes fall in a downturn"], "a": 3, "x": "Automatic stabilisers work through existing tax and benefit rules, so no new decision is needed. Falling income tax receipts in a downturn are one, and a progressive system strengthens the effect. The other options are discretionary fiscal or monetary policy.", "w": ["This is a discretionary fiscal policy change. It needs a deliberate decision, so it is not automatic.", "Bringing forward spending is a deliberate discretionary choice, not an automatic response.", "Bank Rate is monetary policy set by the MPC, not a built-in feature of tax or welfare.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-518", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 1, "q": "ONS figures showed UK public sector net borrowing of £17.0 billion in October 2026 and provisional net debt of £3,058.3 billion. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Both figures are stocks, each measured at a single point in time", "Borrowing is the accumulated stock, and debt is the flow in the month", "Borrowing is a monthly flow; debt is the stock built from past borrowing", "Borrowing and debt are the same thing, just measured in different units"], "a": 2, "x": "The deficit (net borrowing) is a flow over a period. Debt is a stock: the total owed at a point in time, built up from past deficits. Confusing flow and stock is the classic trap.", "w": ["Borrowing is measured over a period, so it is a flow, not a stock.", "Reversed: debt is the accumulated stock and borrowing is the flow.", "", "They differ: borrowing is a flow that adds to debt, a stock. They are not interchangeable."]}
{"id": "econ-519", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a government's actual budget deficit is £90 billion, of which £30 billion is cyclical because output is below potential. What is the structural deficit?", "o": ["£120 billion", "£60 billion", "£30 billion", "£90 billion"], "a": 1, "x": "Actual deficit = structural + cyclical, so structural = 90 − 30 = £60 billion. This is the deficit that would remain if output were at potential. Adding instead of subtracting is the slip.", "w": ["Adding the cyclical part gives £120 billion. The cyclical part is already inside the actual deficit, so subtract it.", "", "£30 billion is the cyclical part itself, not the structural deficit.", "£90 billion ignores the split. It is the actual deficit, not the structural one."]}
{"id": "econ-520", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a government has debt of £850 billion at the start of a year. It cuts its annual deficit from £50 billion to £30 billion. Assuming nothing else changes, what is debt at the end of the year?", "o": ["£820 billion", "£880 billion", "£850 billion", "£930 billion"], "a": 1, "x": "A deficit of £30 billion is still borrowing, so debt rises by £30 billion to £880 billion. A smaller deficit slows the growth of debt; only a surplus reduces it.", "w": ["This subtracts the deficit. Borrowing adds to debt, so debt rises.", "", "Debt stays the same only if the budget balances. A deficit of £30 billion still adds to it.", "This adds both deficits (£50 billion and £30 billion). Only the current year's £30 billion is added."]}
{"id": "econ-521", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "In October 2026 the OBR estimated that if UK unemployment peaked near 7%, annual borrowing could average £20 billion higher than forecast. Which best explains the automatic stabiliser effect?", "o": ["The government would pass new legislation to raise benefit rates", "The MPC would raise interest rates, so debt interest payments rise", "Tax receipts fall and welfare spending rises automatically as unemployment rises", "Higher unemployment raises income tax receipts, because the tax system is progressive"], "a": 2, "x": "Higher unemployment cuts income tax and VAT receipts and raises welfare payments under existing rules. Borrowing rises with no new decision, which cushions the fall in aggregate demand.", "w": ["New legislation would be discretionary policy. Automatic stabilisers work without any new decision.", "Interest rate decisions are monetary policy and not how stabilisers work.", "", "Reversed: unemployment lowers incomes, so income tax receipts fall."]}
{"id": "econ-522", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 3, "q": "A government has a deficit of 6% of GDP in a recession, of which 2% is cyclical. It then cuts discretionary spending by 1% of GDP, and the economy returns to potential output. What is the deficit?", "o": ["5% of GDP", "4% of GDP", "3% of GDP", "1% of GDP"], "a": 2, "x": "Structural deficit = 6 − 2 = 4%. The spending cut lowers it to 3%. At potential output the cyclical part is zero, so the deficit is 3%. Forgetting the cyclical fall gives 5%.", "w": ["This applies the cut to the full 6% and forgets the cyclical 2% disappears at potential output.", "This ignores the discretionary cut. 4% is the structural deficit before the cut.", "", "This subtracts the cut from the cyclical 2% only, instead of from the 4% structural deficit."]}
{"id": "econ-523", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "lv": 1, "q": "What is meant by transfer pricing?", "o": ["The price a government sets for exports to protect home firms", "Charging different prices to customers in different countries for one product", "The fixing of a currency's value against another currency's value", "Prices charged between subsidiaries of one multinational, shifting profit"], "a": 3, "x": "Transfer pricing is the pricing of goods, services or assets traded within a multinational. Prices can be set to move profit to low-tax countries. Do not confuse it with price discrimination, which is about customers.", "w": ["Government-set export prices are not transfer pricing; it concerns trade between firms in the same group.", "That is international price discrimination, which targets customers, not trade inside a group.", "That describes a fixed exchange rate policy, not pricing between subsidiaries.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-524", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Suppose a UK subsidiary (25% corporation tax) overpays its sister firm abroad (10% tax) by £2 million, shifting £2 million of profit. What is the group's total tax saving?", "o": ["£0.5 million", "£0.2 million", "£0.7 million", "£0.3 million"], "a": 3, "x": "UK tax falls by 25% of £2m = £0.5m. Foreign tax rises by 10% of £2m = £0.2m. Net saving = £0.3m. The trap is counting only the UK fall and ignoring tax paid abroad.", "w": ["This is only the UK tax fall; it ignores the extra £0.2m tax paid abroad.", "This is only the extra foreign tax; it ignores the larger UK tax fall.", "This adds the two amounts instead of subtracting the extra foreign tax from the UK saving.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-525", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which best explains why one government's ability to raise corporation tax may be limited in a globalised economy?", "o": ["Mobile firms and capital may relocate to lower-tax countries", "Higher tax rates always cause total tax revenue to fall", "The WTO forbids countries from changing corporation tax", "Firms can only be taxed in the country where their shareholders live"], "a": 0, "x": "Capital mobility and tax competition mean firms can shift activity or profits to lower-tax countries. This limits policy independence. Revenue does not always fall, and corporation tax rates are national decisions.", "w": ["", "Revenue does not always fall; it depends on how mobile the tax base is. 'Always' is the slip.", "The WTO does not set national corporation tax rates; these remain a national decision.", "Firms are taxed mainly where profits arise or are booked, not where shareholders live."]}
{"id": "econ-526", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "lv": 2, "q": "HMRC reported UK transfer pricing yield of £3,387 million in 2024–25, up from £1,786 million in 2023–24. To the nearest whole percent, what was the percentage increase?", "o": ["47%", "90%", "190%", "53%"], "a": 1, "x": "Increase = 3,387 − 1,786 = £1,601m. Percentage change = 1,601 ÷ 1,786 × 100 ≈ 90%. The trap is dividing by the new figure, or reporting the ratio 190%.", "w": ["This divides the change by the new figure (3,387) instead of the original.", "", "This is the new figure as a percentage of the old one; you must subtract 100% to get the increase.", "This is the 2023–24 figure as a share of 2024–25, not a percentage increase."]}
{"id": "econ-527", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "lv": 2, "q": "The UK enforces the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax for groups with revenues over €750 million. A group with €900m revenue pays 9% on profits in one low-tax country. What is the likely result?", "o": ["Nothing, because the minimum applies only to groups with revenues over €1 billion", "The profits are exempt because they are taxed abroad already", "A top-up tax raises the effective rate to the UK's full corporation tax rate", "A top-up tax raises the effective rate on those profits to 15%"], "a": 3, "x": "The group is over the €750m threshold, so a top-up tax brings the effective rate on those profits up to 15%, a 6 percentage point top-up. It sets a floor, not the UK rate.", "w": ["The threshold is €750 million, not €1 billion, so a €900m group is covered.", "Low foreign tax is exactly what triggers the top-up; being taxed abroad gives no exemption.", "The minimum is 15%, a floor; it does not equalise the rate with the UK's higher rate.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-528", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "lv": 3, "q": "Between July and November 2026, market-implied US and euro-area interest rate paths rose by around 65 and 50 basis points. Why does this limit UK policymakers' choices over interest rates?", "o": ["The Monetary Policy Committee is legally required to match US interest rates", "Higher foreign rates lower UK import prices, so UK inflation falls automatically", "Higher foreign rates risk capital outflow if UK rates are cut, weakening the pound", "Higher foreign rates mean the UK must raise taxes to keep the budget balanced"], "a": 2, "x": "Higher overseas returns can pull funds out of UK assets. If the UK cut rates, sterling could fall, raising import prices and inflation. Policy is influenced by global conditions, not legally tied to them.", "w": ["There is no legal requirement to match US rates; the constraint is market pressure, not law.", "Higher foreign rates tend to weaken sterling, which raises import prices rather than lowering them.", "", "Foreign interest rates do not directly force tax rises; this confuses monetary and fiscal policy."]}
{"id": "econ-d046", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, what does the shift of the PPF from PPF1 to PPF2 show?", "o": ["A fall in the economy's productive capacity", "An increase in the economy's potential output", "Unemployed resources being brought into use", "A move from one point to another on PPF1"], "a": 1, "x": "An outward shift of the PPF means the economy can produce more of both goods with its resources: potential (long-run) economic growth.", "w": ["Productive capacity has risen, not fallen, as PPF2 lies outside PPF1.", "", "Using unemployed resources is a movement towards the PPF, not a shift of it.", "A move along PPF1 is a trade-off, not growth; the curve itself has moved."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Consumer goods", "ylabel": "Capital goods", "lines": [{"pts": [[6, 0], [5.93, 0.94], [5.71, 1.85], [5.35, 2.72], [4.85, 3.53], [4.24, 4.24], [3.53, 4.85], [2.72, 5.35], [1.85, 5.71], [0.94, 5.93], [0, 6]], "label": "PPF1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[8, 0], [7.9, 1.25], [7.61, 2.47], [7.13, 3.63], [6.47, 4.7], [5.66, 5.66], [4.7, 6.47], [3.63, 7.13], [2.47, 7.61], [1.25, 7.9], [0, 8]], "label": "PPF2", "c": 1, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [3, 3], "label": "C"}, {"at": [4.24, 4.24], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5.66, 5.66], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d047", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The economy moves from point C to point A. Which statement best describes this?", "o": ["Potential growth caused by an outward shift of the PPF", "A fall in actual output as resources are wasted", "A rise in capacity because capital goods are cheaper", "Actual growth as spare capacity is used up"], "a": 3, "x": "C lies inside PPF1, so resources are idle. Moving to A on PPF1 raises actual output without any change in capacity: actual growth.", "w": ["The PPF does not shift in this move; only actual output changes.", "Output rises from C to A, so it does not fall.", "The move is explained by using idle resources, not by cheaper capital goods or a capacity change.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Consumer goods", "ylabel": "Capital goods", "lines": [{"pts": [[6, 0], [5.93, 0.94], [5.71, 1.85], [5.35, 2.72], [4.85, 3.53], [4.24, 4.24], [3.53, 4.85], [2.72, 5.35], [1.85, 5.71], [0.94, 5.93], [0, 6]], "label": "PPF1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[8, 0], [7.9, 1.25], [7.61, 2.47], [7.13, 3.63], [6.47, 4.7], [5.66, 5.66], [4.7, 6.47], [3.63, 7.13], [2.47, 7.61], [1.25, 7.9], [0, 8]], "label": "PPF2", "c": 1, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 3], "to": [4.24, 4.24], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [3, 3], "label": "C"}, {"at": [4.24, 4.24], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5.66, 5.66], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d048", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy moves from C to B. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Actual and potential growth both occurred, and spare capacity was used up", "Only potential growth occurred, as the economy still has spare capacity", "Only actual growth occurred, as the PPF has not moved", "Output has risen but the economy is now less efficient than at C"], "a": 0, "x": "The PPF shifted out (potential growth), and the economy moved from inside PPF1 to a point on PPF2, using up idle resources (actual growth).", "w": ["", "", "", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Consumer goods", "ylabel": "Capital goods", "lines": [{"pts": [[6, 0], [5.93, 0.94], [5.71, 1.85], [5.35, 2.72], [4.85, 3.53], [4.24, 4.24], [3.53, 4.85], [2.72, 5.35], [1.85, 5.71], [0.94, 5.93], [0, 6]], "label": "PPF1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[8, 0], [7.9, 1.25], [7.61, 2.47], [7.13, 3.63], [6.47, 4.7], [5.66, 5.66], [4.7, 6.47], [3.63, 7.13], [2.47, 7.61], [1.25, 7.9], [0, 8]], "label": "PPF2", "c": 1, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 3], "to": [5.66, 5.66], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [3, 3], "label": "C"}, {"at": [4.24, 4.24], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5.66, 5.66], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d049", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram moves from point A to point B. What does it show?", "o": ["Cost-push inflation caused by a fall in SRAS", "Deflation caused by a fall in AD", "Demand-pull inflation caused by a rise in AD", "Disinflation caused by a rise in SRAS"], "a": 2, "x": "AD shifts right from AD1 to AD2 along an upward-sloping SRAS, raising the price level and real GDP. This is demand-pull inflation.", "w": ["SRAS has not moved; the shift shown is in AD.", "The price level rises, so this is not deflation, and AD moves right.", "", "SRAS is unchanged, and a rise in prices is not disinflation."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[3, 9], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[2, 0.5], [7, 8]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5.8, 6.2], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d050", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram moves from point A to point C. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Prices rise and real GDP falls, consistent with cost-push inflation", "Prices rise and real GDP rises, consistent with demand-pull inflation", "Prices fall and real GDP falls, consistent with deflation", "Prices rise and real GDP falls because AD has shifted left"], "a": 0, "x": "SRAS shifts left from SRAS1 to SRAS2 because costs rise. The new equilibrium C has a higher price level and lower real GDP: cost-push inflation.", "w": ["", "Real GDP falls at C, and AD has not shifted.", "The price level is higher at C, not lower.", "AD1 is unchanged; the shift is in SRAS."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 0.5], [7, 8]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[2, 2.5], [7, 10]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "A"}, {"at": [4.2, 5.8], "label": "C"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d051", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy moves from X to Z. What explains the unchanged real GDP and higher price level?", "o": ["Only a rise in AD from AD1 to AD2, which would raise output", "A rise in AD to AD2 and a fall in SRAS to SRAS2 together", "Only a fall in SRAS to SRAS2, which would lower output", "A fall in both AD and SRAS, which would cause deflation"], "a": 1, "x": "AD rising alone would raise output, and SRAS falling alone would cut it. Both shifts together push prices up and leave output at Q1.", "w": ["AD alone would move the economy to a higher output than Q1.", "", "SRAS alone would reduce output below Q1.", "AD is shown shifting right, and the price level rises, so this is not deflation."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[3, 9], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 0.5], [7, 8]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[2, 2.5], [7, 10]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5, 7], "label": "Z", "guides": ["Q1", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d052", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "A minimum wage is set at Wmin. Which quantity shows the number of workers employed?", "o": ["Qe", "Q2", "Q1", "Q2 minus Q1"], "a": 2, "x": "At Wmin firms hire where the wage meets labour demand D, at point A, so employment is Q1.", "w": ["Qe is employment at the free-market equilibrium, before the minimum wage.", "Q2 is the number of people willing to work at Wmin, not those employed.", "", "Q2 minus Q1 is the surplus of labour, meaning the unemployed."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of labour", "ylabel": "Real wage", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 7], [10, 7]], "label": "Wmin", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E", "guides": ["Qe", "We"]}, {"at": [3, 7], "label": "A", "guides": ["Q1"]}, {"at": [7, 7], "label": "B", "guides": ["Q2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d053", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Which distance shows the real-wage unemployment caused by Wmin?", "o": ["Q1 to Q2, the horizontal gap from A to B", "Q1 to Qe, the horizontal gap from A to E", "Zero to Q2, the full distance from origin to B", "Zero to Q1, the full distance from origin to A"], "a": 0, "x": "At Wmin, labour supplied is Q2 (point B) but labour demanded is only Q1 (point A). The surplus Q1 to Q2 is real-wage unemployment.", "w": ["", "Q1 to Qe is only the jobs lost; the unemployed also include the extra workers from Qe to Q2 drawn in by Wmin.", "Zero to Q2 is the total labour supplied at Wmin, which includes the Q1 workers still employed.", "Zero to Q1 is labour demanded at Wmin, so these are the workers still employed, not the unemployed."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of labour", "ylabel": "Real wage", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 7], [10, 7]], "label": "Wmin", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E", "guides": ["Qe", "We"]}, {"at": [3, 7], "label": "A", "guides": ["Q1"]}, {"at": [7, 7], "label": "B", "guides": ["Q2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d054", "s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "lv": 3, "q": "AD falls from AD1 to AD2 and output falls from Y1 to Y2. Which conclusion about unemployment fits the diagram?", "o": ["Frictional unemployment rises because workers take longer to search", "Structural unemployment rises because an industry has permanently declined", "Seasonal unemployment rises because demand varies across the year", "Cyclical unemployment rises because lower output reduces demand for labour"], "a": 3, "x": "A leftward AD shift cuts real output. Firms need less labour, which is derived demand, so cyclical unemployment rises.", "w": ["Frictional unemployment is short-term job search, not caused by a general fall in AD.", "Structural unemployment comes from industry decline and immobility, not an AD shift across the economy.", "Seasonal unemployment follows the time of year, but the diagram shows a one-off fall in AD.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 8], [8, 0]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 5], "to": [4, 4], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Y1", "P1"]}, {"at": [4, 4], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Y2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d055", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, the price level rises from PL1 to PL2. Which describes the change shown?", "o": ["A movement along AD from X to Z", "A rightward shift of the AD curve", "A leftward shift of the AD curve", "A movement from Z to X along AD"], "a": 0, "x": "A change in the price level moves the economy along the same AD curve. Higher price level means lower real GDP demanded, so X to Z.", "w": ["", "The curve itself has not moved; only the price level changed, so there is no shift.", "The AD curve is drawn in one position, so there is no leftward shift.", "The arrow points from X to Z, not the reverse."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [6, 4], "label": "X", "guides": ["Y1", "PL1"]}, {"at": [3, 7], "label": "Z", "guides": ["Y2", "PL2"]}], "arrows": [{"from": [6, 4], "to": [3, 7], "c": 1}]}}
{"id": "econ-d056", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows AD1 shifting to AD2. Which change could explain this?", "o": ["A rise in the UK price level", "A fall in consumer confidence", "A cut in income tax", "A fall in the UK price level"], "a": 2, "x": "AD2 lies to the right of AD1. Higher disposable income raises consumption at every price level, shifting the whole curve rightwards.", "w": ["A price level change causes a movement along AD, not a shift.", "Lower confidence cuts C and would shift AD leftwards.", "", "A price level change moves along the curve rather than shifting it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.5, 8], [8.5, 0.5]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[2.5, 9.5], [9.5, 2.5]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 4.5], "to": [7, 4.5], "c": 2}]}}
{"id": "econ-d057", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "lv": 3, "q": "AD1 shifts to AD2 as shown. Which statement fits the diagram?", "o": ["A rise in G, shifting AD1 to AD2", "A rise in X, moving up along AD1", "A fall in C, moving down along AD1", "A rise in M, shifting AD1 to AD2"], "a": 3, "x": "AD2 is left of AD1, so AD has fallen at every price level. A rise in imports lowers (X-M) and shifts AD left.", "w": ["A rise in G would shift AD rightwards, not leftwards as drawn.", "A rise in X would raise AD, and a non-price change shifts the curve rather than moving along it.", "A fall in C not caused by price level shifts the curve rather than moving along it.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[2.5, 9.5], [9.5, 2.5]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0.5, 8], [8.5, 0.5]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}], "arrows": [{"from": [7, 4.5], "to": [5, 4.5], "c": 2}]}}
{"id": "econ-d058", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "The figure shows a consumption function C and the line C = Y. At which point do households save nothing?", "o": ["Point A", "Point D", "Point B", "Every point on C"], "a": 2, "x": "Saving is zero where consumption equals disposable income, which is where C meets the C = Y line, at point B.", "w": ["At A income is zero but consumption is positive, so households dissave.", "At D consumption is below income, so households are saving a positive amount.", "", "Saving varies along C: negative left of B, zero at B, positive right of B."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Disposable income (Y)", "ylabel": "Consumption (C)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "C = Y", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 8]], "label": "C", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [0, 2], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5, 5], "label": "B"}, {"at": [8, 6.8], "label": "D"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d059", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Using the values read from the axes, what is the $MPC$ between points P and Q on the consumption function C?", "o": ["0.75", "0.50", "2.00", "0.33"], "a": 1, "x": "Income rises from £2,000 to £8,000 (a change of £6,000) and consumption from £3,000 to £6,000 (a change of £3,000). MPC = ΔC/ΔY = 3/6 = 0.50.", "w": ["0.75 is C/Y at Q (6/8), the average propensity to consume, not the change in C over the change in Y.", "", "2.00 divides the change in Y by the change in C (6/3), the reverse of the MPC formula.", "0.33 would need C to rise by only £2,000 as Y rises by £6,000, but the axes show a rise of £3,000."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": true, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "Disposable income (£000)", "ylabel": "Consumption (£000)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 7]], "label": "C", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [2, 3], "label": "P"}, {"at": [8, 6], "label": "Q"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d060", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The figure shows a shift from AD1 to AD2. Which event best explains it and its effect on equilibrium?", "o": ["Falling confidence raises precautionary saving; output and price level both fall", "A fall in mortgage rates raises C; output rises and price level rises", "Rising house prices create a wealth effect; output falls and prices fall", "A lower saving ratio raises C; output rises but the price level falls"], "a": 0, "x": "AD shifts left, so output and the price level both fall from E1 to E2. Falling confidence causing precautionary saving cuts consumption and fits this.", "w": ["", "Cheaper mortgages raise consumption and shift AD right, not left as shown.", "A positive wealth effect raises C and shifts AD right, so output would rise.", "A lower saving ratio raises C and shifts AD right, which is the opposite of the shift shown."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output (Y)", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 6]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "AD1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 8], [8, 0]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 7], "to": [1.5, 6.5], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [6, 4], "label": "E1"}, {"at": [4.667, 3.333], "label": "E2"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d061", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows AD1 shifting to AD2. Which change could have caused this shift?", "o": ["A rise in interest rates making loans dearer", "A fall in firms' retained profits", "A rise in business confidence (animal spirits)", "A rise in corporation tax on company profits"], "a": 2, "x": "Higher animal spirits raise expected returns, so firms increase investment. Investment is a component of AD, so the AD curve shifts right from AD1 to AD2.", "w": ["Higher interest rates discourage investment and would shift AD left, not right.", "Lower retained profits reduce internal funds for investment, shifting AD left.", "", "Higher corporation tax cuts post-tax profit and investment, shifting AD left."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 10], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 3, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [4, 7], "to": [5, 8], "c": 3}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "A"}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "B"}], "anim": null}}
{"id": "econ-d062", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows a move from X to Y with AD fixed. Which best explains it?", "o": ["Positive net investment enlarged the capital stock, shifting supply right", "Gross investment equalled depreciation, so capacity expanded", "Lower interest rates raised AD, pushing the price level down", "Animal spirits rose, shifting LRAS1 left to LRAS2"], "a": 0, "x": "Positive net investment raises the capital stock and productive capacity, shifting LRAS and SRAS right. With AD unchanged, output rises and the price level falls from X to Y.", "w": ["", "If gross investment equals depreciation, net investment is zero and capacity does not grow.", "A rise in AD would shift the AD curve and raise prices; AD is fixed here.", "LRAS moves right from LRAS1 to LRAS2, and animal spirits mainly affect AD."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[2, 10], [9, 3]], "label": "AD", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[3, 1], [10, 8]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[6, 0], [6, 10]], "label": "LRAS1", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[8, 0], [8, 10]], "label": "LRAS2", "c": 3, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [6, 9], "to": [8, 9], "c": 3}], "points": [{"at": [6, 6], "label": "X"}, {"at": [7, 5], "label": "Y"}], "anim": null}}
{"id": "econ-d063", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "lv": 3, "q": "A boom in investment shifts AD1 to AD3. What does the diagram show happening?", "o": ["Output rises to Q2 while the price level stays at P1", "Output exceeds LRAS at Q2 and the price level rises to P2", "The price level falls to P2 as output rises to Q2", "Output stays at Q1 because LRAS limits any rise in AD"], "a": 1, "x": "AD3 meets SRAS at B, where output Q2 is beyond LRAS and the price level has risen to P2. This is an inflationary gap, unsustainable until capacity grows.", "w": ["", "", "", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[4, 10], [9, 5]], "label": "AD3", "c": 3, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 0], [6, 10]], "label": "LRAS", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [4, 7], "to": [5.5, 8.5], "c": 3}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "A", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [7, 7], "label": "B", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}], "anim": null}}
{"id": "econ-d064", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows a change in aggregate demand, moving equilibrium from X to Y. Which event could cause this?", "o": ["A rise in income tax rates", "More government spending on new school buildings", "A cut in capital spending on roads", "Higher tax receipts as incomes rise in a boom"], "a": 1, "x": "The diagram shows AD shifting rightwards, raising real GDP and the price level. Building schools is capital spending, part of G, so more of it shifts AD rightwards.", "w": ["Higher income tax reduces disposable income and would shift AD leftwards.", "", "Cutting capital spending lowers G, so AD would shift leftwards, not rightwards.", "This is an automatic stabiliser which dampens AD in a boom; it does not raise it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1.5], [10, 6]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[4, 9], [10, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 7], "to": [7.5, 7.5], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [6, 4], "label": "X"}, {"at": [8, 5], "label": "Y"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d065", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows a fiscal policy change moving AD from AD1 to AD2, so equilibrium moves from point Y to point X. What happens to real GDP and the price level?", "o": ["Price level falls and real GDP rises", "Real GDP falls and the price level rises", "Neither the price level nor real GDP changes", "Both the price level and real GDP fall"], "a": 3, "x": "The diagram shows AD shifting leftwards, as contractionary fiscal policy would cause. Equilibrium moves down SRAS from Y to X, so both real GDP and the price level fall.", "w": ["Output rises only after a rightward AD shift, but here AD has shifted leftwards.", "Output and prices move in the same direction along an upward-sloping SRAS curve.", "AD has shifted, so the equilibrium price level and output must change.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[4, 9], [10, 3]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1.5], [10, 6]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [7.5, 7.5], "to": [5, 7], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [8, 5], "label": "Y"}, {"at": [6, 4], "label": "X"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d066", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "lv": 3, "q": "In a recession AD falls from AD1. Without stabilisers it would fall to AD3. Where does the economy end up, and why?", "o": ["Y: extra welfare spending and lower tax receipts limit the fall", "Z: automatic stabilisers fully remove the effect of the recession", "X: stabilisers keep real GDP at its original level", "Y: Parliament passed a new law to raise government spending"], "a": 0, "x": "Stabilisers cushion the fall without new laws. Extra welfare spending and lower tax receipts limit the AD fall to AD2, so output falls only to Y, not Z.", "w": ["", "Z is the outcome without stabilisers, which would let AD fall further to AD3.", "Stabilisers only limit the fall; they do not stop it, so output still falls below X.", "Stabilisers work automatically; a new law would be discretionary policy."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[4, 9], [10, 3]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1.5], [10, 6]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[2.5, 9], [9.5, 2]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD3", "c": 3, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [8, 5], "label": "X"}, {"at": [7, 4.5], "label": "Y"}, {"at": [6, 4], "label": "Z"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d067", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows the market for sterling. What does the move from E1 to E2 represent?", "o": ["An appreciation caused by a rise in demand for £", "A depreciation caused by a rise in the supply of £", "A depreciation caused by a fall in demand for £", "An appreciation caused by a fall in the supply of £"], "a": 2, "x": "D1 shifting left to D2 is a fall in demand for pounds, which lowers the price of the pound from P1 to P2. That is a depreciation.", "w": ["A rise in demand would shift the curve right and raise the price.", "The supply curve S1 has not moved, so supply did not change.", "", "Supply does not move in the diagram, and a lower price is a depreciation."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of pounds (£)", "ylabel": "Price of £ in foreign currency", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 7], [7, 1]], "label": "D2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [4, 4], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d068", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows AD1 shifting to AD2. Which event most likely caused this shift?", "o": ["A depreciation of the pound that raises (X-M)", "Retaliatory tariffs that cut UK export sales", "An appreciation of the pound that raises imports", "A slump in world income reducing overseas demand"], "a": 0, "x": "AD2 lies right of AD1. A depreciation makes exports cheaper and imports dearer, so net trade rises and AD shifts right, raising output and prices.", "w": ["", "Falling exports would reduce (X-M) and shift AD left, not right.", "A stronger pound makes imports cheaper, worsening net trade and shifting AD left.", "Lower world income cuts export demand, shifting AD leftwards."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[3, 9], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 7], "to": [3, 9], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Y1", "P1"]}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Y2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d069", "s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "lv": 3, "q": "A depreciation occurs at T. Why is net trade at B below its level at T?", "o": ["Exports fall because the weaker pound makes them dearer abroad", "The Marshall-Lerner condition holds, so net trade always worsens", "UK incomes fall, which raises spending on imports immediately", "Demand for X and M is inelastic at first, so import bills rise"], "a": 3, "x": "Soon after depreciation, contracts and few substitutes make demand inelastic. Dearer imports raise the import bill before volumes adjust, so (X-M) dips, then improves: the J-curve.", "w": ["A weaker pound makes exports cheaper abroad, not dearer.", "If Marshall-Lerner holds, net trade eventually improves, as the curve later shows.", "Falling incomes would reduce import spending, and this is not what causes the dip.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Net trade (X-M)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 5], [1, 5], [2, 5], [3, 4], [4, 3.3], [5, 3.6], [6, 4.6], [7, 6], [8, 7.4], [9, 8.4]], "label": "Net trade", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [2, 5], "label": "T"}, {"at": [4, 3.3], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d070", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, which curve is the long-run aggregate supply curve?", "o": ["The vertical line, LRAS, showing potential output", "The upward sloping line, SRAS, as costs can change", "The downward sloping line, AD, showing total spending", "None, as the long run has no curve in this diagram"], "a": 0, "x": "The vertical curve marked LRAS shows potential output in the neoclassical view, which does not depend on the price level.", "w": ["", "SRAS is the short-run curve, drawn with some costs fixed.", "AD is aggregate demand, not supply.", "LRAS is clearly drawn and labelled as the vertical line."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[6, 0], [6, 10]], "label": "LRAS", "c": 3}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d071", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The economy starts at E1. A shift of SRAS1 to SRAS2 takes it to E2. What is the most likely cause?", "o": ["A rise in the price level caused by higher AD", "A cut in business taxes that lowers firms' costs", "A rise in raw material prices facing firms", "Faster productivity growth across the economy"], "a": 2, "x": "SRAS2 lies left of SRAS1, so costs have risen. Higher raw material prices raise costs at every price level, raising prices and cutting output.", "w": ["A price level change causes a movement along SRAS, not a shift.", "Lower costs would shift SRAS right, not left.", "", "Higher productivity lowers unit costs and shifts SRAS right."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [8, 10]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1"}, {"at": [4, 6], "label": "E2"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d072", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "lv": 3, "q": "The shock shifts SRAS1 to SRAS2 and is temporary. Which statement best describes the outcome shown?", "o": ["Output rises above LRAS and prices fall at E2", "Output falls below LRAS and prices rise at E2", "Output at E2 is above potential and LRAS shifts right", "Potential output falls because LRAS shifts left to E2"], "a": 1, "x": "E1 is at potential output on LRAS. E2 has a higher price level and lower real GDP, left of LRAS. Potential output is unchanged because LRAS has not moved.", "w": ["E2 has a higher price level than E1, not lower, and output has fallen.", "", "E2 is left of LRAS, so output is below potential, not above.", "LRAS is drawn fixed; a temporary cost shock does not shift it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [8, 10]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[5, 0], [5, 10]], "label": "LRAS", "c": 3}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1"}, {"at": [4, 6], "label": "E2"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d073", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows SRAS shifting from SRAS1 to SRAS2. Which event could explain this shift?", "o": ["A fall in the world price of oil", "A rise in employer National Insurance", "A government subsidy to manufacturers", "An appreciation of sterling"], "a": 1, "x": "SRAS2 lies to the left of SRAS1, so costs have risen at every price level. A higher employer National Insurance rate raises firms' unit costs.", "w": ["Cheaper oil lowers costs and would shift SRAS right, not left.", "", "A subsidy lowers costs and would shift SRAS right, not left.", "Appreciation makes imported inputs cheaper, which shifts SRAS right."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [8, 10]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [7, 7], "to": [5, 7], "c": 2}], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d074", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "SRAS shifts from SRAS1 to SRAS2 with AD unchanged. What happens to the equilibrium?", "o": ["Price level rises to P2 and real GDP rises to Y2", "Price level rises to P2 and real GDP falls to Y2", "Price level falls to P2 and real GDP falls to Y2", "Price level falls to P2 and real GDP rises to Y2"], "a": 3, "x": "SRAS2 is to the right of SRAS1. The equilibrium moves from E1 to E2, where P2 is below P1 and Y2 is above Y1.", "w": ["The price level falls at E2, since E2 sits lower than E1 on the diagram.", "A rightward shift does not lower real GDP, and E2 is also at a lower price level.", "Real GDP at Y2 is higher than Y1, because E2 lies to the right of E1.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[2, 0], [10, 8]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 3], "to": [5, 3], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Y1", "P1"]}, {"at": [6, 4], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Y2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d075", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy moves from X to Y. Which explanation fits the diagram best?", "o": ["Lower raw material costs shifted SRAS from SRAS1 to SRAS2", "Higher employer National Insurance shifted SRAS from SRAS1 to SRAS2", "A fall in the price level caused a movement along SRAS1", "Sterling depreciated, shifting SRAS from SRAS2 to SRAS1"], "a": 0, "x": "X is on SRAS1 and Y is on SRAS2, so the curve shifted right. Lower input costs do this, giving a lower price level and higher real GDP.", "w": ["", "Higher employer NI raises costs and shifts SRAS left, the opposite direction to this shift.", "X and Y lie on different curves, so this is a shift, not a movement along one curve.", "The shift runs from SRAS1 to SRAS2, not the reverse, and depreciation shifts SRAS left."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [8, 10]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 6], "label": "X", "guides": ["Y1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5, 5], "label": "Y", "guides": ["Y2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d076", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "On the diagram, what has caused LRAS1 to shift to LRAS2?", "o": ["A fall in the capital stock", "An improvement in technology", "A fall in the price level", "A rise in aggregate demand"], "a": 1, "x": "LRAS has shifted right, so potential output has risen. That comes from more or better factors of production, such as improved technology.", "w": ["A fall in capital reduces capacity, so LRAS would shift left, not right.", "", "LRAS does not depend on the price level, so a price change cannot shift it.", "A rise in AD moves the economy along LRAS; it does not shift LRAS."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[4, 0.5], [4, 9.5]], "label": "LRAS1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[6.5, 0.5], [6.5, 9.5]], "label": "LRAS2", "c": 2}], "arrows": [{"from": [4.2, 9], "to": [6.3, 9], "c": 2}], "points": [], "areas": []}}
{"id": "econ-d077", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "AD shifts from AD1 to AD2, moving the economy from A to B. What does the diagram show?", "o": ["Output rises; price level stays the same", "Output falls as bottlenecks push prices up", "LRAS shifts right because AD has risen", "Price level rises with output unchanged"], "a": 0, "x": "A and B lie on the flat section of the Keynesian LRAS. Spare capacity lets output rise without pushing up the price level. AD shifts; LRAS does not.", "w": ["", "Output rises from A to B, and bottlenecks only appear further along the curve.", "A change in AD moves the economy along LRAS; it does not shift it.", "Higher prices with fixed output happen on the vertical section, not the flat one."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.5, 2], [4, 2], [5.5, 2.5], [6.8, 3.6], [7.6, 5], [8, 6.5], [8, 9.5]], "label": "Keynesian LRAS", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0.5, 3.2], [4, 0.4]], "label": "AD1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 4], [5.5, 0.4]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [2, 2], "label": "A"}, {"at": [3.5, 2], "label": "B"}], "areas": []}}
{"id": "econ-d078", "s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy is at point X. What happens when AD1 shifts to AD2?", "o": ["Real GDP rises, price level unchanged", "Real GDP falls, price level rises", "Price level rises, real GDP unchanged", "LRAS shifts right to meet the demand"], "a": 2, "x": "X is on the vertical section at full capacity, so higher AD cannot raise real GDP. It only pushes the price level up, from X to Y.", "w": ["Higher output with steady prices happens on the flat section, not at full capacity.", "Output does not fall; it stays at full capacity.", "", "AD does not shift LRAS; only changes in productive capacity do."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0.5, 2], [4, 2], [5.5, 2.5], [6.8, 3.6], [7.6, 5], [8, 6.5], [8, 9.5]], "label": "Keynesian LRAS", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[5, 9.4], [10, 5.4]], "label": "AD1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[7, 9.3], [10, 6.9]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [8, 7], "label": "X"}, {"at": [8, 8.5], "label": "Y"}], "areas": []}}
{"id": "econ-d079", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, at which level of national income is the economy in equilibrium?", "o": ["Y1, where J is above W", "Y2, where J equals W", "Y3, where W is above J", "Any level, since J is constant"], "a": 1, "x": "Equilibrium is where injections equal withdrawals. J and W cross at E, which is at income Y2, so income has no tendency to change.", "w": ["At Y1 the W line is below J, so J exceeds W and income would rise.", "", "At Y3 the W line is above J, so income would fall, not be stable.", "J being constant does not make every level stable; only where it meets W."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "National income (Y)", "ylabel": "Injections and withdrawals", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "W", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "J", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E", "guides": ["Y2", "J=W"]}, {"at": [2, 5], "label": "A", "guides": ["Y1"]}, {"at": [8, 5], "label": "B", "guides": ["Y3"]}], "anim": null}}
{"id": "econ-d080", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The economy is at income Y1 on the diagram. What will happen to national income?", "o": ["It rises, because injections exceed withdrawals", "It stays at Y1, because the flow is continuous", "It rises, because withdrawals exceed injections", "It falls towards Y2, because withdrawals exceed injections"], "a": 3, "x": "At Y1, point X on W lies above J, so W exceeds J. Less spending returns to firms, output and incomes fall, and income moves down to Y2.", "w": ["At Y1 injections are below withdrawals, not above.", "A continuous flow does not mean income is stable; only J = W gives that.", "Withdrawals exceeding injections reduces income; it cannot make it rise.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "National income (Y)", "ylabel": "Injections and withdrawals", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "W", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "J", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E", "guides": ["Y2"]}, {"at": [8, 7], "label": "X", "guides": ["Y1"]}], "anim": null}}
{"id": "econ-d081", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "lv": 3, "q": "The diagram shows a shift from W1 to W2. What most likely caused it and what is the result?", "o": ["A rise in exports, so income rises from Y1 to Y2", "A rise in saving or taxes, so income falls from Y1 to Y2", "A fall in imports, so income rises from Y1 to Y2", "A rise in investment, so income falls from Y1 to Y2"], "a": 1, "x": "W2 lies above W1, so withdrawals are higher at every income. Higher saving or taxes would do this. J is then below W at Y1, so income falls to Y2.", "w": ["Exports are an injection and would shift J upwards, not W.", "", "Falling imports would shift W down, and income would rise to the right.", "Investment is an injection and would raise income, not lower it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "National income (Y)", "ylabel": "Injections and withdrawals", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "J", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "W1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 2.5], [9, 9.7]], "label": "W2", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 5], "to": [3.125, 5], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Y1"]}, {"at": [3.125, 5], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Y2"]}], "anim": null}}
{"id": "econ-d082", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows a move from X to Y. Which event could have caused it?", "o": ["A rise in firms' energy costs", "A fall in consumer confidence", "A rise in net exports", "A fall in firms' wage costs"], "a": 2, "x": "X to Y has output and the price level both rising along SRAS. That needs AD to shift right, as a rise in net exports does.", "w": ["Higher costs shift SRAS left, not AD right, and would cut output.", "Lower confidence shifts AD left, lowering output and prices.", "", "Lower costs shift SRAS right, which lowers the price level."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[3, 9], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X"}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "Y"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d083", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 2, "q": "The economy is at E, with LRAS drawn at potential output. Which statement describes it?", "o": ["Output Q1 is below potential, so there is spare capacity", "Output Q1 is above potential, so inflationary pressure builds", "Output Q1 equals potential output, so there is full employment", "Output Q1 cannot be an equilibrium because it is not on LRAS"], "a": 0, "x": "E lies to the left of LRAS, so actual output is below potential. A negative output gap means spare capacity.", "w": ["", "Output above potential would lie to the right of LRAS, not the left.", "Output equal to potential would be on LRAS, which E is not.", "Short-run equilibrium is AD meeting SRAS; it need not lie on LRAS."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[7, 0], [7, 10]], "label": "LRAS", "c": 3}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d084", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "lv": 3, "q": "AD falls from AD1 to AD2 while SRAS shifts to SRAS2. Compared with E1, what happens at E2?", "o": ["Output falls and the price level is unchanged", "Output rises and the price level falls", "The price level falls and output falls", "The price level falls and output is unchanged"], "a": 3, "x": "Both E1 and E2 sit at the same output, Q1, but E2 has a lower price level, P2. Output is unchanged and prices fall.", "w": ["The price level moves from P1 to P2, so it does change.", "E2 is directly below E1, so output has not risen.", "Output does not fall: both points share the same Q1.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 7], [7, 1]], "label": "AD2", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[3, 1], [9, 7]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5, 3], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q1", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d085", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, the injection alone shifts AD from AD1 to AD2. Which shift is caused by the multiplier effect?", "o": ["AD1 to AD2", "AD2 to AD3", "AD1 to AD3", "AD3 to AD1"], "a": 1, "x": "The injection moves AD from AD1 to AD2. Re-spending of the extra income adds a further shift from AD2 to AD3, which is the multiplier effect.", "w": ["This is the initial injection, before any re-spending of income.", "", "This is the total shift. It includes the injection as well as the multiplier effect.", "This is a leftward shift, which would be a fall in injections."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[2, 8], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[2, 10], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[4, 10], [9, 5]], "label": "AD3", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d086", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 2, "q": "AD shifts from AD1 to AD3, a horizontal shift of 4 units. Real GDP rises only from Y1 to Y3, which is 2 units. Why?", "o": ["The multiplier is below 1 in this economy", "Households stop spending the extra income they get", "The price level rises, so less extra output is bought", "SRAS shifts left when AD shifts to AD3"], "a": 2, "x": "SRAS slopes upwards, so the extra demand pushes the price level up from P1 to P3. Part of the AD shift raises prices rather than output, so real GDP rises by only 2 units, not 4.", "w": ["The size of the multiplier sets how far AD shifts. It cannot explain why output rises by less than that shift.", "Re-spending is what creates the multiplier. The gap comes from the price rise, not from a halt in spending.", "", "Nothing in the diagram shows SRAS shifting. It is one fixed curve."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[2, 8], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[4, 10], [9, 5]], "label": "AD3", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 5], "to": [9, 5], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Y1", "P1"]}, {"at": [7, 7], "label": "E3", "guides": ["Y3", "P3"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d087", "s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "lv": 3, "q": "AD1 shifts to AD2 after an injection, and AD3 shows the final position. The MPT then rises, with the same injection. Which curve shows the new final AD?", "o": ["AD4", "AD3", "AD5", "AD2"], "a": 0, "x": "A higher MPT raises MPW and lowers the multiplier. The final shift is smaller than AD3's but still bigger than the injection alone, so it lies between AD2 and AD3: AD4.", "w": ["", "AD3 is the original final position, which assumed the lower MPT.", "AD5 lies to the right of AD3, which would need a larger multiplier.", "AD2 is the injection alone, which would mean no multiplier. The multiplier is still above 1."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[2, 8], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[2, 10], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[3, 10], [9, 4]], "label": "AD4", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[4, 10], [9, 5]], "label": "AD3", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[5, 10], [9, 6]], "label": "AD5", "c": 1, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d088", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, which movement shows potential growth?", "o": ["X to Y, as spare resources are used up to reach PPF1", "Y to X, as output falls further below capacity", "Y to Z, as capacity rises from PPF1 to PPF2", "Z to Y, as output moves back to the old PPF"], "a": 2, "x": "Potential growth is an outward shift of the PPF. Y lies on PPF1 and Z lies on PPF2, so moving from Y to Z needs higher productive capacity.", "w": ["X to Y is actual growth: a move towards the existing PPF, with no change in capacity.", "A fall from Y to X is a fall in actual output, not a rise in capacity.", "", "Z to Y would need the PPF to shift inwards, which is a fall in capacity."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Capital goods", "ylabel": "Consumer goods", "lines": [{"label": "PPF1", "c": 0, "pts": [[6, 0], [5.91, 1.04], [5.64, 2.05], [5.2, 3], [4.6, 3.86], [3.86, 4.6], [3, 5.2], [2.05, 5.64], [1.04, 5.91], [0, 6]]}, {"label": "PPF2", "c": 1, "dash": true, "pts": [[8, 0], [7.88, 1.39], [7.52, 2.74], [6.93, 4], [6.13, 5.14], [5.14, 6.13], [4, 6.93], [2.74, 7.52], [1.39, 7.88], [0, 8]]}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [3, 3], "label": "X"}, {"at": [4.24, 4.24], "label": "Y"}, {"at": [5.66, 5.66], "label": "Z"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d089", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "In the diagram, what explains the move from E1 to E2?", "o": ["Actual growth from AD rising, with potential output unchanged", "Potential growth, because real GDP has risen from E1 to E2", "Potential growth, because AD1 has shifted to the right", "Actual growth, with the negative output gap getting wider"], "a": 0, "x": "AD1 shifts to AD2, raising real GDP along SRAS. LRAS has not moved, so potential output is unchanged, and the gap to LRAS narrows.", "w": ["", "Potential growth needs LRAS to shift; rising real GDP alone is actual growth.", "An AD shift is a demand-side change; it does not raise productive capacity.", "Real GDP moves from 5 to 6, closer to LRAS at 7, so the negative gap narrows."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"label": "SRAS", "c": 1, "pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]]}, {"label": "AD1", "c": 0, "pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]]}, {"label": "AD2", "c": 3, "pts": [[3, 9], [9, 3]]}, {"label": "LRAS", "c": 2, "pts": [[7, 0], [7, 10]]}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1"}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "E2"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d090", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy moves from E1 to E2 as AD1 shifts to AD2 and LRAS1 shifts to LRAS2. What happens to the negative output gap?", "o": ["It narrows, because actual growth has occurred", "It is unchanged, because both real GDP and LRAS have risen", "It closes, because real GDP has moved closer to LRAS1", "It widens, because LRAS rose by more than real GDP"], "a": 3, "x": "The gap is LRAS minus real GDP. It was 7 − 5 = 2 at E1 and is 9 − 6 = 3 at E2, so potential growth outpaced actual growth.", "w": ["Actual growth narrows the gap only if it exceeds potential growth, and here it does not.", "The gap changes whenever the two grow by different amounts; here it goes from 2 to 3.", "The gap is measured against LRAS2 after the shift, and E2 is still left of it.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"label": "SRAS", "c": 1, "pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]]}, {"label": "AD1", "c": 0, "pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]]}, {"label": "AD2", "c": 3, "pts": [[3, 9], [9, 3]]}, {"label": "LRAS1", "c": 2, "pts": [[7, 0], [7, 10]]}, {"label": "LRAS2", "c": 2, "dash": true, "pts": [[9, 0], [9, 10]]}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1"}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "E2"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d091", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, what does equilibrium at point X show about the economy?", "o": ["A positive output gap, with unemployment below its natural rate", "A zero output gap, because the economy is at full employment", "A negative output gap, with actual output Y1 below potential Y*", "Potential output has fallen to Y1 while actual output stays at Y*"], "a": 2, "x": "X lies to the left of LRAS, so actual output Y1 is below potential output Y*. That is a negative output gap, with spare capacity in the economy.", "w": ["A positive gap would put equilibrium to the right of LRAS, not the left.", "A zero gap needs equilibrium exactly on LRAS, and X is to its left.", "", "LRAS marks potential output and has not moved; it is actual output that falls short."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[5, 0], [5, 10]], "label": "LRAS (Y*)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 8], [8, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4.5, 4.5], "label": "X", "guides": ["Y1", "P1"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d092", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "AD shifts from AD1 to AD2. Which describes the change from point A to point B?", "o": ["A positive output gap opens and the price level rises from P1 to P2", "The positive output gap closes as output moves back towards Y*", "A negative output gap opens because the price level rises to P2", "Potential output rises from Y* to Y2 as the economy grows"], "a": 0, "x": "At A the economy is on LRAS with no gap. At B output Y2 exceeds potential Y*, so a positive gap opens, and the price level rises from P1 to P2.", "w": ["", "There was no gap at A to close; the economy moved from zero gap to a positive one.", "Output Y2 is right of LRAS, so the gap is positive, not negative.", "LRAS has not shifted, so potential output stays at Y*; only actual output rises."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[5, 0], [5, 10]], "label": "LRAS (Y*)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 2, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[2, 10], [9, 3]], "label": "AD2", "c": 3}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 7], "to": [5, 7], "c": 3}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "A", "guides": ["Y*", "P1"]}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "B", "guides": ["Y2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d093", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The x-axis is in £100bn of real GDP. Using the equilibrium at X, what is the output gap as a percentage of potential output?", "o": ["+5.0%", "-5.3%", "-0.4%", "-5.0%"], "a": 3, "x": "Potential output is £800bn and actual output is £760bn. The gap is (760 − 800) ÷ 800 × 100 = −5.0%, dividing by potential output.", "w": ["The gap is negative because X lies left of LRAS, so the sign is wrong.", "This divides by actual output (£760bn) instead of potential output.", "This is the gap in £100bn units (0.4), not the percentage of potential output.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP (£100bn)", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[8, 0], [8, 10]], "label": "LRAS (Y*)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 2], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 9.2], [9, 6.2]], "label": "AD1", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [7.6, 7.6], "label": "X", "guides": ["7.6", "P1"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d094", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which point on the diagram shows the peak (boom) of the trade cycle?", "o": ["Point M", "Point Q", "Point N", "Point P"], "a": 2, "x": "The peak is the highest point of actual GDP before it turns down. That is point N, where output is furthest above trend.", "w": ["M is on the trend line while GDP is rising, so it is part of the recovery.", "Q is well after the peak, on the downward part of the cycle below trend, so it is not the peak.", "", "P is on the downward part of the cycle, below trend, so it is not the peak."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Real GDP", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 3], [8, 6.2]], "label": "Trend growth", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [0.5, 4.05], [1, 4.6], [1.5, 4.45], [2, 3.8], [2.5, 3.15], [3, 3], [3.5, 3.55], [4, 4.6], [4.5, 5.65], [5, 6.2], [5.5, 6.05], [6, 5.4], [6.5, 4.75], [7, 4.6]], "label": "Actual GDP", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 4.6], "label": "M"}, {"at": [5, 6.2], "label": "N"}, {"at": [6.5, 4.75], "label": "P"}, {"at": [7, 4.6], "label": "Q"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d095", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "At which point is real GDP falling and also below its trend level (a negative output gap)?", "o": ["Point P", "Point M", "Point S", "Point N"], "a": 0, "x": "At P actual GDP is on a downward slope and lies below the trend line, so output is falling and the output gap is negative.", "w": ["", "M is on the trend line, so the output gap is zero, and GDP is rising.", "S is below trend, but GDP is rising there, so it is not falling.", "N is the peak, above trend, so the output gap is positive."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Real GDP", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 3], [8, 6.2]], "label": "Trend growth", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [0.5, 4.05], [1, 4.6], [1.5, 4.45], [2, 3.8], [2.5, 3.15], [3, 3], [3.5, 3.55], [4, 4.6], [4.5, 5.65], [5, 6.2], [5.5, 6.05], [6, 5.4], [6.5, 4.75], [7, 4.6]], "label": "Actual GDP", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 4.6], "label": "M"}, {"at": [5, 6.2], "label": "N"}, {"at": [6.5, 4.75], "label": "P"}, {"at": [3.5, 3.55], "label": "S"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d096", "s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "lv": 3, "q": "At point R, actual GDP crosses the trend line while falling from its peak. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["The output gap is positive because GDP is above its level at the start", "The output gap is zero but GDP is falling, so it will go negative", "The output gap is zero, so the economy is at the bottom of the cycle", "The output gap is negative because GDP has fallen from point N"], "a": 1, "x": "At R actual GDP equals trend, so the output gap is zero. GDP is still falling, so it moves below trend and the gap becomes negative.", "w": ["The output gap compares actual GDP with trend, not with the start of the diagram.", "", "A zero gap does not mean the bottom of the cycle; at R GDP is still falling and has not reached its lowest point.", "At R actual GDP is on the trend line, so the gap is zero, not yet negative."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Real GDP", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 3], [8, 6.2]], "label": "Trend growth", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [0.5, 4.05], [1, 4.6], [1.5, 4.45], [2, 3.8], [2.5, 3.15], [3, 3], [3.5, 3.55], [4, 4.6], [4.5, 5.65], [5, 6.2], [5.5, 6.05], [6, 5.4], [6.5, 4.75], [7, 4.6]], "label": "Actual GDP", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 6.2], "label": "N"}, {"at": [6, 5.4], "label": "R"}, {"at": [7, 4.6], "label": "Q"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d097", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows AD1 shifting to AD2. Which policy could have caused this shift?", "o": ["Raising Bank Rate", "Cutting income tax", "Cutting government spending", "Raising income tax"], "a": 1, "x": "A rightward shift of AD is expansionary. Cutting income tax raises disposable income and consumption, shifting AD from AD1 to AD2.", "w": ["A higher Bank Rate raises borrowing costs and shifts AD left, not right.", "", "Lower government spending reduces G and shifts AD left.", "Higher income tax lowers disposable income and consumption, shifting AD left."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [8, 0]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 8], [10, 0]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [1, 6], "to": [3, 6], "c": 1}], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d098", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 2, "q": "AD shifts from AD1 to AD2. What happens in moving from X to Y?", "o": ["Output rises from Q1 to Q2 and the price level falls from P1 to P2", "Output falls from Q1 to Q2 and the price level rises from P1 to P2", "Output rises from Q1 to Q2 and the price level rises from P1 to P2", "Output stays at Q1 while the price level rises from P1 to P2"], "a": 2, "x": "Y lies to the right of and above X on SRAS. Real GDP rises from Q1 to Q2 and the price level rises from P1 to P2.", "w": ["With an upward-sloping SRAS, a rightward AD shift raises the price level, not lowers it.", "Q2 is to the right of Q1, so output rises rather than falls.", "", "Output changes because the economy moves along SRAS to Q2, not staying at Q1."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [8, 0]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 8], [10, 0]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 4], "label": "X", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5, 5], "label": "Y", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d099", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy is at X. Policy-makers consider shifting AD to AD2 or to AD3. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["AD3 closes the output gap at Z but raises the price level more than AD2", "AD2 closes the output gap fully, so Y is at full capacity", "AD3 lowers the price level because it lies further right than AD2", "AD3 raises output permanently beyond the level shown by LRAS"], "a": 0, "x": "Z is where SRAS meets LRAS, so AD3 reaches full capacity. Z is higher up than Y, so the price level rises more, a trade-off.", "w": ["", "Y lies left of LRAS, so a negative output gap remains at AD2.", "A rightward shift along an upward SRAS raises the price level, not lowers it.", "LRAS shows capacity. Demand alone cannot raise output permanently beyond it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [8, 0]], "label": "AD1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 8], [10, 0]], "label": "AD2", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 10], [10, 6]], "label": "AD3", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[8, 0], [8, 10]], "label": "LRAS", "c": 3, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 4], "label": "X"}, {"at": [5, 5], "label": "Y"}, {"at": [8, 8], "label": "Z"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d100", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 1, "q": "AD is unchanged. What does the shift from LRAS1 to LRAS2 show?", "o": ["A rise in consumer spending increasing aggregate demand", "A rise in the economy's productive capacity", "A temporary fall in firms' costs of production", "A fall in the economy's potential output"], "a": 1, "x": "A rightward shift of LRAS means potential output has risen, which is the aim of successful supply-side policies such as investment incentives or better skills.", "w": ["Higher demand would shift AD, not LRAS, and AD is unchanged here.", "", "A temporary fall in costs shifts SRAS in the short run; LRAS shows the economy's capacity.", "A leftward shift of LRAS would show potential output falling, not the rightward shift drawn."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "AD", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[5, 0], [5, 10]], "label": "LRAS1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[7, 0], [7, 10]], "label": "LRAS2", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 9], "to": [7, 9], "c": 0}], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d101", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 2, "q": "LRAS1 shifts to LRAS2 and SRAS1 to SRAS2. What happens in moving from X to Y?", "o": ["Real GDP rises and the price level rises", "Real GDP falls and the price level falls", "Real GDP is unchanged and the price level falls", "Real GDP rises and the price level falls"], "a": 3, "x": "Moving from X to Y, output rises from the LRAS1 level to the LRAS2 level and the equilibrium price level falls, so growth comes with lower inflationary pressure.", "w": ["A rising price level would need AD to shift right, which has not happened.", "Output rises because potential output has risen; it does not fall.", "Real GDP moves from the LRAS1 level to the higher LRAS2 level, so it changes.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "AD", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 2, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[4, 0], [10, 6]], "label": "SRAS2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[5, 0], [5, 10]], "label": "LRAS1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[7, 0], [7, 10]], "label": "LRAS2", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X"}, {"at": [7, 3], "label": "Y"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d102", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "lv": 3, "q": "The economy is at X. AD and SRAS1 stay fixed while LRAS1 shifts to LRAS2. What follows?", "o": ["The inflationary gap closes and actual output is below potential", "The inflationary gap widens as actual output exceeds potential", "Actual output equals potential output at the same price level", "Potential output falls below actual output at point X"], "a": 0, "x": "At X, output is above LRAS1, an inflationary gap. LRAS2 is further right, so output at X is now below potential, leaving spare capacity and removing that gap.", "w": ["", "Potential output has risen, so actual output at X no longer exceeds it.", "Output at X is below the new LRAS2, so the two are not equal.", "A rightward shift raises potential output; it does not lower it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real GDP", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[2, 10], [10, 2]], "label": "AD", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "SRAS1", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[5, 0], [5, 10]], "label": "LRAS1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[7, 0], [7, 10]], "label": "LRAS2", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [5, 9.5], "to": [7, 9.5], "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [6, 6], "label": "X", "guides": ["Y", "P"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d103", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 1, "q": "The economy moves from point A to point B along SRPC. What does this show?", "o": ["Lower inflation and lower unemployment together", "Lower inflation, at the cost of higher unemployment", "Higher inflation, together with higher unemployment", "Higher inflation, at the cost of lower unemployment"], "a": 1, "x": "Moving from A to B, inflation falls from I1 to I2 but unemployment rises from U1 to U2. This is a movement along the curve, a trade-off between the two objectives.", "w": ["The SRPC slopes downwards, so inflation and unemployment do not fall together along it.", "", "Inflation falls from A to B, it does not rise, and the SRPC does not give both rising together.", "Inflation falls from A to B, not rises; the unemployment rate rises, not falls."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Unemployment rate", "ylabel": "Inflation rate", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 8], [1.5, 5.33], [2, 4], [3, 2.67], [4, 2], [5, 1.6], [6, 1.33], [7, 1.14], [8, 1], [9, 0.89]], "label": "SRPC", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [2, 4], "to": [4, 2], "c": 1}], "points": [{"at": [2, 4], "label": "A", "guides": ["U1", "I1"]}, {"at": [4, 2], "label": "B", "guides": ["U2", "I2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d104", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 2, "q": "The economy moves from SRPC1 to SRPC2. Which event is the most likely cause?", "o": ["A fall in aggregate demand along the curve", "A rise in productivity that lowers firms' costs", "A fall in the inflation expected by workers and firms", "A rise in expected inflation or an energy cost shock"], "a": 3, "x": "SRPC2 lies to the right of SRPC1, giving higher inflation at every unemployment rate. Rising inflation expectations or a fall in SRAS from a cost shock cause this outward shift.", "w": ["A fall in AD causes a movement along one curve, not a shift to a new curve.", "Higher productivity lowers costs and would shift the curve inwards, not outwards.", "Lower expected inflation would shift the curve inwards, towards the origin.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Unemployment rate", "ylabel": "Inflation rate", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 8], [1.5, 5.33], [2, 4], [3, 2.67], [4, 2], [5, 1.6], [6, 1.33], [7, 1.14], [8, 1], [9, 0.89]], "label": "SRPC1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 10], [1.5, 7.33], [2, 6], [3, 4.67], [4, 4], [5, 3.6], [6, 3.33], [7, 3.14], [8, 3], [9, 2.89]], "label": "SRPC2", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [4, 2], "to": [4, 4], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [4, 2], "label": "B"}, {"at": [4, 4], "label": "E"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d105", "s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "lv": 3, "q": "Unemployment stays at 4%. The economy moves from B to E. What best describes this?", "o": ["Inflation is 2 percentage points higher, as the trade-off has worsened", "Inflation is 2 percentage points higher, from a move along SRPC1", "Inflation is 4 percentage points higher, from a rise in AD", "Inflation is 2 percentage points lower, as the trade-off has improved"], "a": 0, "x": "At 4% unemployment, inflation is 2% on SRPC1 and 4% on SRPC2, so 2 points higher. The outward shift is a worse trade-off, as with stagflation.", "w": ["", "A move along SRPC1 would change unemployment too; here the whole curve has shifted.", "Inflation rises from 2% to 4%, a 2 point rise, not 4, and AD is not the cause.", "Inflation rises, not falls, and an outward shift is a worse trade-off, not a better one."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": true, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Unemployment rate (%)", "ylabel": "Inflation rate (%)", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 8], [1.5, 5.33], [2, 4], [3, 2.67], [4, 2], [5, 1.6], [6, 1.33], [7, 1.14], [8, 1], [9, 0.89]], "label": "SRPC1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 10], [1.5, 7.33], [2, 6], [3, 4.67], [4, 4], [5, 3.6], [6, 3.33], [7, 3.14], [8, 3], [9, 2.89]], "label": "SRPC2", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [4, 2], "to": [4, 4], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [4, 2], "label": "B"}, {"at": [4, 4], "label": "E"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d157", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Country P's PPF is shown. What is the opportunity cost of one unit of wheat?", "o": ["0.5 units of cloth", "2 units of cloth", "4 units of cloth", "8 units of cloth"], "a": 1, "x": "P gives up 8 cloth to gain 4 wheat, so one wheat costs 8 ÷ 4 = 2 cloth. This is the slope of the PPF.", "w": ["This inverts the ratio and gives the opportunity cost of cloth in wheat.", "", "This is the maximum wheat output, not the cloth given up per wheat.", "This is the maximum cloth output, not the cost per unit of wheat."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Wheat", "ylabel": "Cloth", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [4, 0]], "label": "PPF P", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d158", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "PPF A and PPF B are shown. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["A has a comparative advantage in wheat because it can make more wheat", "Neither country can gain from trade because A can make more of both goods", "B has a comparative advantage in wheat: it gives up 1 cloth, A gives up 2", "B has a comparative advantage in cloth because its PPF is flatter"], "a": 2, "x": "B gives up 4 cloth for 4 wheat, so 1 cloth per wheat. A gives up 10 for 5, so 2. B's lower cost gives it the comparative advantage in wheat.", "w": ["More output is absolute advantage; A's wheat cost of 2 cloth is higher than B's.", "Absolute advantage in both goods does not remove the gains; the opportunity costs still differ.", "", "A flatter PPF means cheaper wheat, not cheaper cloth; A has the lower cost of cloth."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Wheat", "ylabel": "Cloth", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [5, 0]], "label": "PPF A", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [4, 0]], "label": "PPF B", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d159", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Country H specialises in wheat and trades along the dashed trade line. What does point X show?", "o": ["H consumes beyond its own PPF, made possible by specialising and trading", "H could reach X without trade by using its resources more efficiently", "X is the point of productive efficiency on H's PPF", "H has an absolute disadvantage in wheat, so it gains nothing at X"], "a": 0, "x": "X lies outside PPF H, so H cannot produce it alone. By specialising in wheat and exchanging at the terms of trade, H consumes more than before.", "w": ["", "Points outside the PPF are unattainable with H's own resources and no trade.", "Productive efficiency is on the PPF; X lies outside it.", "The trade line shows gains from trade; absolute advantage does not decide this."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Wheat", "ylabel": "Cloth", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 6], [3, 0]], "label": "PPF H", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 7.5], [3, 0]], "label": "Trade line", "c": 2, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [1, 5], "label": "X"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d160", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, which statement about maximum output is correct?", "o": ["Y can make more cars than X", "X can make more of both goods than Y", "Y can make more steel than X", "X and Y can make equal amounts of steel"], "a": 1, "x": "X's PPF meets the axes at 10 steel and 5 cars, Y's at 4 steel and 4 cars, so X has the larger maximum of both goods.", "w": ["Y's maximum is 4 cars, below X's 5.", "", "Y's maximum is 4 steel, well below X's 10.", "X's maximum is 10 steel, Y's is 4."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Cars", "ylabel": "Steel", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [5, 0]], "label": "X's PPF", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [4, 0]], "label": "Y's PPF", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d161", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Using the diagram, which country has a comparative advantage in steel, and why?", "o": ["Y, because it gives up fewer cars for each steel", "Neither, because X is better at both goods", "Y, because its PPF is closer to the origin", "X, because each steel costs it only 0.5 cars"], "a": 3, "x": "X gives up 5 cars for 10 steel, so 1 steel costs 0.5 cars. Y gives up 4 cars for 4 steel, so 1 steel costs 1 car. X's opportunity cost of steel is lower.", "w": ["Y gives up 1 car per steel, more than X's 0.5.", "Absolute advantage in both goods does not rule out comparative advantage; opportunity costs still differ.", "A PPF nearer the origin means lower output, not lower opportunity cost.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Cars", "ylabel": "Steel", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [5, 0]], "label": "X's PPF", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [4, 0]], "label": "Y's PPF", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d162", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "lv": 3, "q": "In the diagram, P and Q specialise and trade cars for steel. Which terms of trade would benefit both countries?", "o": ["1 car for 2 steel", "1 car for 0.5 steel", "1 car for 3 steel", "1 car for 4 steel"], "a": 0, "x": "P's opportunity cost of a car is 3 steel and Q's is 1 steel. Q specialises in cars, and terms between 1 and 3 steel per car leave both better off, so 2 steel works.", "w": ["", "Q would get less than the 1 steel it gives up per car, so it loses.", "P would pay 3 steel, exactly its own cost, so it gains nothing.", "P would pay more than its own cost of 3 steel, so it loses."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 2, "xlabel": "Cars", "ylabel": "Steel", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 9], [3, 0]], "label": "P's PPF", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 8], [8, 0]], "label": "Q's PPF", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d163", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, the country joins a customs union and buys from the member at Pm instead of the non-member at Pw. Which area is the efficiency loss from trade diversion?", "o": ["Area A", "Area C", "Area B", "Area E"], "a": 2, "x": "Area B is the gap between Pm and Pw on the imports now bought from the member. It is extra real resource cost, since the same goods could come from the non-member more cheaply.", "w": ["Area A is trade creation: domestic output replaced by cheaper imports, which is a gain.", "Area C is extra consumption as price falls, which is a gain.", "", "Area E is a transfer from lost tariff revenue to consumers, not a net loss."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of the good", "ylabel": "Price (£)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [8, 8]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2]], "label": "Pw", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]], "label": "Pw + tariff", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [10, 3]], "label": "Pm", "c": 2, "dash": true}], "areas": [{"pts": [[3, 3], [4, 4], [4, 3]], "label": "A", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 4], [7, 3], [6, 3]], "label": "C", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[4, 2], [6, 2], [6, 3], [4, 3]], "label": "B", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[4, 3], [6, 3], [6, 4], [4, 4]], "label": "E", "c": 2}], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d164", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows a market with a tariff on all imports. The country then joins a customs union, and a member can supply tariff-free at Pm. How do imports change?", "o": ["Rise from 2 units to 4 units", "Rise from 3 units to 7 units", "Fall from 6 units to 4 units", "Rise from 4 units to 6 units"], "a": 0, "x": "With the tariff, price is £4, so demand of 6 minus domestic supply of 4 gives 2 imports. At Pm of £3, demand of 7 minus supply of 3 gives 4 imports.", "w": ["", "These are the quantities supplied and demanded at Pm, not the imports before and after.", "Price falls, so demand rises and domestic supply falls; imports therefore rise, not fall.", "These are the quantities supplied and demanded at the tariff price, not the imports before and after."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": true, "ynums": true, "ystep": 1, "xlabel": "Quantity of the good", "ylabel": "Price (£)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [8, 8]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2]], "label": "Pw", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]], "label": "Pw + tariff", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [10, 3]], "label": "Pm", "c": 2, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d165", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "lv": 3, "q": "Using the areas in the diagram, what is the net welfare change for the country from joining the customs union? Lost tariff revenue is B + E.", "o": ["A + C + B", "E − B", "A + C + E", "A + C − B"], "a": 3, "x": "Consumers gain E from the lower price, which offsets that part of the lost tariff revenue. Net change is the gains A and C from trade creation minus the diversion loss B.", "w": ["B is a loss, so it must be subtracted, not added.", "This ignores the trade creation gains A and C.", "E is only a transfer from government to consumers, so it is not a net gain.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of the good", "ylabel": "Price (£)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [8, 8]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2]], "label": "Pw", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]], "label": "Pw + tariff", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [10, 3]], "label": "Pm", "c": 2, "dash": true}], "areas": [{"pts": [[3, 3], [4, 4], [4, 3]], "label": "A", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 4], [7, 3], [6, 3]], "label": "C", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[4, 2], [6, 2], [6, 3], [4, 3]], "label": "B", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[4, 3], [6, 3], [6, 4], [4, 4]], "label": "E", "c": 2}], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d166", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, which area shows the government's tariff revenue?", "o": ["Area A", "Area C", "Area B", "Areas A and C together"], "a": 2, "x": "Revenue is the tariff per unit multiplied by remaining imports. That is the rectangle between Pw and Pw + tariff, over the quantity still imported, which is area B.", "w": ["Area A is a deadweight loss triangle from costly extra domestic output, not revenue.", "Area C is a deadweight loss triangle from lost consumption, not revenue.", "", "Areas A and C together are the deadweight loss, which the government does not receive."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity", "ylabel": "Price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2]], "label": "Pw", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]], "label": "Pw + tariff", "c": 3}], "areas": [{"pts": [[2, 2], [4, 4], [4, 2]], "label": "A", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[4, 2], [6, 2], [6, 4], [4, 4]], "label": "B", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[6, 4], [8, 2], [6, 2]], "label": "C", "c": 0}]}}
{"id": "econ-d167", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "After the tariff is imposed, which expression gives the quantity of imports?", "o": ["Q3 − Q2", "Q4 − Q1", "Q2 − Q1", "Q4 − Q3"], "a": 0, "x": "At Pw + tariff, domestic supply is Q2 and demand is Q3. Imports fill the gap between them, Q3 − Q2.", "w": ["", "Q4 − Q1 is the quantity of imports before the tariff, at the world price.", "Q2 − Q1 is the rise in domestic output caused by the tariff, not imports.", "Q4 − Q3 is the fall in quantity demanded caused by the tariff, not imports."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity", "ylabel": "Price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2]], "label": "Pw", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]], "label": "Pw + tariff", "c": 3}], "points": [{"at": [2, 2], "guides": ["Q1", "Pw"]}, {"at": [4, 4], "guides": ["Q2", "Pt"]}, {"at": [6, 4], "guides": ["Q3", "Pt"]}, {"at": [8, 2], "guides": ["Q4", "Pw"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d168", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "lv": 3, "q": "Which areas measure the loss of efficiency from the tariff, and why?", "o": ["Area B: revenue is a pure loss to consumers", "Areas A and B: both are costs paid by consumers", "Area A only: consumers lose nothing extra", "Areas A and C: dearer home output and lost sales"], "a": 3, "x": "Area A is output now made at higher cost than imports. Area C is consumption lost because of the higher price. Together they are the deadweight loss. Area B is a transfer to government.", "w": ["Area B is transferred to the government, so it is not lost to society.", "Area B is a transfer, not a loss, and area C is a loss that this option omits.", "Consumers also lose area C, the surplus on units no longer bought, so area A alone is incomplete.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity", "ylabel": "Price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "D", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "S", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 2]], "label": "Pw", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4]], "label": "Pw + tariff", "c": 3}], "areas": [{"pts": [[2, 2], [4, 4], [4, 2]], "label": "A", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[4, 2], [6, 2], [6, 4], [4, 4]], "label": "B", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[6, 4], [8, 2], [6, 2]], "label": "C", "c": 0}]}}
{"id": "econ-d169", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 1, "q": "On the J-curve diagram, which point shows the current account at its worst after the depreciation?", "o": ["Point X, where the depreciation happens", "Point Z, where the curve crosses zero", "Point Y, the lowest point of the curve", "None; the balance improves throughout"], "a": 2, "x": "Point Y is the lowest point on the curve. The current account balance is at its most negative there, before trade volumes adjust.", "w": ["X is the starting position at zero balance, before any worsening has occurred.", "At Z the balance has recovered to zero, so it is not the worst point.", "", "The curve falls first, so the balance does not improve throughout."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time after depreciation", "ylabel": "Current account balance", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "Zero balance", "c": 3, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 5], [0.8, 4.2], [1.6, 3.5], [2.4, 3.1], [3.2, 3.1], [4, 3.5], [5, 4.3], [6, 5.2], [7, 6], [8, 6.6], [9, 7], [10, 7.2]], "label": "J-curve", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [0, 5], "label": "X"}, {"at": [2.8, 3.1], "label": "Y"}, {"at": [5.78, 5], "label": "Z"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d170", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows the J-curve after a depreciation. What best explains the fall in the balance before point Y?", "o": ["Export volumes fall sharply because exports are dearer abroad", "Demand is inelastic at first, so dearer imports raise import spending", "Government spending rises, which pulls in more imports", "The financial account moves into deficit as capital leaves"], "a": 1, "x": "In the short run, import and export volumes respond slowly. Imports cost more in pounds, so spending on them rises and the balance worsens.", "w": ["A depreciation makes exports cheaper abroad, not dearer, so export volumes do not fall.", "", "Nothing in the diagram or a depreciation suggests rising government spending.", "The J-curve concerns the current account; no financial account movement explains the dip."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time after depreciation", "ylabel": "Current account balance", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "Zero balance", "c": 3, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 5], [0.8, 4.2], [1.6, 3.5], [2.4, 3.1], [3.2, 3.1], [4, 3.5], [5, 4.3], [6, 5.2], [7, 6], [8, 6.6], [9, 7], [10, 7.2]], "label": "J-curve", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [2.8, 3.1], "label": "Y"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d171", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "lv": 3, "q": "Using the diagram, which statement about the long-run effect of the depreciation is correct?", "o": ["The balance returns to zero only, so the depreciation had no lasting effect", "The balance ends better than at the start, which suggests PEDx + PEDm > 1 in the long run", "The balance ends worse than at the start, so the Marshall-Lerner condition is met", "The balance at W equals that at Y, since adjustment is complete"], "a": 1, "x": "At W the balance is above its starting level, so net exports rose. That happens when the sum of the PEDs exceeds 1 once volumes have adjusted, as the Marshall-Lerner condition requires.", "w": ["The curve rises well above zero, so the effect is lasting, not zero.", "", "W is above the starting balance, not worse, and the Marshall-Lerner condition holds when the balance improves.", "W is far above Y, so the two balances are not equal."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time after depreciation", "ylabel": "Current account balance", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "Zero balance", "c": 3, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 5], [0.8, 4.2], [1.6, 3.5], [2.4, 3.1], [3.2, 3.1], [4, 3.5], [5, 4.3], [6, 5.2], [7, 6], [8, 6.6], [9, 7], [10, 7.2]], "label": "J-curve", "c": 0}], "points": [{"at": [2.8, 3.1], "label": "Y"}, {"at": [9, 7], "label": "W"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d172", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 1, "q": "The diagram shows the market for sterling. Which statement describes the move from E1 to E2?", "o": ["Depreciation caused by a fall in the supply of pounds", "Appreciation caused by a rise in the demand for pounds", "Devaluation caused by a decision of the central bank", "Appreciation caused by a rise in the supply of pounds"], "a": 1, "x": "Demand shifts right from D1 to D2 along S1, so the rate rises from P1 to P2. A market-driven rise under floating rates is an appreciation.", "w": ["", "", "", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of pounds", "ylabel": "Exchange rate (€ per £)", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[2, 10], [10, 2]], "label": "D2", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [6, 6], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d173", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows a change in the market for pounds. Which of these could have caused it?", "o": ["Foreign tourists spend more in the UK", "Foreign firms buy more UK exports", "Higher UK interest rates attract foreign savers", "UK households buy more goods imported from abroad"], "a": 3, "x": "Supply of pounds shifts right from S1 to S2, so the rate falls from P1 to P2. UK buyers of imports sell pounds to get foreign currency, which raises the supply of pounds.", "w": ["Tourists must buy pounds, which raises demand for pounds rather than supply.", "Exports are paid for in pounds, so this raises demand for the currency.", "Foreign savers buying UK assets raise demand for pounds, which pushes the rate up.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of pounds", "ylabel": "Exchange rate (€ per £)", "lines": [{"pts": [[1, 9], [9, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "S1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[2, 0], [10, 8]], "label": "S2", "c": 1, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [6, 4], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d174", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "lv": 3, "q": "The diagram shows the trade balance after a fall in the exchange rate at A. Which best interprets the section from B to C?", "o": ["Import volumes fall further, so the balance keeps worsening", "The Marshall-Lerner condition fails, so the balance recovers", "Volumes adjust, so the balance improves, as Marshall-Lerner implies", "Prices are fixed, so the rise comes from dearer imports"], "a": 2, "x": "From B the balance rises back to the zero line at C and beyond. Demand becomes more elastic as volumes adjust, which fits the Marshall-Lerner condition being met.", "w": ["The curve is rising from B, so the balance is improving, not worsening.", "Recovery needs the condition to hold, not fail.", "", "Dearer imports explain the fall to B; the later rise is from volumes adjusting."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time after the fall in the rate", "ylabel": "Trade balance", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 5], [10, 5]], "label": "Balance = 0", "c": 2, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 5], [1, 4.1], [2, 3.5], [3, 3.15], [4, 3], [5, 3.2], [6, 3.9], [7, 5], [8, 6.1], [9, 7.1], [10, 8]], "label": "Trade balance", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [0, 5], "label": "A"}, {"at": [4, 3], "label": "B"}, {"at": [7, 5], "label": "C"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d175", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, what has caused the move from point X to point Y?", "o": ["A fall in unit labour costs shifting supply from S1 to S2", "A rise in unit labour costs shifting supply from S2 to S1", "A rise in foreign demand shifting D to the right", "A fall in the quality of exports shifting D to the left"], "a": 0, "x": "Supply shifts right from S1 to S2, lowering the export price and raising quantity. Lower unit labour costs are the competitiveness cause that fits this.", "w": ["", "Moving from S2 to S1 would be the opposite shift, raising price and cutting quantity.", "D has not moved; only the supply curve has shifted in the diagram.", "D has not moved, and lower quality would cut both price and quantity."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of exports", "ylabel": "Export price (common currency)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 8]], "label": "S2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5.625, 4.5], "label": "Y", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d176", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows the market for exports. What is the effect of the shift from D1 to D2?", "o": ["Export price rises and quantity exported falls", "Export price is unchanged and quantity exported rises", "Export price and quantity exported both fall", "Export price and quantity exported both rise"], "a": 3, "x": "D2 lies to the right of D1, so demand for exports has risen. With an upward-sloping S, equilibrium moves from X to Y: a higher price and a higher quantity.", "w": ["Quantity does not fall: equilibrium moves up along S from Q1 to the larger Q2.", "With upward-sloping supply, a demand shift changes price as well as quantity.", "Both falling would need a leftward demand shift; D2 is to the right of D1.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of exports", "ylabel": "Export price (common currency)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "S", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 8], [10, 0]], "label": "D1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 10], [10, 2]], "label": "D2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4.375, 4.5], "label": "X", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5.625, 5.5], "label": "Y", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d177", "s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "lv": 3, "q": "Foreign demand D is the same in both cases. After supply shifts from S1 to S2, what does Area B show?", "o": ["Export revenue after the shift, P2×Q2", "Export revenue before the shift, P1×Q1", "The quantity of imports bought after the shift", "The rise in export revenue caused by the shift"], "a": 0, "x": "Revenue is price × quantity. After the shift the price is P2 and quantity Q2, so the rectangle under P2 out to Q2 is P2×Q2, export revenue after the shift.", "w": ["", "Before the shift revenue would be the rectangle P1×Q1, which is larger in height and not the one shaded.", "Area B is a price times quantity rectangle for exports, not an import measure.", "Area B is the whole revenue rectangle after the shift, not the change in revenue."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of exports", "ylabel": "Export price (common currency)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 8]], "label": "S2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D", "c": 1}], "areas": [{"pts": [[0, 4.5], [5.625, 4.5], [5.625, 0], [0, 0]], "label": "Area B", "c": 3}], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [5.625, 4.5], "label": "Y", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d178", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, which expression gives the Gini coefficient?", "o": ["B ÷ (A + B)", "A ÷ (A + B)", "A ÷ B", "(A + B) ÷ A"], "a": 1, "x": "The Gini is the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve (A) as a share of the whole area under the line of equality (A + B).", "w": ["B ÷ (A + B) measures the area under the curve, which falls as inequality rises.", "", "A ÷ B compares the two areas but is not the share of the total, so it can exceed 1.", "(A + B) ÷ A is always above 1, but the Gini runs from 0 to 1."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Cumulative % of households (poorest first)", "ylabel": "Cumulative % of income", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "Line of equality", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.1], [2, 0.4], [3, 0.9], [4, 1.6], [5, 2.5], [6, 3.6], [7, 4.9], [8, 6.4], [9, 8.1], [10, 10]], "label": "Lorenz curve", "c": 0}], "areas": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.1], [2, 0.4], [3, 0.9], [4, 1.6], [5, 2.5], [6, 3.6], [7, 4.9], [8, 6.4], [9, 8.1], [10, 10]], "label": "A", "c": 2, "at": [5.5, 6]}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.1], [2, 0.4], [3, 0.9], [4, 1.6], [5, 2.5], [6, 3.6], [7, 4.9], [8, 6.4], [9, 8.1], [10, 10], [10, 0]], "label": "B", "c": 3, "at": [8, 2.5]}], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d179", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "The Lorenz curve moves from L1 to L2. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["The Gini coefficient has risen and the richest households gain a larger share", "Every household's income has risen, as L2 lies nearer the line of equality", "The Gini coefficient is unchanged because both curves end at the same point", "The Gini coefficient has fallen and the poorest half receives a larger share"], "a": 3, "x": "L2 lies closer to the line of equality than L1, so the area between them shrinks and the Gini falls. At the 50% mark, the poorest half's cumulative share is higher on L2.", "w": ["A curve nearer the line means less inequality, so the Gini has fallen, not risen.", "Lorenz curves show shares, not levels, so they cannot show that everyone's income rose.", "Both curves end at (10, 10), but the area between each curve and the line differs, so the Gini changes.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Cumulative % of households (poorest first)", "ylabel": "Cumulative % of income", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "Line of equality", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.1], [2, 0.4], [3, 0.9], [4, 1.6], [5, 2.5], [6, 3.6], [7, 4.9], [8, 6.4], [9, 8.1], [10, 10]], "label": "L1", "c": 0, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.32], [2, 0.89], [3, 1.64], [4, 2.53], [5, 3.54], [6, 4.65], [7, 5.86], [8, 7.16], [9, 8.54], [10, 10]], "label": "L2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d180", "s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Lorenz curves LX and LY cross at point K. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Left of K the poorest do better under LX, right of K cumulative shares are higher under LY", "LX is more equal at every point, since its curve is closer to the line of equality", "LY is more equal at every point, since its curve lies higher than LX", "Both countries have the same Gini coefficient, as the curves meet at K"], "a": 0, "x": "Left of K, LX lies above LY, so the poorest do better under LX. Right of K, LY lies above LX. Because the curves cross, the two cannot be ranked from the picture alone.", "w": ["", "LX lies below LY to the right of K, so it is not closer to the line everywhere.", "LY lies below LX to the left of K, so it is not higher everywhere.", "Meeting at one point does not equalise the areas, so the Gini coefficients need not match."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Cumulative % of households (poorest first)", "ylabel": "Cumulative % of income", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "Line of equality", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 0.1], [2, 0.4], [3, 0.9], [4, 1.6], [5, 2.5], [6, 3.6], [7, 4.9], [8, 6.4], [9, 8.1], [10, 10]], "label": "LX", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [2, 0], [10, 10]], "label": "LY", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [2.5, 0.625], "label": "K"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d181", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, which line shows the original supply of the primary product before the poor harvest?", "o": ["D1", "S2", "S1", "X"], "a": 2, "x": "S1 passes through the original equilibrium X with D1. S2 lies to the left of (above) S1 and shows the supply after the poor harvest.", "w": ["D1 is the demand curve, not a supply curve.", "S2 is the dashed curve after the shift, not the original.", "", "X is a point of equilibrium, not a curve."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of primary product", "ylabel": "Price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [7, 10]], "label": "S2", "c": 2, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d182", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "The diagram shows supply of a primary product shifting from S1 to S2 after a harvest. What happens to the equilibrium price and quantity?", "o": ["Price falls and quantity rises", "Price rises and quantity falls", "Price and quantity both rise", "Price and quantity both fall"], "a": 1, "x": "S2 lies to the left of S1, so supply has fallen. The new equilibrium Y is at a higher price and a lower quantity than X. Inelastic demand for primaries makes such price swings large.", "w": ["That would follow a rightward supply shift, but S2 lies to the left of S1.", "", "Demand has not shifted, so quantity cannot rise along with price.", "A fall in supply raises price, so price cannot fall here."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of primary product", "ylabel": "Price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 1.8], [8.2, 10]], "label": "S2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X"}, {"at": [4, 5.8], "label": "Y"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d183", "s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "lv": 3, "q": "In the diagram, supply shifts from S1 to S2 and demand falls from D1 to D2. Compared with X, what happens at the new equilibrium Z?", "o": ["Price falls and quantity falls", "Price rises and quantity falls", "Price rises and quantity rises", "Price falls and quantity rises"], "a": 0, "x": "Z, where D2 meets S2, is at a lower price and lower quantity than X. Both shifts cut quantity, and here the fall in demand outweighs the fall in supply, so price falls despite the poor harvest.", "w": ["", "", "", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of primary product", "ylabel": "Price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 5.4], [6.75, 0]], "label": "D2", "c": 3}, {"pts": [[1, 1], [9, 9]], "label": "S1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[0, 1.8], [8.2, 10]], "label": "S2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "X", "guides": ["Q1", "P1"]}, {"at": [2, 3.8], "label": "Z", "guides": ["Q2", "P2"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d184", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, price rises well above the fundamental value line between times 3 and 6. What does this show?", "o": ["Moral hazard among borrowers", "A speculative bubble forming", "A fall in the asset's supply", "Market rigging by regulators"], "a": 1, "x": "Price far above fundamental value, driven by expected further rises, is a speculative bubble.", "w": ["Moral hazard concerns risk-taking when others bear the costs, not price rising above value.", "", "Nothing in the diagram shows supply falling; the gap to fundamental value is the key.", "Regulators do not rig markets, and the diagram shows no manipulation."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Asset price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4.6]], "label": "Fundamental value", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [1, 4.3], [2, 4.8], [3, 5.6], [4, 6.8], [5, 8.2], [6, 9], [7, 5.5], [8, 4.2], [9, 4.3], [10, 4.6]], "label": "Market price", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": []}}
{"id": "econ-d185", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "In the diagram, what best describes the change in market price between times 6 and 7?", "o": ["Fundamental value collapses at the peak", "Prices fall as the bubble bursts, cutting wealth and confidence", "Asymmetric information is removed at the peak", "The market correctly rises towards its fundamental value"], "a": 1, "x": "The sharp fall from the peak is the bubble bursting. Falling asset prices reduce wealth and confidence and can cause bank losses.", "w": ["The fundamental value line stays smooth; only the market price collapses.", "", "The diagram shows nothing about information being removed.", "Price is falling towards value from above, not rising towards it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Time", "ylabel": "Asset price", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 4], [10, 4.6]], "label": "Fundamental value", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 4], [1, 4.3], [2, 4.8], [3, 5.6], [4, 6.8], [5, 8.2], [6, 9], [7, 5.5], [8, 4.2], [9, 4.3], [10, 4.6]], "label": "Market price", "c": 1}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [6, 9], "to": [7, 5.5], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [6, 9], "label": "Peak"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d186", "s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "lv": 3, "q": "In the diagram, a bank expects a bailout so its lending follows MPC. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Lending at Q1 is too low and Area W is a gain", "Lending at Q* is too high because MPC is below MSC", "Lending at Q1 exceeds Q*, and Area W is the welfare loss", "Moral hazard is absent because D slopes down"], "a": 2, "x": "The bank lends to Q1 where D meets MPC. Social cost MSC is higher, so Q* is the best level. Area W is the welfare loss from over-lending.", "w": ["Q1 is above Q*, so lending is too high, and Area W is a loss.", "Q* is the socially best level; the over-lending occurs at Q1.", "", "The downward slope of D is just the benefit curve and has no bearing on moral hazard."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of risky lending", "ylabel": "Cost / benefit (£)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D (benefit)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 5]], "label": "MPC (bailout)", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[0, 3], [8.75, 10]], "label": "MSC", "c": 2}], "areas": [{"pts": [[3.75, 6], [6.667, 3.667], [6.667, 8.333]], "label": "Area W", "c": 3}], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [6.667, 3.667], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1"]}, {"at": [3.75, 6], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q*"]}]}}
{"id": "econ-d187", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In the loanable funds diagram, the government borrows more to fund spending. What does the shift from D1 to D2 show?", "o": ["Supply of funds has risen, lowering the interest rate", "Demand for funds has fallen as firms invest less", "Demand for funds has risen, pushing the interest rate up", "Supply of funds has fallen as saving has dropped"], "a": 2, "x": "Government borrowing adds to the demand for loanable funds, so the demand curve shifts right from D1 to D2. The equilibrium moves from E1 to E2 and the interest rate rises.", "w": ["The shift is in the demand curve, not supply, and the interest rate rises rather than falls.", "D2 lies to the right of D1, so demand has risen, not fallen.", "", "The supply curve S has not moved; only demand has shifted."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of loanable funds", "ylabel": "Interest rate", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "S", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 9.8], [9, 3.4]], "label": "D2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [3, 7.4], "to": [4, 7.4], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1", "r1"]}, {"at": [6, 5.8], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q2", "r2"]}], "anim": "none", "steps": []}}
{"id": "econ-d188", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Using the diagram, what happens when equilibrium moves from E1 to E2, and what is the effect on private firms?", "o": ["The rate rises from r1 to r2, so private investment falls", "The rate falls from r2 to r1, so private investment rises", "The rate stays at r1, so private investment is unaffected", "The rate rises from r1 to r2, so private investment increases"], "a": 0, "x": "E2 is at a higher interest rate (r2) than E1 (r1). Borrowing costs rise, so fewer private investment projects are profitable and firms invest less. This is crowding out.", "w": ["", "The move is from E1 to E2, which raises the rate rather than lowering it.", "The guides show r2 is above r1, so the rate does change.", "Higher borrowing costs reduce private investment; they do not increase it."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Quantity of loanable funds", "ylabel": "Interest rate", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "S", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "D1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[1, 9.8], [9, 3.4]], "label": "D2", "c": 2}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1", "guides": ["Q1", "r1"]}, {"at": [6, 5.8], "label": "E2", "guides": ["Q2", "r2"]}], "anim": "none", "steps": []}}
{"id": "econ-d189", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "lv": 3, "q": "Government spending shifts AD from AD1 to AD2, but crowding out occurs. Which describes the likely final equilibrium?", "o": ["E2 on AD2, because crowding out has no effect on AD", "E1 on AD1, because crowding out fully cancels the AD rise", "Beyond E2, because private investment rises with spending", "E3 on AD3, as crowding out offsets part of the AD rise"], "a": 3, "x": "AD2 is the rise before crowding out. Higher interest rates cut private investment, so AD ends below AD2 but above AD1, on AD3. Equilibrium E3 shows a partial offset.", "w": ["E2 is the outcome with no crowding out, which ignores the fall in private investment.", "E1 would mean full crowding out; the lesson says it usually offsets only part.", "Crowding out reduces private investment, so AD cannot go beyond AD2.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Real output", "ylabel": "Price level", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 1], [10, 9]], "label": "SRAS", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 9], [10, 1]], "label": "AD1", "c": 1, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[3, 9.8], [10, 4.2]], "label": "AD2", "c": 2}, {"pts": [[1, 9.8], [10, 2.6]], "label": "AD3", "c": 3}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 5], "label": "E1"}, {"at": [6, 5.8], "label": "E3"}, {"at": [7, 6.6], "label": "E2"}], "anim": "none", "steps": []}}
{"id": "econ-d190", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 1, "q": "On the Laffer curve shown, which point gives the highest tax revenue?", "o": ["Point A", "Point B", "Point M", "Revenue is the same at all three points"], "a": 2, "x": "Point M is at the top of the inverted U, the revenue-maximising (optimum) rate. A and B lie lower on the curve, so they raise less revenue.", "w": ["A is on the rising section, below the peak, so revenue is lower than at M.", "B is on the falling section, below the peak, so revenue is lower than at M.", "", "A and B happen to match each other, but M is higher than both."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Tax rate (0% to 100%)", "ylabel": "Tax revenue", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 2.88], [2, 5.12], [3, 6.72], [4, 7.68], [5, 8], [6, 7.68], [7, 6.72], [8, 5.12], [9, 2.88], [10, 0]], "label": "Laffer curve", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [2, 5.12], "label": "A"}, {"at": [5, 8], "label": "M"}, {"at": [8, 5.12], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d191", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 2, "q": "The government cuts the tax rate from P to Q, as the arrow shows. What does the diagram show?", "o": ["Revenue rises, because P is on the downward-sloping section", "Revenue falls, because a lower rate always means less tax", "Revenue rises, because Q is the revenue-maximising rate", "Revenue is unchanged, because both points are on one curve"], "a": 0, "x": "P is above the optimum rate, on the falling section. Cutting the rate moves along the curve to Q, where revenue is higher because the tax base grows.", "w": ["", "A lower rate loses revenue only below the optimum; P is above it.", "Q is still above the peak, so it is not the revenue-maximising rate.", "Different points on the curve give different revenue; the curve is not flat."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Tax rate (0% to 100%)", "ylabel": "Tax revenue", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 2.88], [2, 5.12], [3, 6.72], [4, 7.68], [5, 8], [6, 7.68], [7, 6.72], [8, 5.12], [9, 2.88], [10, 0]], "label": "Laffer curve", "c": 0}], "areas": [], "arrows": [{"from": [8, 5.12], "to": [6, 7.68], "c": 2}], "points": [{"at": [8, 5.12], "label": "P"}, {"at": [6, 7.68], "label": "Q"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d192", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Easier avoidance moves the Laffer curve from L1 to L2. What happens to the optimum rate and maximum revenue?", "o": ["The optimum rate rises, because avoidance is more rewarding", "The optimum rate is unchanged; only maximum revenue falls", "The optimum rate falls, but maximum revenue rises", "Both the optimum rate and the maximum revenue fall"], "a": 3, "x": "The peak moves from M1 to M2, which is further left and lower. Avoidance makes high rates less effective, so the best rate and the revenue it raises both fall.", "w": ["M2 is to the left of M1, so the optimum rate falls, not rises.", "M2 is to the left of M1, so the optimum rate does change.", "M2 is lower than M1, so maximum revenue falls, not rises.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "Tax rate (0% to 100%)", "ylabel": "Tax revenue", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 2.88], [2, 5.12], [3, 6.72], [4, 7.68], [5, 8], [6, 7.68], [7, 6.72], [8, 5.12], [9, 2.88], [10, 0]], "label": "L1", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 0], [1, 2.8], [2, 4.8], [3, 6], [4, 6.4], [5, 6], [6, 4.8], [7, 2.8], [8, 0]], "label": "L2", "c": 1, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [5, 8], "label": "M1"}, {"at": [4, 6.4], "label": "M2"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d193", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 1, "q": "In the diagram, G is government spending and T is tax revenue. The economy is at output Y1. What does the gap AB show?", "o": ["The national debt at Y1", "The actual budget deficit at Y1", "A budget surplus at Y1", "The structural deficit only"], "a": 1, "x": "At Y1, spending (point A on G) is above revenue (point B on T). The gap is that year's actual deficit, a flow, made up of structural and cyclical parts.", "w": ["Debt is a stock built up over many years. A gap between G and T in one year is a flow.", "", "A surplus needs T above G. Here G is above T, so the government is in deficit.", "The structural deficit is measured at potential output. This gap is at Y1 and includes the cyclical part."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "National output (income)", "ylabel": "Government spending and revenue (£bn)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [10, 6]], "label": "G (spending)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 8]], "label": "T (revenue)", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[4, 0], [4, 10]], "label": "Y1", "c": 3, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 7.2], "label": "A"}, {"at": [4, 4.4], "label": "B"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d194", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Potential output is Yp and the economy is at Y1, below it. Which gap shows the structural budget deficit?", "o": ["Gap AB", "Gap AB minus gap CD", "The distance from A to C", "Gap CD"], "a": 3, "x": "The structural deficit is the gap between G and T at potential output Yp, which is CD. It would remain even with output at its potential level.", "w": ["AB is the actual deficit at Y1, which includes the cyclical part as well.", "AB minus CD is the cyclical deficit, caused by output being below potential.", "A to C compares points on the same curve G at different outputs. It is not a budget gap.", ""], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "National output (income)", "ylabel": "Government spending and revenue (£bn)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [10, 6]], "label": "G (spending)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 8]], "label": "T (revenue)", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 0], [6, 10]], "label": "Yp", "c": 2, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[4, 0], [4, 10]], "label": "Y1", "c": 3, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [4, 7.2], "label": "A"}, {"at": [4, 4.4], "label": "B"}, {"at": [6, 6.8], "label": "C"}, {"at": [6, 5.6], "label": "D"}]}}
{"id": "econ-d195", "s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "lv": 3, "q": "Output grows from Yp to Ye, where G and T cross at E, so the budget balances. Why does this not remove the structural deficit?", "o": ["Ye is above potential, so the balance at E is temporary", "The structural deficit is measured at Ye, so it has fallen to zero", "Balancing the budget at E means debt falls to zero", "At E the cyclical deficit has risen, offsetting the structural one"], "a": 0, "x": "Ye is above potential Yp, so extra tax receipts are temporary. The structural deficit CD is measured at Yp and remains. When output falls back to Yp, the deficit returns.", "w": ["", "Structural deficits are measured at potential output Yp, not at Ye, and gap CD is still there.", "Balance in one year stops new borrowing but does not erase the existing debt stock.", "Above potential the cyclical element is a surplus, not a deficit, and it only masks the structural gap."], "fig": {"type": "line", "x": [0, 10], "y": [0, 10], "grid": false, "xnums": false, "ynums": false, "xlabel": "National output (income)", "ylabel": "Government spending and revenue (£bn)", "lines": [{"pts": [[0, 8], [10, 6]], "label": "G (spending)", "c": 0}, {"pts": [[0, 2], [10, 8]], "label": "T (revenue)", "c": 1}, {"pts": [[6, 0], [6, 10]], "label": "Yp", "c": 2, "dash": true}, {"pts": [[7.5, 0], [7.5, 10]], "label": "Ye", "c": 3, "dash": true}], "areas": [], "arrows": [], "points": [{"at": [6, 6.8], "label": "C"}, {"at": [6, 5.6], "label": "D"}, {"at": [7.5, 6.5], "label": "E"}]}}
